hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3601","3137 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hujr narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Khalid, he is al-Hadhdha, from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Mutarrif, from Iyad ibn Himar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever takes a find, let him witness two just; let him keep its container and strap; let him not conceal nor hide. If its owner comes, he is worthier of it; and if its owner does not come, it is Allah's wealth He gives whom He wills. We stood thereby that the reality of what is in the first hadith, of one just or two just, is: two just. It may mean taking the foundling out with people that his picking it was to go with it, and he is thereby blameworthy with them, dropping justice with it. It may mean meant thereby preserving the find for its owner, and that the hand falling upon it by picking is the finder's hand seeking by his picking preserving it for its owner, not a possessing hand taking it for himself, not for its owner. We looked into that; we found hands upon things are proof necessitating things' disposal to what what hands own are disposed to, besides hands' ownership, of accepting their sayings therein, and disposing them after their deaths in settling debts, inheritances, and bequests. So it was due upon possessors in what fell in their hands upon the path we mentioned to establish proof upon themselves for owners of what came in their hands of that, by acknowledging it and witnessing upon it; for proof to stand it is in their hands upon the path picking is upon, of carrying out the obligatory therein, and preventing inheritances from it, and disposing it in what other than it is disposed in; and till it is preserved so, and till everyone whose hand falls upon it besides its finder exemplifies therein the obligatory, till it reaches its lord's hand or what besides it must reach of the rulings Allah commanded therein upon His Prophet's tongue; and Allah we ask for success."
"3602","500 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying on Makkah's trees and its herbage, and al-Abbas's saying to him thereupon when he stood upon his preventing of it: Except idhkhir; and his saying to him answering his speech: Except idhkhir. 3138 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj, Musa ibn Harun al-Bardi, and Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, they said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on Makkah's conquest day: This town Allah made sacred the day He created heavens and earth; it is sacred by Allah's sanctity till Resurrection Day. Fighting in it was never lawful for anyone before me, nor lawful for me except an hour of daytime. It is sacred by Allah's sanctity till Resurrection Day: its thorn is not cut, its game not startled, its find not picked except one announcing it, its herbage not cut. Al-Abbas said: O Messenger of Allah, except idhkhir; for it is for their smiths and houses. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Except idhkhir."
"3603","3139 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mabad; and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abu Yusuf narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah made Makkah sacred the day He created heavens, earth, sun, and moon, and placed it between these two mountains. It was never lawful for anyone before me, nor lawful for me except an hour of daytime. Its herbage is not cut, its tree not cut, its game not startled, none lifts its find except its announcer. Al-Abbas said: Except idhkhir, for Makkah folk cannot do without idhkhir for their houses and graves. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Except idhkhir."
"3604","3140 - al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it, except he said: Al-Abbas said: O Messenger of Allah, Makkah folk have no patience without idhkhir. He said: Except idhkhir."
"3605","3141 - And Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sallam al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us, he said: Khalid informed us, from Khalid, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah made Makkah sacred; it was never lawful for anyone before me, nor lawful for anyone after me. It was only made lawful for me an hour of daytime. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith before it."
"3606","3142 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, except he said: Al-Abbas stood - and he was an experienced man - and said: Except idhkhir, for it is for our houses, graves, and smiths. He said: Except idhkhir."
"3607","3143 - And Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubayd ibn Yaish al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Salih narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Muslim ibn Yannaq, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressing on conquest day, saying: O people, Allah made Makkah sacred the day He created heavens and earth; it is sacred till Resurrection Day. Its tree is not cut, its game not startled, none takes its find except announcer. Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib said: O Messenger of Allah, except idhkhir, for it is for roofs of houses and graves. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Except idhkhir."
"3608","3144 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, he said: Abu Hurayrah narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in his address when Makkah was conquered: Allah withheld from Makkah folk killing - so he said, while it is: the elephant - and empowered over them His Messenger and the believers. It was never lawful for anyone before me, nor lawful for anyone after me. It was lawful for me only an hour of daytime, and it is this hour of mine, so that its tree is not cut, its thorn not cut. Al-Abbas stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, except idhkhir, for we put it in our graves and houses. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Except idhkhir."
"3609","3145 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, except he said: Allah withheld from Makkah folk the elephant; and except he said: A man of Quraysh stood, in place of what is in the first hadith of its narrator saying: Al-Abbas stood."
"3610","3146 - And Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Darawardi informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah narrated to us, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood upon al-Hajun and said: By Allah, you are Allah's best land, and Allah's most beloved land to Allah; had I not been expelled from you I would not have left. It was never lawful for anyone before me. Then he mentioned the like of it, except he said therein: Its stray is not picked except for announcer. A man called Shah said: O Messenger of Allah, except idhkhir. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. A questioner asked of what is ascribed in these hadiths to al-Abbas or to whoever mentioned besides him, of his saying to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he mentioned Makkah's trees' sanctity and its herbage's sanctity: except idhkhir, excepting from that; and denied that was from al-Abbas, and that the Messenger of Allah would assent to anyone upon that. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's help and aid, is: these reports are established, sound in coming, all accepted. What was from al-Abbas or besides him therein is not denied of its like. The Messenger of Allah leaving its disapproval against him is also not denied. How would he disapprove against him what he is praiseworthy in, since he knew of Makkah folk's need to idhkhir what they are upon of it, and said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what he said, seeking from him his Lord's review therein, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked in the Miraj hadith his Lord when He obligated upon his community fifty prayers in day and night, for lightening time after time till He returned them to five prayers. As the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to recite the Quran upon one letter, and reviewed therein time after time till returned to seven letters. Like that is what was from al-Abbas or besides him of what we mentioned. His saying: except idhkhir, cutting speech there, is for his knowing the Prophet's understanding what he wanted of it, of asking his Lord about that. He sufficed from speech with it, as Arabs use in their speech for brevity, silence from speech with it for knowing their addressee's understanding what they addressed him with for its sake, till they bring part of the word and leave its rest. And of that is their saying: Enough with the sword, Shah, meaning witness, till that rose and the Quran came with it. Of that is His saying: Had there been a Quran by which mountains are moved, earth cut, or dead spoken to; then He cut the rest of speech, which the knowledge folk differed over what it is: some said: it is: they would disbelieve in it; some said: it is: this Quran would be. And of that His saying: Had not Allah's bounty upon you and His mercy, and that Allah is Relenting, Wise; leaving mention of what would be were it not His bounty and mercy. And of that His saying: Is one devout in night hours, prostrating and standing, fearing the Hereafter and hoping his Lord's mercy; then He said: Are those knowing and those not knowing equal; leaving mention of who is not like him, for sufficing with addressees' understanding with it. Like that is al-Abbas's or whoever said it besides him saying to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): except idhkhir, sufficing from completing speech with what he wanted, for knowing the Prophet's understanding of what he wanted. This speaker said: The Prophet's answer to him was with no time between question and answer in which revelation for that answer would be. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's help and aid, is: Allah's subtle power may bring revelation in that time from where we do not conceive the like's coming therein. It may be what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did therein was by Gabriel casting that to him, as he said to the one asking him in Abu Qatadah's hadith: What if I am killed in Allah's path, patient, seeking reward, advancing, not retreating; does Allah expiate my sins? He said: Yes. When he turned, he said to him: Except debt be upon you; so Gabriel said to me. That indicates Gabriel's presence at his first answer, and his saying to his questioner what he said to him as second answer. When we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we shall mention afterward in our book, Allah willing, of his saying to Hassan in his lampooning the polytheists against him: Lampoon them, Gabriel with you. When Gabriel was with Hassan for lampooning Quraysh, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), by his being with him in his address informing people therein from Allah of their religion's laws and obligations upon them, is worthier; and Gabriel being with him in that time fitter. So it became clear, by Allah's praise and blessing, there is nothing denied in anything this ignorant of the Messenger of Allah's reports denied of what we mentioned from him; and Allah we ask for success."
"3611","501 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Makkah's herbage: is its sanctity in all states, or upon its sanctity in a state apart from a state, and with a deed apart from a deed? Abu Jafar said: The knowledge folk differed over Makkah's grass and what besides it the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade, in its mowing and feeding it to camels and others. They said therein three sayings we shall mention in this chapter, Allah willing; no saying to them therein besides them. As Jafar ibn Ahmad ibn al-Walid al-Aslami narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Yusuf, he said: I asked Abu Hanifah about the Sanctuary's grass. He said: It is neither grazed nor cut. I asked Ibn Abi Layla, and he said: No harm to graze it and cut it. I asked al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, and he said: I asked Ata ibn Abi Rabah about it, and he said: No harm to graze it, not cut it. Abu Yusuf said: Ata's saying therein is dearer to me. When they differed therein this differing, we sought the worthiest of what they said therein of these sayings of theirs."
"3612","We found Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us; and Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, both said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj and Abd al-Malik informed us, from Ata, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, that Umar ibn al-Khattab saw a man cutting of the Sanctuary's trees and feeding it to a camel of his. He said: Bring me the man. He was brought, and he said: O servant of Allah, did you not know Makkah is sacred: its thorn trees not cut, its game not startled, its find not lawful except for announcer? He said: O Commander of the Faithful, by Allah nothing carried me upon that except I have an emaciated mount, and I feared it would not convey me to my folk; I have no provision nor expense. He pitied him after intending against him, and ordered for him a sound loaded camel of charity camels, and gave it to him, saying: Do not return to cutting of the Sanctuary's trees anything. We narrated in the chapter before this chapter the Messenger of Allah's forbidding cutting Makkah's herbage. Some held cutting is what is taken by hand, apart from other than it, of feeding camels it, upon what we narrated in this chapter from Ata, and upon what we mentioned from Abu Yusuf of agreeing with him upon it. Others held that is forbidden; for those things are forbidden in themselves, so all deeds done in them, of grazing them and cutting them, are forbidden; as game forbidden in itself, all things therein are unlawful for its sanctity in itself. This saying with us is worthiest of these sayings with truth; for Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed the man he saw grazing his camel of the Sanctuary's trees with what he addressed him in what we mentioned in this hadith. That indicates grazing's sanctity therein, as it indicates cutting's sanctity of it. Some narrated a hadith on Madinah's sanctity, and forbidding cutting its herbage, and that its trees not be cut except a man feed his camel; so they argued thereby upon its like of Makkah's trees and herbage."
"3613","3147 - It is what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Hassan, that Ali, upon him peace, took out the sheet he heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his sword's scabbard; therein: Ibrahim, upon him peace, made Makkah sacred, and I made Madinah sacred. Its herbage is not cut, its game not startled, its tree not cut except a man feed his camel. We considered it and found it broken-chained; for Abu Hassan did not meet Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. What he narrates of Ali's hadith is taken from Ubaydah al-Salmani and his like of his companions from him. When that is so, what we narrated in this chapter opposing it, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, is worthier than it; especially since that was from Umar in the presence of others of the Messenger of Allah's companions, and they did not disapprove it against him nor oppose him therein. That indicates their following him upon it; and Allah we ask for success. Then we found this hadith connected-chained."
"3614","3148 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Hafs ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Ibrahim, meaning Ibn Tahman, narrated to me, from al-Hajjaj, meaning Ibn al-Hajjaj al-Ahwal al-Bahili, from Qatadah, from Abu Hassan al-Araj, from al-Ashtar, that he narrated to him from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, then he mentioned the like of Ibn Abi Dawud's hadith we mentioned in this chapter from Hudbah. Abu Jafar said: This al-Hajjaj is an Imam in hadith, praiseworthy in narration. Then someone said: How may this hadith be connected-chained, while Abu Hassan only mentioned it from al-Ashtar, and al-Ashtar's death was in Ali ibn Abi Talib's days, may Allah be pleased with him; when it is negated he heard from Ali, it is more negated he heard from al-Ashtar. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's help and aid, is: Abu Hassan mentioned in this hadith from al-Ashtar that he narrated it to him; thereby he verified his hearing it from him. It may be Abu Hassan saw al-Ashtar in Ali's life, and he narrated to him this hadith from Ali, not seeing Ali or seeing him and not hearing from him. Abu Jafar said: This hadith after its establishment does not necessitate in Makkah's herbage its equaling Madinah's herbage in this meaning; for each's ruling in this meaning may be contrary to the other's ruling, as their ruling differs in the lawfulness of entering Madinah's sanctuary without ihram, and unlawfulness of entering Makkah's sanctuary except with ihram; and as their ruling in killing their game differs: whoever kills game in Makkah's sanctuary expiates it, and whoever kills game in Madinah's sanctuary does not expiate it. When each sanctuary's ruling differs in what we mentioned, it is not denied to differ in feeding camels of their trees: forbidden in Makkah's trees, lawful in Madinah sanctuary's trees; and Allah we ask for success."
"3615","502 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the meaning by which it is lawful for whoever buys food in bulk to sell it. 3149 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Nasr ibn Ali informed us; and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, he said: I saw people beaten in the Messenger of Allah's time when they bought food in bulk to sell it till they shelter it to their lodgings. He said: In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah forbidding bulk food buyers to sell it till they shelter it to their lodgings; and what they moved it to of places is lodgings for those moving it to them."
"3616","3150 - And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Hashim al-Balabakki narrated to us, he said: Shurayh ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, he said: Food owners were beaten in the Messenger of Allah's time when they bought food in speculation and sold it, sheltering it to their lodgings."
"3617","3151 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Hashim narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Ishaq and Ahmad differed over who Muhammad ibn Hashim narrated this hadith from, from al-Awzai, as we mentioned. This hadith's meaning is as the hadith before it's meaning."
"3618","3152 - Muhammad ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"3619","3153 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi Razin narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Hamzah ibn Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Umar, then he mentioned the like of it. He said: In this hadith's chain is contrary to what is in the chains of what we narrated before it returning to al-Awzai; for in this is from al-Zuhri from Hamzah, and in what before it from al-Zuhri from Salim. It is the sound, no disagreement among chain knowledge folk therein. Likewise others than al-Awzai narrated it from al-Zuhri, of them Mamar, upon what we mentioned in the hadith at this chapter's start."
"3620","3154 - And upon what Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, he said: I saw people beaten in the Prophet's time when they bought food in bulk to sell it till they take possession of it."
"3621","3155 - And upon what Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Anbasah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, he said: Salim informed me, from his father, then he mentioned the like of it. And of them Salih ibn Kaysan."
"3622","3156 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Harrani informed us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Salih, meaning Ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, that Salim informed him that Ibn Umar said: I saw people, then he mentioned the like of it, except he said: Till they shelter it to their lodgings. This hadith is also narrated from Nafi, from Ibn Umar."
"3623","3157 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Mualla ibn Mansur al-Razi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: We would meet riders in the Messenger of Allah's time and buy food of them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell it till you take full possession and move it. This hadith with us does not oppose what we narrated before it; for every place moved to is a lodging for its mover to it."
"3624","3158 - And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We would meet riders and buy food of them in bulk. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us selling it till we move it from its place or transfer it. This hadith's meaning returns to Abu Umayyah's hadith's meaning."
"3625","3159 - And as al-Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Ghalib narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that they would buy food of riders in the Messenger of Allah's time; he sends upon them one preventing them selling it where they bought it, till they convey it where they sell food. It may be the places they moved it to are food-selling localities."
"3626","3160 - And as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan al-Basri informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jahdam narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn Nafi', from his father, from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to send men to prevent owners of food from selling it where they bought it, until they moved it to another place."
"3627","3161 - And as 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding that goods be sold where they are bought, until the one who bought them takes possession of them to his lodging, and he would indeed send men who would beat us for that. He said: So this hadith agreed with what Musa ibn 'Uqbah narrated about it, while Ayyub, 'Ubaydullah, 'Umar ibn Nafi', and Malik ibn Anas opposed them in it; and even though we did not mention it, we shall mention it at the end of this discussion. So this was preferable in our view, because four are more deserving of retention than two. As for the hadith of Malik:"
"3628","3162 - For Yazid narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: In the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we used to buy food, then he would send to us one who ordered us to move it from the place where we bought it to another place before we sold it."
"3629","3163 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, from Malik... Then we considered: has anything contrary to this been narrated from Ibn 'Umar that falls under this chapter?"
"3630","3164 - So we found Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar, 'Umar ibn Muhammad, and Malik informed me that Nafi' narrated to them from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys food, let him not sell it until he takes full possession of it. He said: So the meaning of 'until he takes full possession of it' is until he completes its measure if it is measured, or its weight if it is weighed, or its count if it is counted; and that included moving it from one place to another. Likewise what he bought in bulk was intended to be moved from one place to another until its sale becomes lawful after that."
"3631","3165 - So we found Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from 'Ubayd ibn Hunayn, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, he said: I bought oil in the market, then a man came to me and offered me profit until I was pleased. When I took his hand to strike the bargain, a man from behind me took my arm and held my hand. I turned and there was Zayd ibn Thabit. He said: Do not sell it until you take possession of it to your house, for the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that."
"3632","3166 - And we found Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from 'Ubayd ibn Hunayn, from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I bought oil in the market, and when I became entitled to it a man met me and offered me a good profit, so I wanted to strike his hand. A man from behind me took my arm; I turned to him and there was Zayd ibn Thabit. He said: Do not sell it where you bought it until you take possession of it to your lodging, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that goods be sold where they are bought until the merchants take possession of them to their lodgings. Jarir and Ibn Ishaq differed in the wording of this hadith: one said 'to your lodging' and the other said 'to your house' - it returns to the meaning of what we narrated before it. And it is established by verifying these reports that what is bought in bulk is not to be sold until it is moved from the place where it was bought to another place. This is what al-Shafi'i held in this meaning; and in what we mentioned of that is proof that what cannot bear transport from place to place, like houses and lands, may be sold after buying them without taking possession, because the element present in other things - transport which stands in place of measuring for what is measured - is not feasible in them. This is what Abu Hanifah held regarding the sale of bought houses and lands before taking possession from the seller. And we ask Allah for success. Then an objector said: You have narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his forbidding the sale of food until it is fully received, and you have narrated from him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his forbidding the sale of goods where they are bought until they are moved - so what is your position on one who claims these reports contradict one another?"
"3633","Chapter 503 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who abandons prayer among the Muslims, not by denying it: is he thereby an apostate from Islam or not? 3167 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik ibn Anas narrated to him from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Ibn Muhayriz, that a man from Banu Kinanah called al-Mukhdaji heard a man in Syria called Abu Muhammad saying: Witr is obligatory. Al-Mukhdaji said: So I went to 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit and met him as he was going to the mosque, and I told him what Abu Muhammad had said. 'Ubadah said: Abu Muhammad has erred. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Five prayers which Allah has prescribed for the servants; whoever comes with them without neglecting any of them by belittling their right, he has a covenant with Allah that He will admit him to Paradise; and whoever does not come with them has no covenant with Allah - if He wills He punishes him, and if He wills He admits him to Paradise."
"3634","3168 - And 'Abd al-Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb ibn Hayyan al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Ibn Muhayriz, that a man from Banu Kinanah, then from Banu Mukhdaj, met a man from the Ansar called Abu Muhammad and asked him about witr. He said to him: It is obligatory. The Kinani said: Then I met 'Ubadah, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Yunus, from Ibn Wahb, from Malik, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, exactly."
"3635","3169 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Ibn Muhayriz, from al-Mukhdaji, from Abu Muhammad al-Ansari, that he said: Witr is obligatory like the obligation of prayer. So I mentioned that to 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, and he said: Abu Muhammad has erred; rather it is a sunnah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did it. Five prayers... then he mentioned what is in the hadith of Yahya ibn Sa'id, but he did not attribute it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3636","3170 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Asbagh 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Yahya al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, he said: My uncle Wasi' ibn Habban and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Uqbah ibn Kudaym differed about witr. My uncle said: It is a sunnah which should not be abandoned; and 'Abd al-Rahman said: It is obligatory like the obligation of prayer. So I met Ibn Muhayriz al-Jumahi and asked him. He said: Al-Mukhdaji informed me that he and a man from Syria called Abu Muhammad differed about it, while 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit was then at Tabariyyah. So I went to him and said: Abu al-Walid, I differed with Abu Muhammad about witr; I said it is a sunnah which should not be abandoned, and he said it is obligatory like the obligation of prayer. 'Ubadah was a man with sharpness in him. He said: Abu Muhammad has erred; it is not as he said, but as you said. I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his mouth to my mouth - I do not say 'so-and-so said': Five prayers Allah has made obligatory upon His servants; whoever meets Him without neglecting them by belittling their right meets Him... and the rest of the speech fell out, of what is mentioned in the two hadiths of Malik and al-Layth from Yahya ibn Sa'id which we mentioned in this chapter, up to what is in it of his saying: and he has no covenant - if He wills He punishes him, and if He wills He forgives him. Abu Ja'far said: The al-Mukhdaji mentioned in this hadith is named Rufay' according to what Yahya ibn Ma'in mentioned; and Abu Muhammad mentioned in it is named Sa'd ibn Aws. So in what we narrated of the hadiths of Yahya and 'Abd Rabbihi, the two sons of Sa'id, and Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, is proof that abandoning prayer without denial does not expel from Islam."
"3637","3171 - And as Muhammad ibn 'Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh ibn Khalid narrated to us, from 'Uqayl ibn Khalid, he said: Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban narrated to me that 'Abdullah ibn Muhayriz narrated to him that a man disputed with a man from the Ansar called Abu Muhammad about witr. Abu Muhammad said: It is like prayer; and the other man said: It is from the sunnah, it should not be abandoned but it is not like an obligation. He said: So I asked 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit al-Ansari about that and told him what each of us had said. He was a man with sharpness in him. He said: Abu Muhammad has erred - repeatedly. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Allah has made five prayers obligatory upon His servants; whoever comes with them on the Day of Resurrection without neglecting any of them by belittling their right meets Him, and he has a covenant with Him by which He admits him to Paradise; and whoever neglects any of them meets Him with no covenant with Him - if He wills He punishes him, and if He wills He admits him to Paradise."
"3638","3172 - And as al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Muhammad ibn al-'Ajlan narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Ibn Muhayriz, he said: A man from among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Abu Muhammad mentioned witr and said: It is obligatory. So I mentioned that to 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, and he said: Abu Muhammad has erred. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Five prayers... then he mentioned the rest of the hadith like what is in the two hadiths of Malik and al-Layth which we mentioned in this chapter. And this meaning has also been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Ka'b ibn 'Ujrah al-Ansari, from him as well."
"3639","3173 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Nu'man al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Sa'id ibn Ka'b ibn 'Ujrah al-Ansari narrated to me, from his father, from Ka'b, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were in the mosque, seven of us - three of us from our Arabs and four from our freedmen. He said: What has seated you here? We said: Prayer. He patted the ground with his finger, then bowed his head for a while, then raised his head to us and said: Do you know what your Lord says? We said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: He says: Whoever prays the prayer at its time and establishes its limits, he has thereby a covenant with Allah that when he comes to Him, Paradise; and whoever does not establish prayer at its time and does not establish its limits, he has thereby no covenant with Me - if I will I admit him to the Fire, and if I will I admit him to Paradise."
"3640","3174 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, he said: Malik - meaning Ibn Mighwal - narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, from Ka'b, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us one day while we were in the mosque, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: In the hadith of 'Ubadah: if he does not come with them, meaning the five prayers. And in the hadith of Ka'b: whoever does not establish prayer at its time and does not establish its limits. Then in both of their hadiths together: he has no covenant with Allah - if He wills He punishes him in the hadith of 'Ubadah, and in the hadith of Ka'b: He admits him to the Fire; and in both of their hadiths together: and if He wills He admits him to Paradise. So in that is proof that He did not thereby expel him from Islam and make him an apostate polytheist, because Allah Almighty does not admit to Paradise one who associates partners with Him, by His saying: Whoever associates partners with Allah, Allah has forbidden Paradise to him; and He does not forgive him, by His saying Almighty: Allah does not forgive that partners be associated with Him, and He forgives what is less than that for whom He wills. Then an objector said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while you narrate from him:"
"3641","3175 - So he mentioned what Yazid ibn Sinan had narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Between the servant and disbelief - or he said: polytheism - is abandoning prayer."
"3642","3176 - And what Yazid had narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: The basis of the hadith is: between the servant and disbelief."
"3643","3177 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to me, he said: Jabir narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Between a man and disbelief is abandoning prayer."
"3644","3178 - And as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rabi'ah narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that the disbelief mentioned in this hadith is other than disbelief in Allah Almighty; rather with the people of language it is that it covers the faith of one who abandons prayer and conceals it until it becomes dominant over him, covering it. From that is what Labid mentioned, meaning: its clouds covered the stars; and from that is the saying of Allah Almighty: Like rain whose growth pleases the disbelievers, meaning the farmers who conceal what they sow in the earth, not the disbelievers in Allah Almighty. And from that is what is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of the solar eclipse."
"3645","3179 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik informed him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And I saw - or I was shown - the Fire, and I saw most of its people were women. They said: Why, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Because of their ingratitude. It was said: Do they disbelieve in Allah Almighty? He said: They are ungrateful to the husband and ungrateful for kindness; so He named what comes from them of covering kindness as disbelief. And from that is what is narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Abusing a Muslim is wickedness, and fighting him is disbelief - and we have mentioned that with its chain in what preceded in this book of ours; and that was not disbelief in Allah Almighty, but rather what had mounted upon his faith and covered it, of his ugly deed. Likewise his saying: There is nothing between the servant and disbelief except abandoning prayer is from this meaning as well, Allah knows best, so that these reports are sound and do not conflict. The people of knowledge have differed about one who abandons prayer as we mentioned: some of them made him thereby an apostate from Islam and made his ruling the ruling of one who is asked to repent from that - if he repents, otherwise he is killed; among them al-Shafi'i. And among them are those who did not make him thereby an apostate, but made him among the sinful Muslims and among the people of major sins among them; and among those who said that is Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, and his companions. This view was preferable to us by analogy, because we found for Allah Almighty obligations upon His servants at specific times - among them the five prayers, and among them the fast of Ramadan - and one who abandons the fast of Ramadan deliberately, without denying its obligation upon him, is not thereby a disbeliever, nor out of Islam; so likewise prayer."
"3646","Chapter 504 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Whoever does not guard the five prayers will be on the Day of Resurrection with Pharaoh, Haman, Qarun, and Ubayy the owner of the bones. 3180 - Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah and Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to me, from Ka'b ibn 'Alqamah, from 'Isa ibn Hilal al-Sadafi, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned prayer one day and said: Whoever guards it, it will be for him light, proof, and salvation on the Day of Resurrection; and whoever does not guard it, it will not be for him light, nor proof, nor salvation, and he will be on the Day of Resurrection with Pharaoh, Qarun, Haman, and Ubayy the owner of the bones."
"3647","3181 - And Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari and Bakr ibn Idris al-Azdi narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri' narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, from Ka'b ibn 'Alqamah, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Then an objector said: In this hadith one who abandons prayer without denial is mentioned as being on the Day of Resurrection with those mentioned among the people who are among the people of the Fire; so in that is proof that he is a disbeliever by abandoning prayers like their disbelief in what they disbelieved. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that the matter is not as he imagined, because Allah Almighty gathers in Hell those mentioned in this hadith and others besides them among the hypocrites, and others besides them among the people of Islam who neglect His obligations upon them and violate His sanctities upon them and devour the wealth of orphans, by His saying about them: Those who devour the wealth of orphans unjustly only devour fire in their bellies, and they shall roast in a blaze; and among them others besides them whom He mentioned in His Book and upon the tongue of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So some of them will be with others in Hell as mixed people - among them disbelievers and among them Muslims - and the abode of His punishment gathers them all together for what they were upon, of disbelief, of neglecting Islam, and of hypocrisy. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"3648","Chapter 505 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who abandons Friday three times. 3182 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-'Ala' ibn Muhammad ibn Sayyar narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us; and Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd al-Tanafisi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us; then both together said: from 'Abidah ibn Sufyan, from Abu al-Ja'd al-Damri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever abandons Friday three times, Allah seals his heart."
"3649","3183 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to me, from Usayd ibn Abi Usayd, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever abandons Friday three times without necessity, Allah seals his heart."
"3650","3184 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Usayd ibn Abi Usayd narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: This Usayd ibn Abi Usayd is al-Barrad. Then an objector said: Is one who abandons Friday until its time expires free of deserving this threat or not deserving it - so what is the point of specifying three? So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that it is mercy from Allah Almighty in giving him respite three times so that he returns to it and his heart is not sealed, or he persists in abandoning it three times and his heart is sealed. In that is proof that he was not a disbeliever by abandoning it the first time when its time expired. And we ask Allah for success."
"3651","Chapter 506 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the one who was ordered to be flogged in his grave with a hundred lashes, then kept asking and supplicating until it was reduced to one lash. 3185 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn 'Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from 'Asim, from Shaqiq, from Ibn Mas'ud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: A servant from among the servants of Allah was ordered to be struck in his grave with a hundred lashes; he kept asking and supplicating until it became one lash. So he was flogged once, and his grave was filled with fire upon him. When it was lifted from him he said: For what did you flog me? They said: You prayed a prayer without purification, and you passed by an oppressed one and did not help him. So in that is proof that the abandoner of that prayer had not prayed it until its time expired; and in Allah's answering his supplication is proof that he was not thereby a disbeliever, for had he been a disbeliever his supplication would have been included in the saying of Allah Almighty: And the supplication of the disbelievers is only in error. And we ask Allah for success."
"3652","Chapter 507 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: People shall certainly stop abandoning Fridays, or Allah shall certainly seal their hearts, or they shall certainly be among the heedless. 3186 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Aban al-'Attar narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Zayd, from Abu Sallam, from al-Hadrami ibn Lahiq, from al-Hakam ibn Mina', that he heard Ibn 'Abbas and Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrating that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said while upon the sticks of his pulpit: People shall certainly stop abandoning Fridays, or Allah shall certainly seal their hearts, or they shall certainly be among the heedless."
"3653","3186M - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Abu Salamah Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Zayd, from Abu Sallam, from al-Hadrami, from al-Hakam ibn Mina', that he heard Ibn 'Umar and Ibn 'Abbas, then both mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"3654","3187 - And 'Ali ibn Zayd al-Fara'idi narrated to us, he said: Abu Tawbah narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, from Zayd, he said: I heard Abu Sallam, he said: al-Hakam ibn Mina' narrated to me that 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar narrated to him and Abu Hurayrah that both heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then both mentioned the like of it. And what we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter suffices us from speech in this chapter. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"3655","Chapter 508 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Whoever misses 'Asr prayer, it is as if he has been bereft of his family and wealth. 3188 - 'Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi 'Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One who misses 'Asr prayer, it is as if he has been bereft of his family and wealth."
"3656","3189 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: 'Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3657","3190 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3658","3191 - And Yazid, Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah, and Fahd narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3659","3192 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Shayban - meaning al-Nahwi - narrated to us, from Yahya, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"3660","3193 - And Yazid narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar and Abu Salih narrated to us, they said: al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Nafi' informed us, from Ibn 'Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3661","3194 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3662","3195 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed us; and al-Rabi' al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Nawfal ibn Mu'awiyah al-Dili, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So the meaning of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): it is as if he has been bereft of his family and wealth, is: it is as if his family and wealth have been diminished; and from it is the saying of Allah Almighty: And He will never diminish your deeds, meaning: He will never reduce your deeds. Likewise Wallad al-Nahwi narrated to us from al-Musadiri from Abu 'Ubaydah. And in that is proof that he was not thereby a disbeliever, for had he been a disbeliever, what he lost of the departure of his faith would have been greater than what he lost of the departure of his family and wealth; and the intent was the mention of his family and wealth. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"3663","Chapter 509 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his forbidding waste of wealth. 3196 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd al-Tanafisi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Suqah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Ubaydullah al-Thaqafi, from Warrad, he said: al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah wrote to Mu'awiyah - and Warrad claimed he wrote it with his hand: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah has forbidden three and prohibited three: disobedience to parents, burying daughters alive, and withholding and demanding; and He prohibited three: gossip, wasting wealth, and persistent begging."
"3664","3197 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-'Absi narrated to us, he said: Shayban - he is al-Nahwi - informed us, from Mansur, from al-Sha'bi, from Warrad the scribe of al-Mughirah, from al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Almighty dislikes three things for you: gossip, excessive asking, and wasting wealth; and He has forbidden you three things: burying daughters, disobedience to mothers, and withholding and demanding. So we considered what wasting of wealth in this hadith is."
"3665","3198 - So we found Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: Fayd ibn al-Fadl al-Suhaymi narrated to us - Abu Ja'far said: he is a branch of Bajilah, from the kin of Abu Yusuf the judge, for Abu Yusuf is from Bajilah, ally of the Ansar, except that they gave birth to him - he said: al-Sari ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: 'Amir al-Sha'bi narrated to us, he said: Masruq narrated to us, from 'Abdullah, he said: A visitor came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was with him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I am obeyed among my people, so what shall I command them? He said: Command them to spread peace and to speak little except in what concerns them. He said: O Messenger of Allah, from what shall I forbid them? He said: Forbid them gossip, excessive asking, and wasting wealth - meaning by wealth livestock, that they not be neglected but treated well, thus in the hadith - and forbid them disobedience to mothers, burying daughters, and withholding and demanding. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith, even if its pivot is al-Sari ibn Isma'il, about whom critics spoke, he is an old shaykh from whom the eminent Kufans and others narrated, and he is not abandoned in hadith. So in this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is his forbidding waste of wealth, and the interpretation of waste of wealth as livestock - that they not be neglected and be treated well. This interpretation is good, for maintaining them in what they cannot maintain themselves - food, drink, and clothing for the children of Adam, and fodder for other animals - is obligatory upon their owners; and if their owners fall short of that they are sinful and held accountable for it. What strengthens that is what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3666","3199 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah gave counsel while his tongue could hardly speak, then he mentioned a word, saying: Prayer and what your right hands possess."
"3667","3200 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Nufayli narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Taymi narrated to us, from Anas, he said: The last counsel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when death came to him was: Prayer and what your right hands possess; he kept gargling it in his chest while his tongue could not utter it. Abu Ja'far said: Except that we found Sulayman al-Taymi had inserted between himself and Anas in this hadith an unnamed man."
"3668","3201 - As Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Waki' ibn al-Jarrah narrated to me, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from one who heard Anas ibn Malik saying: The general counsel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while his soul was gargling was: Prayer and what your right hands possess. So we looked into that unnamed man in this hadith whether anyone named him."
"3669","3202 - So we found Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The general counsel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when death came to him was: Prayer and what your right hands possess, until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began gargling it with his tongue. Then we considered whether this is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from another chain."
"3670","3203 - So we found al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi had narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Safinah the freedman of Umm Salamah, from Umm Salamah, she said: The general counsel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was: Prayer, prayer, and what your right hands possess, until he began rattling it in his chest while his tongue could not utter it. He said: In this hadith the Messenger of Allah's joining in his counsel what right hands possess to prayer, and stressing the command about that to people, proves its obligation - the obligation which allows no falling short, and without which faith is not complete. This interpretation for this meaning is the best interpretation for the prohibition of wasting wealth. Others interpreted it contrary to that, holding it is the prohibition of wasting the wealth which Allah made a support for people in their livelihoods and in what their affairs cannot be set right without, of livestock and other than livestock; and they argued with what is narrated from 'Amr ibn al-'As and from Qays ibn 'Asim in this meaning."
"3671","As Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn al-Furat narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Malik al-Himyari, from Bahir ibn Dhakhir al-Ma'afiri, that he heard 'Amr ibn al-'As in his Friday sermon saying: O people, beware of four traits, for they call to toil after rest, to straitness after ease, and to humiliation after honor: beware of many dependents, lowly condition, wasting wealth, and gossip after talk without attainment or benefit."
"3672","And as Yunus, al-Rabi' al-Muradi, and Sulayman al-Kaysani narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Ziyad al-Jassas, from al-Hasan, from Qays ibn 'Asim that when death came to him he said to his sons: Hold to wealth and earn it, for it alerts the noble and makes one independent of the base. Others interpreted it with another interpretation."
"3673","As 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Suqah narrated to us, from Ibn Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: A man asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr about wasting wealth, and he said: That Allah provides you provision and you spend it in what He forbade you. He said: All these interpretations are possible for what is intended by wasting wealth, except that the strongest in our hearts is the first interpretation among them; Allah knows best what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by them or by others. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"3674","Chapter 510 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what is said to one who calls with the call of ignorance or boasts with the boast of ignorance. 3204 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn al-Haytham ibn al-Jahm al-'Abdi al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: 'Awf al-A'rabi narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from 'Utayy ibn Damrah, he said: I saw with Ubayy ibn Ka'b a man boasting with the boast of ignorance, so Ubayy bit him without euphemism. His companions looked at him, and he said: As if you disapproved it. Ubayy said: I fear no one in this ever, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever boasts with the boast of ignorance, bite him and do not euphemize."
"3675","3205 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah - he is Ibn Hafs - narrated to us, he said: al-Sari ibn Yahya narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from 'Utayy, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whomever you hear calling with the call of ignorance, bite him with his father's shame and do not euphemize. He said: In this hadith is the Messenger's command about one heard calling with the call of ignorance, what he commanded therein. Then an objector said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while you narrate from him:"
"3676","3206 - So he mentioned what Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud had narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Zadhan, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Modesty is from faith, and faith is in Paradise; obscenity is from harshness, and harshness is in the Fire. He said: In this hadith obscenity is in the Fire, and the meaning of obscenity in the Fire is: the people of obscenity are in the Fire, for obscenity does not stand by itself; rather what is meant by mentioning it is those in whom it is. So our answer in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that the obscenity meant in this hadith is other than the obscenity meant in the first hadith - it is obscenity against one who does not deserve to be treated obscenely. Whoever commits that obscenity is among the people of the threat in the hadith in which that obscenity is mentioned. As for what is mentioned in the first hadith, it is punishment for one from whom came the claim of ignorance, because he calls by a man from among the people of the Fire - as they used to say: O Banu Bakr, O Banu Tamim, O Hamdan. Whoever calls thus to those ignorant ones who are among the people of the Fire deserves punishment. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made his punishment to be met with what is in the second hadith, so that it belittles him and what he called to, and so people desist from that in future and do not return to it. And this hadith is also narrated with another wording:"
"3677","3207 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-A'la informed us, he said: Khalid - he is Ibn al-Harith - narrated to us, he said: 'Awf narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from 'Utayy ibn Damrah, he said: I witnessed him one day - meaning Ubayy ibn Ka'b - when a man was boasting with the boast of ignorance, so he bit him with his father's shame and did not euphemize. The people seemed to disapprove that from him, so he said: Do not blame me, for the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: Whomever you see boasting with the boast of ignorance, bite him and do not euphemize. Its meaning is the meaning of the hadith before it, for the meaning of one who boasts with the boast of ignorance is from consoling himself to the people of ignorance, that is: attributing himself to them. Then an objector said: You have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what proves the rejection of this meaning."
"3678","3208 - So he mentioned what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah had narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar al-Ramadi narrated to us. 3209 - And what al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb had narrated to us, he said: 'Imran ibn Abi 'Imran al-Sufi narrated to us. 3210 - And what Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala' narrated to us - all together said: from Sufyan, he said: I memorized it from 'Amr, he said: I heard Jabir saying: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a campaign when a man from the Emigrants kicked a man from the Ansar. The Ansari said: O Ansar! And the Emigrant said: O Emigrants! The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard that and said: What is with the call of ignorance? They said: O Messenger of Allah, a man from the Emigrants kicked a man from the Ansar. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave it, for it is foul. This objector said: Had what is in the first hadith been as you narrated it, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have reproached one who abandoned the speech in the first hadith toward one who called with what he called in the other hadith. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that what is in the hadith does not contradict what is in the first hadith, for what is in this hadith is calling by the people of emigration to Allah and to His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the people of support for Allah Almighty and His Messenger - it was not like calling to an ignorant man from among the people of the Fire, a disbeliever in Allah and His Messenger. So there came about one who calls to the ignorant what is in the first hadith, and the like of it did not come about one who calls to an emigrant to Allah Almighty and to His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3679","Chapter 511 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the man who used to write for him: when he dictated 'Forgiving, Merciful' he would write 'Knowing, Wise,' and say to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I write such-and-such of this kind, and he would say: Yes, write as you wish. 3211 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man used to write before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he had read al-Baqarah and Al 'Imran; when a man read al-Baqarah and Al 'Imran he was counted eminent among us. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would dictate to him 'Forgiving, Merciful,' and he would write 'Knowing, Wise,' and say to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I write such-and-such, and he would say: Yes, write as you wish. And he would dictate to him 'Knowing, Wise,' and he would say: I write 'Hearing, Seeing,' and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say to him: Write whichever of that you wish, for it is so. Then he apostatized from Islam and joined the polytheists, saying: I am the most knowledgeable of you about Muhammad; he would entrust the matter to me until I wrote what I wished. That reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: The earth will not accept him. Anas said: Abu Talhah informed me that he saw the land where he died and found him cast out. Abu Talhah said: What is the matter with him? They said: We buried him repeatedly, but the earth did not accept him."
"3680","3212 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ayyub informed me, from Humayd, from Anas, then he mentioned the like of it. Then an objector said: You mentioned in what preceded in this book of yours, in the chapter on the difficulty of what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The Qur'an was revealed in seven letters, what you mentioned therein; and you mentioned therein that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not permit them what he permitted them therein of what you interpreted the seven mentioned letters upon, except from necessity and their inability to memorize the letters exactly; and that in reality what was revealed to him is as in the copied codices transmitted to us, by whose contents proof is established upon us; and that it is not allowed for us to recite anything of the Qur'an contrary to the wordings in them, even if its meaning is the meaning of what is in them. Yet in this hadith is what contradicts that and returns matters to the meanings in reality, to what was said upon them, even if wordings differ while meanings are equal. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that what is in this hadith is not of the meaning we mentioned in that chapter, for the meaning we mentioned in that chapter is about the Qur'an, not about other than it; and what is in the hadith we mentioned in this chapter may be about what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dictated to that scribe of his letters to people in calling them to Allah Almighty and describing Him to them as He is, glorious and mighty - of things which he ordered that scribe with, while the scribe wrote contrary to them of what had their meaning, since all of them describe Allah as He is."
"3681","Chapter 512 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what Muhammad ibn al-Hasan used as proof, of what Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, used to say about the permissibility of usury between Muslims and polytheists in the abode of war. 3213 - Yahya ibn 'Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Thawr, from Ma'mar, from Thabit, from Anas ibn Malik, that al-Hajjaj ibn 'Ilat al-Sulami said: O Messenger of Allah, I have family and wealth in Mecca, and I intend to go to them; if you permit me to say things about you I shall do so. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him to say what he wished. When he arrived in Mecca he said to his wife: The companions of Muhammad have been taken as spoils, and I have only come to take my wealth to buy from their spoils. That spread among the people of Mecca, and it reached al-'Abbas - meaning Ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib - so he was stricken and the Muslims in it hid, while the polytheists showed joy at that. Al-'Abbas would not pass by any of their gatherings but they said: O Abu al-Fadl, may Allah not grieve you. He sent a servant of his to al-Hajjaj ibn 'Ilat saying: Woe to you, what have you brought? What Allah and His Messenger promised is better than what you brought. Al-Hajjaj said to his servant: Convey peace to Abu al-Fadl and tell him to empty a house for me, for the news is as pleases him. When the servant came to him and told him, he stood to him, kissed between his eyes, and embraced him. Then al-Hajjaj ibn 'Ilat came to him and was alone with him in one of his houses, saying: Allah Almighty has given victory to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Khaybar and granted him its wealth and captives as spoils; I have only come to take my wealth. Conceal me for three nights, then mention as you wish. So al-'Abbas concealed him, then told the Muslims the true news and they rejoiced."
"3682","3214 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Qurrah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman and 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that 'Amir ibn Yahya al-Ma'afiri informed them from Hanash that he said: We were with Fadalah ibn 'Ubayd in a campaign when a necklace containing gold, silver, and jewels fell to me and my companions. I wanted to buy it, so I asked Fadalah. He said: Remove its gold and place it in the scale, and place gold in the other scale; then take only like for like, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him take only like for like."
"3683","3215 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abu Hani' al-Khawlani informed me that he heard 'Ulayy ibn Rabah al-Lakhmi saying: I heard Fadalah ibn 'Ubayd al-Ansari saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought, while at Khaybar, a necklace containing gold and beads, from the spoils being sold. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the gold in the necklace to be removed alone, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gold for gold, weight for weight."
"3684","3216 - And as Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: al-Muqri' narrated to us, he said: Haywah narrated to us, from Abu Hani', then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: In these reports usury was then unlawful in the abode of Islam among the people of Islam. Then we found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his sermon in the Farewell Pilgrimage."
"3685","3217 - What al-Rabi' al-Muradi had narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in his sermon on the day of 'Arafah in the Farewell Pilgrimage: The usury of ignorance is abolished, and the first usury I abolish is the usury of al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib, for it is abolished entirely."
"3686","3218 - And what Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, he said: Hannad ibn al-Sari narrated to us, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Gharqadah - meaning Shabib - from Sulayman ibn 'Amr, from his father 'Amr ibn al-Ahwas, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Verily every usury of the usury of ignorance is abolished; you have your principal wealth, you do no wrong nor are wronged."
"3687","3219 - And what 'Ali ibn Ma'bad had narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn 'Azib ibn Shabib ibn Gharqadah Abu Gharqadah narrated to us, from Shabib ibn Gharqadah, from Sulayman ibn 'Amr, from 'Amr ibn al-Ahwas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So in that is proof that usury stood in Mecca while it was an abode of war until it was conquered, for the departure of ignorance was only by its conquest. In the Messenger's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The first usury I abolish is our usury, the usury of al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib, is proof that al-'Abbas's usury stood until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) abolished it, for he abolishes only what stood, not what had fallen before his abolishing it. The conquest of Khaybar was in year seven of the Hijrah, the conquest of Mecca in year eight, and the Farewell Pilgrimage in year ten. So in that is proof that al-'Abbas had usury until the conquest of Mecca, while he was Muslim before that; and in that is proof that usury was lawful between Muslims and polytheists in Mecca while it was an abode of war, though then unlawful among Muslims in the abode of Islam. In that is proof of the permissibility of usury between Muslims and belligerents in the abode of war, as Abu Hanifah and al-Thawri say."
"3688","Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah with that. Muhammad said: And it is our saying."
"3689","And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sufyan with that. Abu Ja'far said: Ibrahim al-Nakha'i said it before them."
"3690","As Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas narrated to us, he said: 'Ali narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Aban ibn Salih narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no harm in a dinar for two dinars in the abode of war between Muslims and belligerents. Among what proves that the ruling of usury between Muslims and belligerents in the abode of war differs from its ruling between them in the abode of Islam is that al-'Abbas's usury which the Prophet's abolition of the usury of ignorance met cannot be free of one of two aspects: its origin was before the prohibition of usury, then the prohibition of usury supervened; or it was during the prohibition of usury - I mean by that prohibition in these two aspects in the abode of emigration. If it was before the prohibition then the prohibition supervened in the abode of emigration and in the abode of war, it must be void in whichever places, of war or Islam. If it was after the prohibition it is more void. Since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in his sermon what proves it stood until he abolished it, that proves before his abolishing it, it was in the place of usury therein - unlike usury in the abode of emigration - for had it been in the abode of emigration it would not have stood in any condition after the prohibition, for if its origin was during its prohibition it was not established, and if before its prohibition then its prohibition supervened and abolished it. If one confuses with what was in al-'Abbas's affair - the Muslims capturing him and taking ransom from him - verifying thereby that he was not Muslim in Mecca, saying: had he been Muslim before its conquest his Islam would have negated that from him, it is said to him: the capture and ransom do not negate his Islam, for the Prophet ransomed captives of Badr among whom were Muslims held back, and al-'Abbas declared his Islam and the Prophet affirmed it."
"3691","Chapter 513 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about inheritances divided in ignorance and inheritances of ignorance which Islam met before they were divided. 3221 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahim known as Sa'iqah narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Sha'tha', from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every division divided in ignorance stands upon what it was divided; and every division which Islam meets is upon the division of Islam. Abu Ja'far said: As for Ibn 'Uyaynah, he narrated this hadith from 'Amr and did not go beyond him with it."
"3692","3222 - As 'Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr, then he mentioned it. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith too is what strengthens what we mentioned in the first chapter, for in it is what necessitates that had the division of inheritance been in Mecca before its conquest upon other than the division of Islam, that would have stood upon that division, even though its division then in the abode of emigration and in the rulings of Islam therein contradicted it. Likewise dealing with the usury we mentioned then in Mecca between Muslims and its polytheist people was lawful, though in the abode of emigration and in the rulings of Islam therein contrary to that. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"3693","Chapter 514 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about rulings of usurpations in ignorance over which they disputed to him in Islam. 3223 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah together narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from 'Alqamah ibn Wa'il, from Wa'il ibn Hujr, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when two men came disputing about land. One said: O Messenger of Allah, this one seized my land in ignorance - he is Imru' al-Qays ibn 'Abis al-Kindi - and his opponent is Rabi'ah ibn 'Aydan. He said to him: Your proof, your proof. He said: I have no proof. He said: His oath. He said: Then he will take it away. He said: You have nothing but that. When he stood to swear, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever cuts off land unjustly shall meet Allah while He is angry with him."
"3694","3224 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from 'Alqamah ibn Wa'il, from his father, he said: A man from Hadramawt and a man from Kindah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Hadrami said: O Messenger of Allah, this one overcame me in land that was mine. The Kindi said: It is my land in my hand; I cultivate it; he has no right in it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Hadrami: Do you have proof? He said: No. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then I make him swear. He said: He has no oath. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have nothing from him but that. He went to make him swear, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If he swears over your wealth unjustly to consume it, he shall meet Allah Almighty while He turns away from him."
"3695","3225 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Jandal ibn Waliq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: The Hadrami said: O Messenger of Allah, this one overcame me in land that belonged to my father. In this hadith is the dispute of the two men mentioned therein to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a usurpation one claimed against the other that it was from him in ignorance; and the Messenger's calling the claimant for proof if he had it for what he claimed before him, and informing him that he has the oath of the defendant if he seeks it. In that is proof that had he established proof before him for what he claimed, he would have judged for him against the one he claimed against; and in that is proof that the usurper did not own by his usurping what he usurped in ignorance. Likewise a belligerent who usurps a belligerent's land in the abode of war, then both become Muslim and dispute about it to the Imam of the Muslims - he looks between them in that and judges between them as he judges the like had it been between two Muslims in the abode of Islam. Muhammad ibn al-Hasan also held this view, except that he used to say: if their king to whom dispute was brought in his abode of rule gave it to its usurper by his usurping, then dispute about that is brought to the Imam of the Muslims in the abode of Islam, he enforces that and does not return it to the usurped one; but if there was no dispute to their king nor enforcement from him to its usurper, he looks between the usurper and the usurped one and judges as he judges usurpation of the people of Islam."
"3696","Chapter 515 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the man who used to write for him: he would dictate 'Knowing, Wise' and he would write 'Hearing, Knowing,' while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not reproach him - then he apostatized from Islam. Was he from Quraysh, from the Ansar, or from others? 3226 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn Abi al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Suhayb narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: There was a Christian man who became Muslim and read al-Baqarah and Al 'Imran, and wrote for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); then he returned to Christianity, saying: Muhammad reads only what I wrote for him. Allah Almighty caused him to die; they buried him, and in the morning the earth had cast him out. They said: This is the deed of Muhammad and his companions; they dug up our companion and threw him out. So they dug and deepened, and in the morning the earth had cast him out. They said: This is the deed of Muhammad and his companions; they dug up our companion and threw him out. So they dug for him and deepened in the earth as much as they could, and in the morning the earth had cast him out; so they knew it was not from people, and they threw him away. Abu Ja'far said: By this hadith it became clear, by Allah's praise, that he was neither from Quraysh nor from the Ansar, but a Christian. Then an objector said: You mentioned before this chapter in this book of yours that you denied this man was the one to whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dictated while he wrote contrary, with the Messenger approving for him of the meaning he dictated the meaning of what he wrote - yet in this chapter is what proves he wrote for the Prophet other than what he dictated. The answer is that the earlier scribe and this scribe are two different men, and the earth casting out this one does not contradict the earlier account."
"3697","Chapter 516 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to Usamah ibn Zayd about the man he killed after he said to him: I am a Muslim - what he said to him in that. 3227 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Zibyan, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent us to some people of Juhaynah called al-Huraqat. I came upon a man among them and went to stab him; he said: There is no god but Allah. So I stabbed him and killed him. I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him. He said: You killed him after he testified that there is no god but Allah! I said: O Messenger of Allah, he said it only seeking refuge. He said: Did you split open his heart?"
"3698","3228 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Adam informed us, from Abu Mu'awiyah, from al-A'mash, from Abu Zibyan, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent us to al-Huraqat of Juhaynah. We attacked at dawn while they had noticed us; we went out in their tracks, and I caught up with a man among them. Whenever I reached him he said: There is no god but Allah; then I killed him, saying: He did not say it sincerely; he said it only from fear of weapons. He said: Did he say: There is no god but Allah, then you killed him?! Why did you not split open his heart to know whether he said it only from fear of weapons? Usamah said: He kept repeating it to me: Did he say: There is no god but Allah, then you killed him?! Until I wished I had not become Muslim except that day."
"3699","3229 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn 'Ali narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman - meaning Ibn Mahdi - narrated to us, he said: Mansur ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated to us, from Husayn, from Abu Zibyan, he said: I heard Usamah ibn Zayd saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent us in an army to al-Huraqat - a clan of Juhaynah. When we defeated them, I and a man from the Ansar rushed to a man among them. He said: There is no god but Allah. The Ansari held back from him, while I thought he said it only seeking refuge, so I killed him. The Ansari returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him the account. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Usamah, you killed a man after he said - meaning: There is no god but Allah. What will you do with 'There is no god but Allah' on the Day of Resurrection? He kept saying that until I wished I had not become Muslim except that day. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is Usamah's killing of the one he killed after his saying: There is no god but Allah, and the Messenger's reproach of that; yet Usamah has the standing in Islam which he has. Then an objector said: This proves the hadith has no basis, for otherwise his conditions with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have remained as they were before that, given this grave crime. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that it may be that the meaning by which Usamah's conditions remained after this deed as before is a meaning that necessitated excuse for him in it - that he interpreted and erred in interpretation, killing on interpretation, not deliberately shedding protected blood. Excuse by interpretation removes sin though the act remains wrong, and the Prophet's reproach was to teach caution in blood, not to declare him apostate. And we ask Allah for success."
"3700","Chapter 517 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the people whom Khalid ibn al-Walid killed after they said: We have left, we have left. 3230 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Khalid ibn al-Walid to Banu Jadhimah. He called them to Islam, but they did not do well saying: We have become Muslim; instead they kept saying: We have left, we have left. Khalid began killing and capturing, and gave each man among us his captive. Then one day Khalid ordered each man among us to kill his captive. I said: By Allah, I shall not kill my captive, nor shall any man among my companions kill his captive. When we came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we mentioned Khalid's deed to him. He raised his hands and said: O Allah, I disavow to You what Khalid did - twice."
"3701","3231 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Nuh ibn Habib al-Qumasi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. He said: In this hadith is Banu Jadhimah's saying: We have left, we have left; and what Khalid did among them; and what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did about Khalid's deed - all mentioned in this hadith. Then an objector said: What is the meaning that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left taking their due from Khalid when he did among them after their Islam? So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that their saying: We have left may be upon Islam, may be upon entering the religion of the Sabians, and may be upon other than that - except that it is a removal from one thing to another. What the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did - his reproach of Khalid ibn al-Walid for what he did - was that he should have verified their affairs and stood upon their intent by their saying: We have left: is that toward Islam or toward other than it? Since he did not do that, he disavowed to Allah Almighty what he did, but did not exact for them what he did not know with certainty was obligatory for them in Khalid's killing them. And we ask Allah for success."
"3702","Chapter 518 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what happened between 'Ammar ibn Yasir and Khalid ibn al-Walid about the people to whom both were sent, who protected themselves. 3232 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Shakib al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn 'Ubaydullah, from Muhammad ibn Shaddad, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: al-Ashtar narrated to me, he said: Khalid ibn al-Walid narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me with 'Ammar in a detachment. We found a household who had declared unity. 'Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: These have protected themselves from us by their declaring unity. So I deemed him foolish and paid no heed to his saying. When we returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he complained of me to him. When he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not vindicate him against me, he turned away with his eyes shedding tears. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Khalid, do not revile 'Ammar, for whoever reviles 'Ammar, Allah reviles him; and whoever deems 'Ammar foolish, Allah Almighty deems him foolish. He said: I said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, none of my sins is more fearful to me than it; so seek forgiveness for me. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought forgiveness for me. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is 'Ammar's saying about that household who had declared unity - that they protected themselves by their declaring unity - and that Khalid paid no heed to his saying. Khalid's intent about that household was like Usamah's intent about his slain one whom he killed after his declaring unity; and what 'Ammar did among them hit the truth of Allah's ruling about them. Each of them acted upon interpretation, and the Prophet vindicated the interpreter who hit the truth and sought forgiveness for the one who erred, without declaring him disbeliever."
"3703","Chapter 519 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the Khath'amite party to whom he sent Khalid, and his killing them after they protected themselves by prostration. 3233 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Khalid ibn al-Walid: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him to some people of Khath'am. They protected themselves by prostration, yet he killed them. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid their blood-money with half the indemnity, then said: I am free of every Muslim with a polytheist; their two fires shall not see each other. A questioner asked about the meaning by which what Khalid ibn al-Walid did to those people was lifted from him, after he stood upon their prostration and Islam being obligatory for them thereby. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that prostration does not prove the reality of one from whom it comes, whose Islam was not known before that - for it may be to Allah Almighty, thus Islam for its doer; or it may be in glorification of a chief, thus not Islam for its doer but offense for him and for the one done to if he approves it from its doer. Since prostration is as we mentioned, bearing what we described, Khalid's act fell into what no proof stood against him in killing one whose killing may be lawful for him - except that verification was due in that until he knew those people's intent by their prostration: is it Islam or other? For that reason the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) paid their blood-money with what he paid, voluntarily from him and graciously, without its being obligatory upon Khalid. And we ask Allah for success."
"3704","Chapter 520 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the earth casting out the buried man who killed the one who said: There is no god but Allah, killing him on the assumption that was seeking refuge. 3234 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from 'Asim al-Ahwal, from al-Sumayt ibn al-Sumayr, from 'Imran ibn Husayn, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent us in a detachment. A man from the offspring of my father charged a man from the polytheists; when he covered him with the spear he said: I am a Muslim. So he killed him, then came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O Messenger of Allah, I have sinned, so seek forgiveness for me. He said: What is that? He said: I charged a man, and when I covered him with the spear he said: I am a Muslim; I thought he was seeking refuge, so I killed him. He said: Did you not split open his heart to make clear to you? He said: And would it be clear to me? He said: He said to you with his tongue, yet you did not believe him for what is in his heart. The man did not live long before he died; they buried him, and in the morning he was upon the face of the earth. We said: An enemy dug him up. So we ordered our slaves and freedmen to guard him, and in the morning he was upon the face of the earth. We said: Perhaps they were heedless, so we guarded ourselves, and in the morning he was upon the face of the earth. So we came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him. He said: The earth accepts one worse than him, but Allah Almighty wished to inform you of the enormity of blood. Then he said: Take him to the foot of this mountain and pile stones upon him. So we did."
"3705","3235 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from 'Asim al-Ahwal, he said: al-Sumayt narrated to us, from 'Imran, he said: A man from the offspring of my father met the enemy, then he mentioned this hadith. Abu Ja'far said: We mentioned in what preceded in these chapters of this kind what suffices us from speech in this chapter - except that in this chapter is a phrase, the saying of al-Khuza'i, the man of the story mentioned therein, to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I have committed a sin, so seek forgiveness for me. That proves he was among those against whom proof stood for his crime in killing one who said to him the like of what the one he killed said, killing him upon that. Yet in it is his thought by his saying: I am a Muslim, seeking refuge - that may be addition from him in excusing himself to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for killing that man: because killing one seeking refuge by that saying is lighter than killing one who said that saying not to seek refuge but from true entry into Islam. That did not lift from him the punishment of his sin therein; and what was from Allah Almighty therein was because of that. Allah knows best the reality of the matter therein. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"3706","Chapter 521 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about hides of carrion: their purification by tanning, and what contradicts that. 3236 - Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abi Layla, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ukaym, he said: The letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was read to us while we were in the land of Juhaynah, I being a young lad: Do not benefit from carrion by hide nor sinew."
"3707","3237 - 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja' ibn al-Walid al-Sakuni narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Ghaniyyah, from al-Hakam, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: The letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us."
"3708","3238 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, from al-Hakam, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to us. Abu Ja'far said: What is in Shu'bah's hadith of Ibn 'Ukaym's saying: The letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was read to us while we were in the land of Juhaynah, I being a young lad, verifies his presence at that and his hearing it from the Messenger's letter; while what is in Ibn Abi Ghaniyyah's hadith: The letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us, and what is in al-Shaybani's hadith: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to us, may be of what Ibn 'Ukaym did not attend, his saying: The letter came to us meaning: He wrote to our people - as al-Nazzal ibn Sabrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us."
"3709","3239 - Fahd ibn Sulayman and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, they said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from al-Nazzal ibn Sabrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: In ignorance we and you were called Banu 'Abd Manaf; today we are Banu 'Abdullah and you are Banu 'Abdullah - meaning Nazzal's people. Thus in the hadith; yet that was not by Nazzal's hearing it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nor his presence at his saying it - rather by his people's hearing it from him and their presence at his saying it. This is allowed in Arab speech and found in much hadith."
"3710","3240 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Khalid - meaning al-Hadhdha' - from al-Hakam, he said: We came to 'Abdullah ibn 'Ukaym; the elders entered while I sat at the door. They came out and informed me from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ukaym that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to Juhaynah: Do not benefit from carrion by hide nor sinew - written two months before he died. Abu Ja'far said: By this hadith we stood upon the time in which the Messenger's letter with what he wrote therein was; then we uncovered the reality of this hadith."
"3711","3241 - So we found 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Amr al-Dimashqi had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Sadaqah ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Maryam, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ukaym, he said: Elders of Juhaynah narrated to me, saying: The letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us, or the letter was read to us: Do not benefit from carrion by anything. So what is in this hadith verifies that Ibn 'Ukaym did not witness that of the Messenger's letter nor attend its reading to those mentioned therein as read to; and these elders of Juhaynah were not named to us so we know them and know they are among those from whom the like is taken for their companionship to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or for conditions in them besides that necessitating acceptance of their narrations. Since we did not find that for them, no proof stands with this hadith in our view. The hadith of Ibn 'Abbas from Maymunah which we mentioned before in this book of ours - his ordering them to tan the hide of the sheep that died, and his saying to them then: Only its meat was forbidden - is preferable for soundness of its coming, straightness of its path, and probity of its narrators. It is also narrated from Ibn 'Abbas of this hadith, mentioning the sheep belonged to Sawdah bint Zam'ah, and mentioning what proves that saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to them was after Allah Almighty sent down the prohibition of carrion."
"3712","3242 - As Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari had narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us; and as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us - Salih said in his hadith: from Simak ibn Harb, and Ibn Abi Dawud said in his hadith, he said: Simak ibn Harb narrated to us - then both said together in their hadiths: from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: A sheep belonging to Sawdah bint Zam'ah died. She said: O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so has died - meaning the sheep. He said: Why did you not take its hide? She said: Should we take the hide of a sheep that died?! The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Almighty only said: Say: I do not find in what is revealed to me forbidden to one who eats it - the verse; there is no harm in your tanning it and benefiting from it. She said: So she sent, had its hide flayed and tanned, and made from it a waterskin until it wore out. Then we found from Ibn 'Abbas in that also:"
"3713","3243 - What Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Wa'lah, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any hide tanned has become pure."
"3714","3244 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ibn Wa'lah, from Ibn 'Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the leather is tanned it has become pure."
"3715","3245 - And what al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Jizi had narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Wa'lah, that he said to Ibn 'Abbas: We raid the land of the West, and our waterskins are only hides of carrion. Ibn 'Abbas said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Any skin tanned has become pure."
"3716","3246 - And what al-Rabi' also had narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, that he heard Abu al-Khayr informing from Ibn Wa'lah that he asked Ibn 'Abbas saying: We raid this West, and they have skins holding water while they are idolaters. Ibn 'Abbas said: Tanning is purification. Ibn Wa'lah said to him: From your opinion or from something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Rather from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). In that is what necessitates permitting hides of carrion when tanned; and in this chapter are reports narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides these reports sufficing from the rest. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"3717","Chapter 522 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his forbidding riding upon hides of predators. 3247 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Nasr ibn Marzuq together narrated to us, they said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Majid ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from 'Asim ibn Damrah, from 'Ali, peace be upon him, that he was brought a mule with a brocade saddle. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade brocade, riding upon it, sitting upon it, hides of tigers, sitting upon them, and riding upon them."
"3718","3248 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from al-Hasan ibn Suhayl ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mitharah - they are hides of predators."
"3719","3249 - Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Humran narrated to me, he said: Mu'awiyah performed Hajj and invited some Ansar in the Ka'bah, saying: I adjure you by Allah, did you not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding rows of tigers? They said: O Allah, yes. He said: And I bear witness."
"3720","3250 - Isma'il ibn Hamdawayh al-Bikandi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal al-Anmati narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Shaykh al-Huna'i, he said: I was among a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Mu'awiyah. Mu'awiyah said: I adjure you by Allah, do you know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade riding rows of tigers? They said: O Allah, yes. He said: And I bear witness."
"3721","3251 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Bahir ibn Sa'd - meaning al-Kala'i - from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from al-Miqdam ibn Ma'di Karib: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade riding upon hides of predators."
"3722","3252 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id - meaning Ibn Abi 'Arubah - narrated to us; and Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Qasim al-Kufi narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah, from Qatadah, from Abu Malih ibn Usamah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade hides of predators. Abu Ja'far said: In what we narrated in the chapter before this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Any hide tanned has become pure, is what generalized all hides, including hides of predators. No one may exclude from what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) generalized except by what necessitates exclusion - a recited verse, a transmitted sunnah, or consensus of the people of knowledge. Since that is so, hides of predators enter the hides whose purification by tanning is obligatory; and we understand the prohibition in the reports we narrated in this chapter about riding upon hides of predators was not because they are impure after tanning, but for another meaning - the riding of non-Arabs upon them. What proves what we mentioned is in the hadith of 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, of his reporting from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his forbidding brocade - riding upon it and sitting upon it - yet that was no prohibition of wearing clothes made from it, for Companions and Followers wore brocade."
"3723","Among what proves what we mentioned is what Yusuf ibn Yazid had narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab saw a man wearing a cap whose lining was of fox hides. He threw it off his head saying: How do you know it may not be from a slaughtered animal? In this is proof that had he known it was slaughtered he would not have disliked his wearing what is in it."
"3724","And what Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Mu'min al-Marwazi had narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Hubayrah narrated to us by hearing, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Abu Nadrah, from Mutarrif ibn 'Abdullah, he said: I entered upon 'Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, while a tailor was sewing a cloak of his upon a fox-fur covering."
"3725","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Artah informed us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir that he saw no harm in hides of predators when tanned."
"3726","Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed me, from Qurrah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Haywa'il, he said: Abu Ayyub wanted to ride for a need, so I called for his mount with tiger saddles. He removed the pad, and I said to him: The two side-pieces are tiger. He said: Only the pad is forbidden. Do you not see that Abu Ayyub, may Allah be pleased with him, disliked riding upon the tiger pad but did not dislike riding upon the saddle whose side-pieces were tiger? In that is proof of what we mentioned. These Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we mentioned - their school about tiger hides was what we narrated from them; and in that is proof they disliked of them only what makes them like non-Arabs in using them. We know of no one among the Companions in that besides what we mentioned; and we found from their Followers, may Allah be pleased with them, in that what proves permissibility too, and that the dislike attached for what we mentioned, not for what necessitates prohibition."
"3727","As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, that 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr had tiger saddles."
"3728","And as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn 'Atiq, he said: I saw al-Hasan al-Basri upon a striped saddle, and I saw Muhammad ibn Sirin upon a striped saddle. Abu Ja'far said: In what we mentioned of the use by those Followers we mentioned is proof they did not see riding upon them as unlawful. There remains in this chapter the hadith of Abu Rayhanah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his forbidding riding upon tigers, which we delayed to bring in a chapter after this chapter more fitting - Allah willing. And with Allah is success."
"3729","Chapter 523 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his forbidding mukama'ah and mu'akamah. 3253 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Lahi'ah narrated to me, from 'Ayyash ibn 'Abbas, he said: Abu al-Husayn al-Haytham ibn Shafi narrated to me, he said: I set out with Abu 'Amir al-Hajari to Iliya' to pray therein, while the judge of Iliya' people then was Abu Rayhanah al-Azdi. One day Abu 'Amir preceded me going early to the mosque. I sat with my companion, who said to me: Did you catch Abu Rayhanah's stories? I said: No. He said: He narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade ten: filing teeth, tattooing, plucking, a man lying with a man without garment, a woman lying with a woman without garment, silk - placing it at the bottom of your clothes as non-Arabs do, silk - placing it at the top of your clothes as non-Arabs do, tiger, plunder, and the ring except for one with authority."
"3730","3254 - Yahya ibn 'Uthman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam, Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr, and Hassan ibn Ghalib al-Hajari narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Suwayd ibn Hayyan narrated to us, he said: 'Ayyash ibn 'Abbas al-Qitbani narrated to me, from al-Haytham ibn Shafi, he informed him, he said: I went out with Abu 'Amir al-Hajari, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3731","3255 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: al-Mufaddal ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from 'Ayyash ibn 'Abbas al-Qitbani, from Abu al-Husayn al-Haytham ibn Shafi, that he heard him saying: I went out with a companion of mine named Abu 'Amir, a man from al-Ma'afir, to pray in Iliya', then he mentioned the like of it. Thus Ibn Lahi'ah, 'Abdullah ibn Suwayd, and al-Mufaddal ibn Fadalah narrated this hadith, all saying therein: mukama'ah of a man with a man, and mukama'ah of a woman with a woman. Yahya ibn Ayyub also narrated from 'Ayyash ibn 'Abbas but differed, saying: mu'akamah."
"3732","3256 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from 'Ayyash ibn 'Abbas, from al-Haytham Abu al-Husayn al-Hajari, from Abu 'Amir al-Hajari, that he heard Abu Rayhanah, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid ten traits: mu'akamah of a man with a man, and a woman with a woman in a garment with nothing between them - meaning a blanket - filing, plucking, tattooing, plunder, riding tigers, taking brocade upon the shoulder, taking brocade at the bottom of cloaks, and the ring except for one with authority. Abu Ja'far said: The mukama'ah mentioned in the hadiths of Ibn Lahi'ah, 'Abdullah ibn Suwayd, and al-Mufaddal ibn Fadalah is the lying together mentioned therein; and the mu'akamah mentioned in Yahya ibn Ayyub's hadith is joining one thing to another - from it is said: I packed clothes when I tied some to others. Among what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the prohibition of these meanings."
"3733","3257 - What Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahim al-Marwi had narrated to us, he said: Duhaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, he said: al-Dahhak ibn 'Uthman narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'id al-Khudri, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man shall not look at the nakedness of a man, nor a woman at the nakedness of a woman; nor shall a man lie with a man in one garment, nor a woman with a woman in one garment."
"3734","3258 - And what Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ya'la ibn al-Harith al-Muharibi narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Hisham, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: A woman shall not touch a woman skin to skin, nor a man a man. Al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated Abu Rayhanah's hadith which we mentioned from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Husayn, but differed in its chain and text from those we mentioned in this chapter."
"3735","3259 - As al-Rabi' al-Muradi had narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid - meaning Ibn Abi Habib - from Abu al-Husayn al-Hajari, from Abu Rayhanah - without mentioning anyone between him and him - that he said: It reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade filing, tattooing, plucking tufts, secret marriage, mukama'ah, continuous fasting, and touching. 3260 - And 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz permitted us from Abu 'Ubayd about mukama'ah: it is a man lying with a man in one garment, taken from kami', the bedfellow; he said: from it the husband of a woman is called her kami'. Abu 'Ubayd said in this permission: This hadith is narrated from al-Layth's hadith, mentioning what Abu al-Nadr narrated from al-Layth ibn Sa'd, from 'Ayyash ibn 'Abbas, raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade muka'amah. Abu 'Ubayd said: Muka'amah is a man muzzling his companion, taken from the camel's muzzle when it is in heat - it is said: I muzzled him; so he is muzzled; likewise every bound mouth is muzzled. Dhu al-Rummah said, describing fear stopping speech. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made veiling the face like that muzzling. As for mukama'ah, it is a man lying with his companion in one garment, taken from kami', the bedfellow. Aws ibn Hujr said likewise. As for filing in this hadith, 'Ali permitted us from Abu 'Ubayd, he said: it is she who files her teeth for beautification. And we ask Allah for success."
"3736","Chapter 524 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Returning is like raiding. 3261 - 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from al-Layth ibn Sa'd, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh al-Kindi narrated to me, from Shufayy al-Asbahi, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Returning is like raiding. Thus 'Abd al-Malik narrated to us without mentioning anyone between Haywah and Shufayy."
"3737","3262 - As for Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Kufi, he narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rumh narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Haywah ibn Shurayh, from Ibn Shufayy, from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: This Ibn Shufayy is Husayn ibn Shafi."
"3738","As al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Jizi and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Sa'id ibn Kathir ibn 'Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Nafi' ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Haywah ibn Shurayh, from Husayn ibn Shafi, from his father, he said: In Paradise is a river of oil. Abu Ja'far said: Shufayy is Ibn Mati'; I heard Yahya ibn 'Uthman saying: Shufayy was the son of Tubay''s wife, and Tubay' was the son of Ka'b's wife. So we considered the Messenger's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Returning is like raiding, finding it may be joined to speech preceding it which 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr did not attend from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - Allah knows best - that the Messenger was asked about people who returned, fearing those of their enemy more numerous would rally against them, to their Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to add to their number strength to fight their enemy, then rally against their enemy raiding; that was their duty. 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr in what he missed of that and what he caught is like what 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Omen is in three: woman, horse, and house - a piece of it flew in the sky and a piece on earth; she said: By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not say it thus; rather he said: The people of ignorance used to say that. Like Zayd ibn Thabit when it reached him from Rafi' ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, of his mentioning from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade sharecropping, saying: I know better the Prophet's forbidding it - that he forbade it only in a specific form. Likewise this returning may be joined to what preceded it, making it like raiding in reward."
"3739","Chapter 525 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The raider has his reward, and the equipper has his reward and the raider's reward. 3263 - 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from al-Layth ibn Sa'd, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to me, from Shufayy al-Asbahi, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The raider has his reward, and the equipper has his reward and the raider's reward. Thus 'Abd al-Malik narrated to us, not inserting anyone between Haywah and Shufayy therein."
"3740","3264 - Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Kufi had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rumh narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Haywah ibn Shurayh, from Ibn Shufayy, from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. The people of knowledge differed about equipping payments in raiding; the highest we found therein of what is narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3741","3265 - What Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn 'Uthman ibn Kathir ibn Dinar narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Amr ibn 'Utbah narrated to me, from Ibn Jarir ibn 'Abdullah al-Bajali, from his father, that Mu'awiyah wrote to Jarir about a levy he struck: As for after, we have lifted from you and your son the equipping payment. So Jarir wrote to him: I pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon Islam; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held my hand and stipulated upon me: sincere counsel to every Muslim. If I am active in this levy we go out in it; if not, we gave from our wealth what the goer takes. Al-Mas'udi said: This is the best we heard about equipping payments. Haywah's hadith which we mentioned in this chapter is narrated by 'Abdullah ibn Lahi'ah from Haywah contrary to what al-Layth narrated from him in chain and text."
"3742","3266 - As Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la had narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to me, from Haywah ibn Shurayh, from Husayn ibn Shafi al-Asbahi, from the Companions that they said: O Messenger of Allah, give us ruling about the equipper and the equipped in the path of Allah Almighty. He said: The raider has the reward of what he intended, and the equipper has the reward of the equipper and the equipped - without mentioning anyone between Husayn ibn Shafi and the Companions. Abu Ja'far said: As for what later people of knowledge after the Companions and their Followers said in this chapter."
"3743","As Ahmad ibn Abi 'Imran had narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sama'ah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan informed us, he said: Ya'qub narrated to us, from Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, he said: I dislike equipping payments when Muslims have fay'; if they have no fay' there is no harm in some strengthening others - Muhammad did not report disagreement between Abu Yusuf and Abu Hanifah in that. Abu Ja'far said: We considered what we mentioned in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then from those Companions we mentioned, then what we mentioned from those after them among the people of knowledge. What we mentioned therein from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whose outward is permissibility of equipping payments may be at need when Muslims then had no fay' sufficing; and what we mentioned therein from Jarir, which Mu'awiyah did not reproach, may also have been when Muslims had no fay' sufficing them. What we mentioned therein from Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, and his companions was in our view - Allah knows best - upon that what is taken in equipping payments is taken only for the need with which accepting charity is allowed; when Muslims have fay' it is more fitting to refrain from charity and what has its ruling, since it is the washings of people's sins, and to suffice with fay' which is contrary to that and not from the washings of people's sins. When that is absent, need permits accepting that from necessity. We mentioned in this chapter and the chapter before it Shufayy al-Asbahi with dammah; and we ask Allah for success."
"3744","3267 - What Yunus had narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed us that Abu 'Ali al-Hamdani narrated to him, he said: We were with Fadalah ibn 'Ubayd at Rudis in the land of Rome when a companion of ours died. Fadalah ibn 'Ubayd ordered his grave leveled, then said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding leveling them."
"3745","3268 - And what 'Imran ibn Musa al-Ta'i had narrated to us, he said: 'Ayyash ibn al-Walid al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-A'la ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Thumamah ibn Shufi, he said: We went out in a campaign in Mu'awiyah's time, with Fadalah ibn 'Ubayd al-Ansari over us. A cousin of mine called Nafi' ibn 'Ubayd died. He stood with us at his pit; when we buried him he said: Lighten over his pit, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command leveling graves. So by these two hadiths we understood that the Thumamah mentioned in one is the Abu 'Ali mentioned in the other, and that the Abu 'Ali mentioned in one is the Thumamah mentioned in the other. And we ask Allah for success."
"3746","Chapter 526 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about apes and pigs: are they of what was transformed of nations or not? 3269 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from 'Alqamah ibn Marthad, from al-Mughirah ibn 'Abdullah al-Yashkuri, from al-Ma'rur ibn Suwayd, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about apes and pigs: are they of what was transformed? He said: Allah Almighty never destroys a people or transforms a people then makes for them offspring or progeny; apes and pigs were created before that."
"3747","3270 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3748","3271 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated to us, from Mis'ar ibn Kidam, from 'Alqamah ibn Marthad, from al-Mughirah al-Ashkuri - Rawh said: thus Yusuf said - from al-Ma'rur, from 'Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah never destroys a people then makes for them offspring or progeny."
"3749","Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from 'Alqamah ibn Marthad, from al-Mustawrid ibn al-Ahnaf, from Ibn Mas'ud that he was asked about apes and pigs: are they from the offspring of the apes and pigs that were transformed, or from offspring of apes and pigs that were on earth before that? 'Abdullah said: Allah never transforms a nation then makes for it progeny; rather these are from offspring of apes and pigs that were on earth before that - Yazid did not mention in this hadith of his from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3750","3272 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal and Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, they said: Dawud ibn Abi al-Furat narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Zayd al-'Abdi narrated to us, from Abu al-A'yan, from Abu al-Ahwas al-Jushami, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: We asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about apes and pigs: are they from the offspring of the Jews? He said: Allah Almighty never curses a people then transforms them so they have offspring; rather this is a creation that existed. When Allah was angry with the Jews He transformed them and made them like them. Some people said: In Allah's Book is what rejects these reports you narrated in this chapter about denying one Allah destroyed or transformed having offspring or progeny - His saying Almighty: And He made of them apes and pigs - meaning those He made of the people He was wrathful with and cursed; mentioning that with definiteness, not indefiniteness, so it was upon the existing known apes and pigs, not upon others. Had it been upon other apes and pigs it would have been: He made among them apes and pigs, indefinite not definite. So our answer to them in that - by Allah's guidance and help - is that apes and pigs may have been created before that upon what they are, like other created things, not transformed from a form to apes and pigs; they reproduced and had progeny like other creatures. Then Allah's making the people apes and pigs was making them like the existing apes and pigs in form, without offspring remaining for them. And we ask Allah for success."
"3751","Chapter 527 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his fearing the mouse may be of the transformed, and whether after that came what lifted that fear, making clear to him that it is not of the transformed. 3273 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A nation of the Children of Israel was lost, and it is not known what it did; I fear the mouse may be it - for when it finds sheep milk it drinks it, but when it finds camel milk it does not drink it."
"3752","3274 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn 'Ali narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a mouse and said: Treacherous, and I know nothing treacherous except from the Jews. Abu Ja'far said: In what we narrated in the chapter before this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Allah never destroys a people then makes for them offspring or progeny, is proof that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about the mouse in the two hadiths we narrated in this chapter was from him before Allah informed him that He makes no offspring or progeny for those He destroys. That fear he had was thereby removed; and those who reported this chapter from him did not know what came from him after that of what we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter. It is established thereby, since mice are of those with reproduction and progeny, that they are of the kind whose creation by Allah Almighty preceded His transforming those He transformed of those servants He cursed into what He transformed them - and with Allah Almighty is success."
"3753","Chapter 528 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about lizards: what permits eating them and what forbids it. 3275 - Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-'Absi narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hasanah, he said: We alighted in a land with many lizards, and famine struck us. We cooked some; the pots were boiling when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and said: What is this? We said: Lizards we caught. He said: A nation of the Children of Israel was transformed into beasts on earth, and I fear these may be them; so overturn them."
"3754","3276 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Wahb al-Juhani narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hasanah narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: Thus al-A'mash narrated this hadith; Husayn narrated it differing in its chain."
"3755","3277 - As Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Husayn, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Thabit ibn Yazid al-Ansari, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when people caught lizards, grilled and ate them. I caught a lizard, grilled it, then brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He took a palm stalk and began counting his fingers with it, saying: A nation of the Children of Israel was transformed into beasts on earth, and I do not know - perhaps these are they. I said: People grilled and ate them. He neither ate nor forbade."
"3756","3278 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Husayn, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: Thabit ibn Wadi'ah. Al-Hakam also narrated it, differing from al-A'mash in its chain and differing from Husayn in its chain."
"3757","3279 - As Fahd had narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah narrated to us, from Shu'bah, he said: al-Hakam narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, from Thabit ibn Wadi'ah al-Ansari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he was brought a lizard. He said: A nation transformed."
"3758","3280 - And as Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah had narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, he said: I heard Zayd ibn Wahb, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, from Thabit ibn Wadi'ah, that a man brought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a lizard. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: A nation was lost, Allah knows best. 'Adi ibn Thabit also narrated it from Zayd, differing from them all in its chain."
"3759","3281 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Sa'igh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Adi ibn Thabit, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Thabit ibn Wadi'ah al-Ansari, from a man of Banu Fazarah who brought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lizards he had hunted. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began turning them over, looking at the lizard among them, saying: A nation transformed; I do not know what it did, nor do I know - perhaps these are of them."
"3760","3282 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid and 'Affan narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr narrated to us, from Husayn - a man of Banu Fazarah - he said: Samurah ibn Jundub informed me that an Arab Bedouin came to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was preaching, interrupting his sermon, saying: O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about the lizard? He said: A nation of the Children of Israel was transformed; I do not know which beasts were transformed."
"3761","3283 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Aqil Bashir ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nadrah narrated to us, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that a Bedouin asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: I am in a walled garden full of lizards, and it is our family's food. He was silent. We said to him: Ask again. He asked again, and he was silent. Then we said to him: Ask again. He asked again, and he said: Allah was wrathful with a tribe of the Children of Israel and transformed them into beasts crawling on earth; I do not think them but these. I neither eat them nor forbid them. We mentioned in the chapter in which we mentioned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about apes and pigs what we mentioned therein - that Allah never destroys a people then makes for them offspring or progeny. So in that is proof that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) feared about the lizard was from him before Allah informed him that He makes no offspring or progeny for what He transforms. So in that is proof the lizard is not disliked for what is in these hadiths we mentioned in this chapter. What is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting eating the lizard is later than that; among what is narrated from him permitting eating it:"
"3762","3284 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Wahb and 'Abd al-Samad narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Tawbah al-'Anbari, he said: I heard al-Sha'bi saying: Have you seen so-and-so when he narrates from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I sat with Ibn 'Umar and never heard him narrate from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except that he said: Some companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were eating lizards when a woman from among the Prophet's wives called to them: They are lizards! The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Eat, it is not of my food. In Wahb's hadith: for it is lawful."
"3763","3285 - And what Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus and Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, they were informed from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, may Allah be pleased with him, from Ibn 'Abbas, that Khalid ibn al-Walid entered with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the house of Maymunah. A roasted lizard was brought; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stretched his hand to it. Some women in Maymunah's house said: Tell the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what he wants to eat of it. They said: It is a lizard. He withdrew his hand. I said: Is it unlawful? He said: No, but it was not in my people's land, so I find myself disliking it. So I drew it to me and ate it while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at me, and he did not forbid me."
"3764","3286 - And what Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from al-Shaybani, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, he said: We were invited to a wedding in Medina. Food was brought to us and we ate; then thirteen lizards were brought to us - some eating and some leaving. In the morning I came to Ibn 'Abbas and told him that. Someone with him said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I neither eat it nor forbid it, nor command it nor prohibit it. Ibn 'Abbas said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent only permitting or forbidding. Meat was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he stretched his hand to eat; Maymunah said: O Messenger of Allah, it is lizard meat. He withdrew his hand, then said: This is meat I never ate. Al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, Khalid ibn al-Walid, and a woman with them ate; and Maymunah said: I shall not eat food the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not eat of."
"3765","3287 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: My aunt Umm Hufayd gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dried cheese, clarified butter, and lizards. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate of the cheese and butter but did not eat of the lizards; yet they were eaten at the Prophet's table - had it been unlawful they would not have been eaten at the Prophet's table."
"3766","3288 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Habib al-Mu'allim narrated to us, from 'Ata', from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a platter with lizards. He said: Eat, for I dislike them. So in what we mentioned is proof of the permissibility of eating lizard meat; and all else narrated in this besides that - in what we narrated in this chapter is what suffices of it. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"3767","Chapter 529 - Explanation of the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: When a fly falls in one's food, let him dip it then throw it, for in one wing is cure and in the other disease; it puts forward disease and delays cure. 3289 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la and Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed me, from Sa'id ibn Khalid al-Qarazi, he said: I came to Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman visiting him at Quba'. He served us butter and a lump; a fly fell in the butter, and Abu Salamah began dipping it with his little finger. I said: May Allah forgive you, uncle, what are you doing? He said: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a fly falls in food, dip it, for in one wing is poison and in the other cure; it puts forward poison and delays cure."
"3768","3290 - And Bakkar and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Sa'id ibn Khalid, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a fly falls in food, dip it, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3769","3291 - And al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us, he said: 'Utbah ibn Muslim narrated to me, from 'Ubayd ibn Hunayn, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: When a fly falls in one's drink, let him immerse it all then throw it, for in one wing is poison and in the other cure."
"3770","3292 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Thumamah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Anas narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: And Hammad narrated to us, from Habib ibn al-Shahid, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3771","3293 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, from Muhammad ibn al-Ajlan, that al-Qaqa ibn Hakim informed him, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, and he added: For one guards against the wing in which is the disease, so let him dip it then throw it away."
"3772","3294 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Murajja ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Hisham al-Qurdusi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When a fly falls into the vessel of one of you, let him dip it, for in one of its wings is disease and in the other is cure."
"3773","3295 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from Said, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, raised to the Prophet, he said: When a fly falls into the vessel of one of you, let him submerge it, for in one of its wings is poison and in the other is cure. An objector ignorant of the reports of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: does the fly choose to put forward one wing and hold back the other? Our answer, by Allah's aid: had he read Allah's Book with understanding he would find proof of the truth of this saying, namely His saying about the bee, that He inspired it to take houses from mountains and trees, and His saying about the earth that it will report its news because your Lord inspired it - that inspiration is His causing it to do what He wills; so likewise the fly is inspired to dip the diseased wing and guard with the other, like the ant warning its fellows against Sulayman and his hosts, and like the hoopoe reporting to Sulayman about the woman ruling them - all by Allah's inspiration, without prior speech or choice. So too with the fly. We ask Allah for success."
"3774","Chapter 530 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: Whoever says to his brother: Come, let me gamble with you, let him give charity, and the addition in the hadith of al-Awzai: let him give the gamble in charity. 3296 - Abu Jafar said: We already narrated this hadith in this book through Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala, from Ibn Wahb, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says to his companion: Come, let me gamble with you, let him give charity. Then we found it through al-Awzai, from al-Zuhri, with this chain: let him give the gamble in charity."
"3775","3297 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us as he said: Ali ibn Bahr ibn Barri narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf informed me, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says in his oath: By al-Lat and al-Uzza, let him say: There is no god but Allah, and whoever says to his companion: Come, let me gamble with you, let him give the gamble in charity. But we found this hadith from Dawud ibn Rushayd, from al-Walid, from al-Awzai, with this added phrase attributed to al-Awzai himself."
"3776","3298 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Humayd informed me, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: al-Awzai said: he gives the gamble in charity. Abu Jafar said: We could not determine whether this added phrase in al-Awzai's hadith over Yunus's hadith is from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or from al-Awzai explaining the Prophet's intent in commanding charity, and al-Awzai with his knowledge would not say such an explanation unless he had warrant for it, since the like of it is not said by mere opinion or deduction. We considered the meaning of: let him give the gamble in charity: gambling is unlawful, and what comes to the gambler through it is unlawful for him and must be returned to the one he took it from. When two men intend to gamble each brings out wealth of his own, either to win or to add to. So the charity commanded is charity of what he himself put out to disobey Allah with, redirecting it to charity as nearness to his Lord, as expiation for intending what was forbidden - not charity of what he would win from the other's wealth, which is unlawful and takes the ruling of misappropriated spoils, and Allah does not accept charity from misappropriation, as narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3777","3299 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Zaidah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Musab ibn Sad, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah does not accept prayer without purification, nor charity from misappropriated spoils."
"3778","3300 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Malih ibn Usamah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. An objector said: What is your proof? What you narrated says give the gamble in charity, and the gamble is what returns to him from another's wealth, not what he put out of his own wealth. Our answer, by Allah's aid: Things are named for what is near them even if not identical to it, as in Allah's saying about divorced women reaching their term then being retained or released - meaning nearing the term, since after the actual term they are strangers with no return, as shown by the other verse forbidding preventing them from remarrying their husbands. Likewise the Muslims call the son of Ibrahim, Ismail or Ishaq, the sacrificed one, for his nearness to sacrifice though he was not sacrificed. So too the gamble here means nearness to gambling, not the actual gamble; abundant in Arab speech. So the one who put out wealth to win thereby what is forbidden is commanded to redirect it to charity for Allah as nearness, hoping it is expiation for his attempted disobedience. We ask Allah for success."
"3779","Chapter 531 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying about each of two funeral processions brought past him, one praised with good and the other with evil. 3301 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A funeral passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and they praised it with good, tongues following one another with good, so he said: It has become due. And a funeral passed and evil was said of it, tongues following with evil, so he said: It has become due. Then he said: You are Allah's witnesses on earth."
"3780","3302 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah Musa ibn Ismail al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: A funeral passed and it was praised with good, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It has become due. Then another passed and it was praised with evil, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It has become due."
"3781","3303 - Mubashshir ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Mubashshir al-Basri Abu Bishr narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: They passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a funeral and praised it with good, so he said: It has become due. And they passed by him with another and praised it with evil, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It has become due. Then he said: You praised this one with good, so Paradise became due for him, and you praised this one with evil, so the Fire became due for him, and you are Allah's witnesses on earth."
"3782","3304 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mamar narrated to us - Abu Jafar said: this is Abu Mamar al-Zamin - he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A funeral was brought past the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and it was praised with good, so the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Due, due, due. And a funeral was brought past and it was praised with evil, so he said: Due, due, due. Then Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: May my father and mother be ransom for you, a funeral passed and was praised with good and you said due thrice, and a funeral passed and was praised with evil and you said due thrice. So the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whomever you praise with good, Paradise becomes due for him, and whomever you praise with evil, the Fire becomes due for him, and you are Allah's witnesses on earth."
"3783","3305 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Misar narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Amir ibn Masud, from Amir ibn Sad, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man who had died was mentioned before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he was praised with evil, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Due, due. And a man was mentioned before him and praised with good, so he said: Due, due. Then a man said: Due, due - meaning what do you mean by due? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Some of you are witnesses over others."
"3784","3306 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: I heard Nafi ibn Umar al-Jumahi narrating from Umayyah ibn Safwan, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Zuhayr al-Thaqafi, from his father, that he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying at al-Nabaah or al-Nabawah near Taif: You are about to know the people of Paradise from the people of the Fire, or your best from your worst. Nafi said: I only recall he said the people of Paradise from the people of the Fire. Then a man from the people said: By what, O Messenger of Allah? He said: By good praise and by evil praise; you are witnesses, some of you over others."
"3785","3307 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Nafi ibn Umar informed us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. We considered these reports and found in some of them from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): whomever you praise with good, Paradise becomes due for him, and whomever you praise with evil, the Fire becomes due for him. Its outward meaning suggested Paradise or the Fire becoming obligatory by that praise alone, so we sought the best understanding of what was intended by this saying and its place among these reports."
"3786","3308 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi and Shayban ibn Farrukh together narrated to us, they said: Dawud ibn Abi al-Furat narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Buraydah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad al-Duali, he said: I came to Madinah when sickness had fallen upon it and people were dying swiftly, and I sat with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. A funeral passed and its owner was praised with good, so Umar said: Due. Then another passed and its owner was praised with evil, so Umar said: Due. Then a third passed and its owner was praised with evil, so Umar said: Due. Abu al-Aswad said: Why did you say due, O Commander of the Believers? He said: I said as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any Muslim whom four testify for with good, Allah admits him into Paradise. We said: And three? He said: And three. We said: And two? He said: And two. Then we did not ask him about one. Abu Jafar said: Its meaning in our view - and Allah knows best - is that testimony of good for one testified for is a covering from Allah upon him in this world, and whomever Allah covers in this world He does not lift His covering from him in the Hereafter, as narrated from him in what we already cited in this book: Three I testify for, and a fourth, had I testified I would hope not to sin, then he mentioned the three, then said: And the fourth: Allah does not cover a servant in this world except He covers him in the Hereafter. So that becoming due is covering in this world by good praise, and in the Hereafter by covering therein from what is feared, namely the Fire. And praise with blame in this world is lifting the cover from the one so praised, the opposite of the one praised with good, and so in the Hereafter he is the opposite of the one praised with good in this world, thus deserving the Fire. This deduction of Umar from the Prophet's saying Due, with what he said alongside it, is among the finest and best deductions. We ask Allah for success."
"3787","Chapter 532 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the reason for which was revealed: Had it not been for a prior decree from Allah, a great punishment would have touched you for what you took. 3309 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Abu Zumayl Simak al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abbas said: When they took captives, meaning on the day of Badr, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Bakr and Umar, what do you think about these captives? Abu Bakr said: O Messenger of Allah, they are sons of uncles and kin; I think you take ransom from them as strength for us against the disbelievers, and perhaps Allah will guide them to Islam. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What do you think, O son of al-Khattab? Umar said: By Allah, I do not see what Abu Bakr sees, O Prophet of Allah; rather I think you give us power over them so we strike their necks, give Ali power over Aqil so he strikes his neck, and give me power over so-and-so, a relative of Umar, so I strike his neck, for these are the leaders of disbelief and its chiefs. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) inclined to what Abu Bakr said and not to what I said. Next morning I came and found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr sitting weeping. I said: O Messenger of Allah, tell me what you and your companion weep for; if I find weeping I will weep with you. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I weep for what your companions proposed to me of ransom; your punishment was shown to me nearer than this tree, a tree near the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then Allah revealed: It is not for a prophet to have captives until he has thoroughly subdued in the land; you desire the goods of this world while Allah desires the Hereafter, and Allah is Mighty, Wise; had it not been for a prior decree from Allah, a great punishment would have touched you for what you took; so eat of what you have taken as lawful and good. So Allah made spoils lawful for them. An objector said: In what you narrated from Ibn Abbas there is no mention that they took anything, only Abu Bakr's counsel to take ransom. Our answer: This hadith is as he says, except that Abu Hurayrah differed from Ibn Abbas, reporting that the Muslims had already taken some spoils before Allah revealed this verse."
"3788","3310 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us as he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: On the day of Badr the Muslims hastened and took of the spoils, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Spoils were not lawful for any dark-headed people before you; when a prophet before you and his companions took spoils, they gathered them and fire descended from heaven and consumed them. Then Allah revealed: Had it not been for a prior decree from Allah, a great punishment would have touched you for what you took; so eat of what you have taken as lawful and good."
"3789","3311 - al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us as he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"3790","3312 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us as he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Amash, from Dhakwan, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Spoils were not lawful for any black-headed one before us; fire would descend and consume the spoils. Then was revealed: Had it not been for a prior decree from Allah. He said: It preceded in the prior decree. This hadith shows the threat in this verse was for their taking spoils before they were made lawful, not for other than that mentioned in Ibn Abbas's hadith, and this is likelier with the verse, for it says: would have touched you for what you took, affirming a prior taking upon which the threat fell. In this is a lesson for people of knowledge against hastening before Allah's command, for this happened to the people of Badr about whom the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who tells you that Allah looked upon the people of Badr and said: Do what you will, I have forgiven you - yet the threat could reach them, so how much more those below their rank. As for what scholars said about the prior decree, they differed; it is narrated from Abd Allah ibn Abbas."
"3791","What Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman together narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Abi Talhah narrated to me, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas about: Had it not been for a prior decree from Allah, a great punishment would have touched you for what you took. He said: Mercy from Allah had preceded for them before they committed the disobedience. Abu Jafar said: This is one view said about it, and another view has been said, namely."
"3792","What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, they said: Uthman ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from al-Hasan about His saying: Had it not been for a prior decree from Allah. He said: Allah was going to feed this ummah spoils, but they took ransom from the people on the day of Badr before being commanded to, so Allah turned to them in forgiveness, blamed them for it, then made it lawful for them and made it spoils."
"3793","Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, from Awf, from al-Hasan about His saying: Had it not been for a prior decree from Allah, he said: Allah was going to feed this ummah spoils, but they did what they did before spoils became lawful for them."
"3794","Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated to me, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid about this verse, he said: It preceded that He made spoils lawful for this ummah. He said: And al-Hasan said: It preceded from Allah that He does not punish a people except after prior warning to them, and there had been no prior warning to them about it. And another view has been said."
"3795","Namely what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ashath, from al-Hasan about: Had it not been for a prior decree from Allah. He said: Forgiveness for the people of Badr. All these interpretations are possible for what was interpreted upon them of what we mentioned, and Allah knows best His intent. And with Allah is success."
"3796","Chapter 533 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his forbidding wearing a ring except for one with authority. 3313 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Mufaddal ibn Fadalah informed us, he said: Ayyash ibn Abbas narrated to us, from al-Haytham ibn Shafi al-Hajari, from Abu Amir, from Abu Rayhanah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade wearing a ring except for one with authority. We already mentioned this hadith earlier in this book with chains including this chain and others. We considered them to know what was intended, and found rings were not among Arab dress nor what they used. What proves that is what was narrated from Anas ibn Malik about it."
"3797","3314 - From what Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to write to Khosrow and Caesar, so it was said to him: They do not accept your letter except with a seal. So he took a silver ring engraved: Muhammad, Messenger of Allah."
"3798","3315 - And from what Ali narrated to us, he said: Shabab ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to write to the Romans, then he mentioned the like of it. This hadith shows he only took it when he needed it, to seal the letter he wrote to the non-Arabs mentioned, since they only recognized incoming and outgoing letters if sealed. And his saying in Abu Rayhanah's hadith: except for one with authority, for the ruler's need to seal his outgoing letters, proves that whoever needs to correspond with people is likewise permitted it. And all people need that for sealing their wealth and guarding their trusts and the like. So that proves its permissibility for all people. That is further proved by."
"3799","3316 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar - Abu Jafar said: Abu Bishr is Jafar ibn Abi Wahshiyyah."
"3800","3317 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, he said: Nafi narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a gold ring with its stone toward his palm, so the people took it too, then he threw it away and took a ring of silver. In that is proof that people used to do as he did in imitation of him, and proof of the permissibility of taking rings for all people. We ask Allah for success."
"3801","Chapter 534 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) showing that a man in his speech should not break it except at a point where breaking is proper, nor shift its meaning from what he spoke for. 3318 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Tamim ibn Tarafah, from Adi ibn Hatim, he said: Two men came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and one of them pronounced the testimony, saying: Whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger is guided, and whoever disobeys them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What an evil preacher you are, stand up. Abu Jafar said: The meaning in our view - and Allah knows best - is that it reverts to the sense of inversion, as if: whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger, and whoever disobeys them, is guided - and that is disbelief. He should have said: and whoever disobeys them has strayed, or stopped at: is guided, then begun anew: and whoever disobeys them has strayed. Otherwise it reverts to the inversion mentioned, like Allah's saying: When Ibrahim raised the foundations of the House, and Ismail - meaning Ibrahim and Ismail raised the foundations - and like His saying about women who despair of menstruation and those who have not menstruated, with the waiting period of three months applying to both groups together. Since this is disliked in sermons and ordinary speech, it is more strongly disliked in Allah's Book, and the Prophet's prohibition of such speech is more emphatic. We ask Allah for success."
"3802","Chapter 535 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of speech that some claimed is poetry and others denied is so. 3319 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, and Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from al-Miqdam ibn Shurayh, from his father, he said: I said to Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) quote anything of poetry? She said: Yes, from the poetry of Ibn Rawahah, and he would sometimes say this verse - followed by the verse in the narration."
"3803","3320 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hujr informed us, he said: Sharik informed us, from al-Miqdam ibn Shurayh, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: it was said to her: Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) quote anything of poetry? She said: He used to quote the poetry of Ibn Rawahah - followed by the verse in the narration."
"3804","3321 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Awn al-Makhzumi narrated to us, he said: al-Ajlah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Aishah married a female relative of hers to a man of the Ansar. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and said: Have you escorted the girl? They said: Yes. He said: Did you send with her one to sing? She said: No. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Ansar are a people with romantic inclination, so why did you not send with her one to say - followed by the verse in the narration."
"3805","3322 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq informed us that a man of Banu Qays said to al-Bara while he listened: Did you flee from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Hunayn? Al-Bara said: But the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not flee. Hawazin were archers; when we charged the enemy they fled, then the enemy turned to fighting. I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a white mule, with Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith holding its bridle, saying - followed by the verse in the narration."
"3806","3323 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah informed us, from Abu Ishaq, he said: A man said to al-Bara: O Abu Umarah, did you turn back on the day of Hunayn? He said: No, by Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not turn back. But we met archers whose arrows hardly fell amiss, the host of Hawazin, and they showered us with arrows hardly missing. They advanced there toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on his white mule with Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib leading him. Then he dismounted, sought Allah's help, and said - followed by the verse. He said: Then he drew them up in ranks."
"3807","3324 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas, he said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out on a cold morning while the Muhajirun and Ansar dug the trench with their hands, and he said - followed by the verse - and they answered him - with the verse about struggle."
"3808","3325 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Bara, that he narrated to them that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say - followed by the verse in the narration."
"3809","3326 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Shabab ibn Sawwar narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq, from his father, he said: I heard al-Bara ibn Azib saying: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) carrying dust on the day of Uhud until the dust covered the hair of his chest, chanting the words of Abd Allah ibn Rawahah, saying - followed by the verse. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would prolong his voice with it."
"3810","3327 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Bara, the like of Abu Ishaq's hadith from al-Bara, except that he said: He said it repeatedly."
"3811","3328 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the Trench saying - then he mentioned the like of it - except that he did not say: prolonging his voice with it, nor that he said it repeatedly."
"3812","3329 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The truest word spoken by a poet is the word of Labid - followed by the verse in the narration - and Ibn Abi al-Salt almost became Muslim."
"3813","3330 - Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays: he heard Jundub saying: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a raid and his finger was injured, so he said - followed by the verse about the path."
"3814","3331 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Jundub ibn Abd Allah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was walking when a stone struck his finger, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. Abu Jafar said: An objector rejected all these reports, denying that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said any of what is mentioned in them, citing Allah's saying: We did not teach him poetry, nor is it fitting for him. Our proof against him, by Allah's aid: what he cited does not refute any of what we narrated, for it is Allah informing His creation that He did not teach His Prophet poetry, in reply to the idolaters saying of him: Nay, he forged it; nay, he is a poet. Allah informed creation he is unlike what they said, then followed with: nor is it fitting for him - given the rank He bestowed with prophethood, a rank given to no other creature. Those of His creation taught poetry are known: they compose intentionally, praising some, satirizing others, describing what their hearts incline to - while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is unlike that. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) himself repudiated what they attributed to him."
"3815","3332 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn al-Mufaddal al-Hafari narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Adi ibn Thabit, from al-Bara ibn Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, so-and-so son of so-and-so satirized me, knowing I am not a poet to satirize him back, so curse him to the number of his satires of me, or in place of his satires of me. Abu Jafar said: Then Allah made clear upon their tongues that what they heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not as they claimed - that he is a poet speaking poetry as poets do - for they set it to poetry and it would not cohere on anyone's tongue as poetry."
"3816","3333 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us. 3334 - And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah informed us, he said: Humayd ibn Hilal al-Adawi informed us, from Abd Allah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, he said: My brother Unays said to me: I am going to Makkah, look after me till I return. He went and tarried. I said: What kept you? He said: I met at Makkah a man of your religion claiming Allah sent him. I said: What do people say of him? He said: They say poet, and they say soothsayer. I have heard the soothsayers' speech and his is not theirs, and I set his words to poetic measures and it does not cohere on anyone's tongue as poetry. Abu Dharr said: O son of my brother - Unays being one of the poets - by Allah he is truthful and they are liars. Abu Jafar said: In poetry there are wisdoms, including the Prophet's saying: Some poetry is wisdom - which we shall mention later in this book where it fits better, God willing. So what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke of what is narrated in these reports was of the wisdoms contained in poetry; he spoke it as wisdom, Allah causing wisdom to flow upon his tongue - not as poetry devoid of wisdom. Proof: he brought only what he needed of that kind and nothing else. A man may utter measured speech which he or another could build into poetry, yet it is not poetry nor is its speaker a poet - just as one unschooled in crafts of the tongue like jurisprudence may quote something as jurists quote it without thereby becoming a jurist. Likewise quoting a verse or less in poetic meter does not make one a poet. Al-Khalil ibn Ahmad - whose standing in Arabic, especially poetry, meter and prosody, is known - held that rajaz verses are not poetry but speech in poetic meter, rhymed yet not poetry. In what we mentioned is exposed the ignorance of this objector and his denial of what is not denied to the Prophet; for it does not contradict the verse he cited, and what he spoke in the reports was wisdom therein or something of poetry that stuck to his tongue which he uttered - without being a poet nor entering the sense Allah negated of him. We ask Allah for success."
"3817","Chapter 536 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what he did when Allah forbade wine, in commanding one who asked about turning it into vinegar - forbidding it and not permitting him. 3335 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah Musa ibn Masud narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Suddi, from Abu Hubayrah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with an orphan in his care, having wine at the time wine was forbidden, and said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we make it vinegar? He said: No. So he poured it into the valley till it flowed."
"3818","3336 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: al-Suddi narrated to me, from Abu Hubayrah, from Anas, that a man said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I have wine. He said: Pour it out. He said: Shall I make it vinegar? He said: No."
"3819","3337 - Yahya ibn Ismail al-Baghdadi Abu Zakariyya narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Suddi, from Abu Hubayrah, from Anas ibn Malik, that Abu Talhah asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about orphans who inherited wine. He said: Pour it out. He said: Shall I not make it vinegar? He said: No."
"3820","3338 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Israil, from al-Suddi, from Abu Hubayrah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"3821","3339 - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Hammad al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Ismail - meaning al-Suddi - narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abbad, from Anas, from Abu Talhah, that he had wealth of orphans and bought wine with it. When wine was forbidden, he said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I make it vinegar? He said: No."
"3822","3340 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Humayd, son-in-law of Ubayd Allah ibn Musa, narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, from Mujalid, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Said, he said: I had wealth of orphans, and when the prohibition of wine came down, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to pour it out."
"3823","3341 - Yahya ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Scholars differed about a man with juice that becomes wine who wants to treat it till it becomes vinegar: some forbade it, citing these reports as proof - among them Malik and al-Shafii - except that Malik permitted treating the dregs of wine till they become vinegar."
"3824","As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: I heard Malik saying about a man who puts juice upon dregs to become vinegar, he said: No harm in that if he intends it for vinegar. In Malik's permitting treatment of dregs - and dregs only come from wine - till they return to vinegar, likewise. And among what those who held the view we mentioned, that treating wine till it returns to vinegar is disliked, cite as proof."
"3825","What Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Nufayli narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Hasan, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, that a merchant bought wine and he commanded him to pour it into the Dijlah. They said to him: Why not command him to make it vinegar? And he forbade that. This may mean Uthman only forbade that because the wine asked about was not from juice he owned that became wine, but from juice he purchased by an unlawful purchase, so he did not own it thereby and he did not command treating it, since he did not own its origin. The holders of this view also narrated for their view."
"3826","What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from al-Zuhri, from al-Qasim, from Aslam, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Do not eat of wine that was spoiled until Allah has initiated its spoiling."
"3827","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aslam, freedman of Umar, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was brought tila at al-Jabiyah, then being cooked, like thickened rubb. He said: In this drink is what it has reached, and no vinegar from spoiled wine is drunk until Allah initiates its spoiling; then vinegar is good, and no harm on one who buys vinegar found with the People of the Book so long as he does not know they deliberately spoiled it after it became wine. Abu Jafar said: Part of the opponents' proof is that in this hadith, that no wine deliberately spoiled is drunk until Allah initiates its spoiling, is not from Umar's speech but from al-Zuhri's speech, joined to Umar's speech when brought the tila in which he said: In this drink is what it has reached. Proof is what Musa ibn Uqbah said to him: Separate the Prophet's speech from your own - since he would narrate the Prophet's hadiths mixing them with his own words. Also proved by the narration of other than Ibn Abi Dhib of this hadith from him, namely Yunus ibn Yazid."
"3828","As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, that he used to say: No good in vinegar from wine deliberately spoiled until Allah spoils it; then vinegar is good, and no harm on one buying vinegar found with the People of the Book so long as he does not know it was wine and they deliberately spoiled it with water - if it was wine and they deliberately spoiled it to become vinegar, no good in eating that. Abu Jafar said: Clear by this hadith that what was attributed in Ibn Abi Dhib's hadith to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, is only his statement about the drink brought to him - In this drink is what it has reached - specifically, and the rest therein is from Ibn Shihab's speech, not another's. Then those who forbade that said to those who permitted it - among the permitters many Kufans, including Abu Hanifah and his companions: Did any Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) precede you in this view as an imam for what you said? Part of their proof was."
"3829","What Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Amr informed us, from Busr ibn Ubayd Allah, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, that Abu al-Darda used to eat murri into which wine was put, saying: The sun and salt slaughtered it. Then they said to them: What is the meaning of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding the pouring out of the orphans' wine and forbidding making it vinegar - if nothing else is allowed for orphans, then for others it is likelier not allowed. Their answer was that wine is not property of orphans after Allah forbade it; it was theirs before that, then ceased to be property for them. So though orphans, they are in that like adults. And there was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the prohibition of wine descended."
"3830","3342 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh, Ibn Lahiah and al-Layth ibn Sad informed me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Thabit ibn Yazid al-Khawlani who informed him, he said: I met Abd Allah ibn Umar and asked him about the price of wine. He said: I will tell you about wine. I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque; while he was sitting with knees drawn up, he loosened his sitting, then said: Whoever has any of this wine, let him inform me of it. People began coming to him, one saying: I have a rawiyah, another: I have a rawiyah, another: I have a skin, or whatever Allah willed he had. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gather at such-and-such a stony ground, then inform me. They did, then informed him. He stood and I stood with him; I walked at his right while he leaned on me. Then Abu Bakr caught up, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) moved me back and put Abu Bakr in my place. Then Umar ibn al-Khattab caught up and he moved me back and put him at his left. He walked between them till he stood over the wine and said to the people: Do you know this? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, this is wine. He said: You spoke truth. Then he said: Allah has cursed wine, its presser and the one for whom it is pressed, its drinker and pourer, its carrier and the one to whom it is carried, its seller and buyer, and the eater of its price. Then he called for a knife and said: Sharpen it. They did, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it tearing the skins with it. People said: In these skins is benefit. He said: Yes, but I do this in anger for Allah for what is therein of His wrath. Then Umar said: I will suffice you. He said: No. And some narrators add over others in the story."
"3831","3343 - And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to me that Abu Tumah narrated to him that he heard Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab narrating this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3832","3343M - And what al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Azdi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Talq ibn al-Samh al-Lakhmi narrated to us, he said: Abu Shurayh Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Sharahil ibn Bukayl, from Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the prohibition of wine descended. He ordered the vessels of wine gathered in one place. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came in the morning holding my left hand with his right, then Umar ibn al-Khattab came and moved me from his left, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took my right hand with his left and took Umar with his right hand his left. We walked with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) between us. Then Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, came, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) released my hand, moved Umar from his left, and took Abu Bakr's hand with his right his left. We walked till we came to the vessels gathered, containing wine and skins, and he said: Bring me a blade or knife. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bared his forearms and took the blade. Abu Bakr and Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, we will suffice you. He said: Tear them for what is therein of Allah's anger: Wine is unlawful; Allah has cursed its drinker, seller and buyer, carrier and the one to whom it is carried, presser and the one for whom it is pressed, the keeper over it, and the eater of its price. This hadith shows the Prophet tore the skins, which are not wine at all, in anger for Allah at the delay of those who kept it after Allah's prohibition. He punished them by tearing their skins, for they should have hastened to destroy what Allah forbade so none could benefit by it as before its prohibition. They were not like the elders of the Ansar such as Ubayy, Abu Talhah and Suhayl ibn Bayda, who when drinking with Anas ibn Malik pouring for them, a man passed and said: Do you not know wine has been forbidden? They said: Pour out what is in your vessel, O Anas. Anas said: They never returned to it till they met Allah, may Allah be pleased with them. Those who lagged behind their deed were punished by tearing their skins and destroying them, denied benefit thereof. This in our view was in the era when punishments for sins fell upon wealth, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about the withholder of zakat: We shall take it and half his wealth as a due of Allah's dues, and as he said about the thief of unguarded produce on the mountain: upon them double compensation and exemplary lashes, and as he said after forbidding hunting in Madinah: whomever you find hunting therein, take his spoil. More than one Companion, Umar ibn al-Khattab and Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, held that ruling remained after the Prophet, including what is narrated from Umar."
"3833","As Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, from his brother, from Sulayman, that is Ibn Bilal, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he would go out in the morning inspecting the markets; when he saw milk he ordered the skins opened, and if he found any adulterated with water he poured it out. We know adulterated milk still has benefit its owners could use, but Umar only poured it out fearing its people would cheat people with it. Likewise the Prophet's forbidding one who asked to make wine into vinegar may have been for the like: fear he would be alone with it and commit what Allah forbade thereof, so he ordered it poured out. Strengthening this interpretation is what he did with the skins he tore, though he saw other skins containing wine and did not tear them, since their owners had not done what the owners of those had done."
"3834","3344 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me - from Malik ibn Anas and others - from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Walah al-Sabai of Egypt, that he asked Ibn Abbas about what is pressed from grapes. Ibn Abbas said: A man gifted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a rawiyah of wine, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Did you not know Allah has forbidden it? He said: No. Then he whispered to a man beside him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What did you whisper to him? He said: I commanded him - or said: I commanded him to sell it. He said: The One who forbade drinking it forbade selling it. He said: So he opened the two waterskins till what was in them was gone."
"3835","3345 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal informed me, from Yahya ibn Said, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Walah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Do you not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not tear the rawiyah containing wine as he tore the skins in Ibn Umar's hadith - proving the tearing was anger at those who concealed it after its prohibition in Ibn Umar's hadith. So it is also possible that the one who concealed it among those who asked about turning it into vinegar was refused as punishment, not because had it been turned to vinegar it would remain unlawful. If one asks what analogy requires in this scholarly disagreement, it is said: Analogy requires permissibility, for we see lawful juice becoming wine by itself or by others' treatment is the same, unlawful for the quality arising in it regardless of origin. Likewise wine turning to vinegar, whether by itself or by human action, is one thing: the arising quality of vinegar gives it vinegar's ruling, returning it to lawfulness and removing wine's ruling of prohibition. Likewise tanning carrion: treating it while unlawful till it returns lawful is like its returning lawful if left to dry in sun and wind, removing carrion's foulness and restoring it to the ruling of hides from slaughtered kinds. We ask Allah for success."
"3836","Chapter 537 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his concession for the pilgrim in ihram to bandage his eyes with aloes when they ail him. 3346 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, from Aban ibn Uthman, who narrated to him, from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted - or said: When the muhrim complains of his eyes, he may bandage them with aloes. We considered this hadith to know what the concession is. We found bandaging is covering what is bandaged, and aloes in itself is not perfume, so we understood the concession was not for the aloes itself but for the bandage covering, i.e. covering the muhrim's face or part of it; otherwise it would be called ointment not bandage. An objector said: How can that be when something narrated from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, refutes it?"
"3837","He mentioned what Yunus and Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from Abd Allah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, he said: I saw Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, at al-Arj covering his face with a purple velvet cloth while in ihram."
"3838","And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Said, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: al-Furafisah ibn Umayr al-Hanafi informed me that he saw Uthman at al-Arj, then he mentioned the like of it. He said: This hadith proves Uthman saw no harm in covering the face in ihram, so the concession in the first hadith was not as you said. Our answer, by Allah's aid: Uthman may have done that from necessity calling him to it, with expiation alongside, as narrated from Abd Allah ibn Abbas in the like."
"3839","From what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu Mabad, freedman of Ibn Abbas, that Ibn Abbas said to him: O Abu Mabad, return my taylasan to me, while in ihram. He said: I said: You used to forbid this! He said: I intend to ransom it. So Uthman, had he been asked about what he did, might have said he did it to ransom. In what we mentioned is clear that covering the face in ihram is forbidden for the muhrim. This view is also narrated from Abd Allah ibn Umar."
"3840","As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, and as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: What is above the chin is of the head, so the muhrim shall not cover it. So Abd Allah ibn Umar also held this view, and analogy requires it: for the woman has wider dispensation in ihram than the man, wearing the shirt and covering her head in ihram, while the man neither covers his head nor wears the shirt therein. Since the woman despite her wider dispensation does not cover her face therein, the man is likelier. So said Abu Hanifah and Malik ibn Anas. We ask Allah for success."
"3841","Chapter 538 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the rulers after him who in their rule are successors of prophethood - who are they? 3347 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Harb al-Khawlani al-Abrash narrated to us, he said: al-Zubaydi narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, from Amr ibn Aban ibn Uthman, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that he used to narrate that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Tonight a righteous man was shown that Abu Bakr was attached to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Umar was attached to Abu Bakr, and Uthman was attached to Umar. When we rose from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we said: As for the righteous man, it is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and as for the attaching of some to others, they are the rulers of this affair with which Allah sent His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Jafar said: In this hadith the rulers of the affair with which Allah sent His Prophet after him are these three mentioned. They may be his rulers after the Prophet, with other rulers after them besides them. So we examined that."
"3842","3348 - We found Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) liked dreams and would ask about them. One day he said: Which of you saw a dream? A man said: I, O Messenger of Allah; I saw as if a scale was let down from heaven, and you and Abu Bakr were weighed in it and you outweighed Abu Bakr. Then Abu Bakr and Umar were weighed in it and Abu Bakr outweighed Umar. And Umar and Uthman were weighed in it and Umar outweighed Uthman. Then the scale was lifted. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was distressed by it and said: Caliphate of prophethood, then Allah gives kingship to whom He wills. Then we considered whether other than this hadith is narrated about it, since in this hadith is the lifting of the scale by which those weighed, whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said are the rulers of that affair after him."
"3843","3349 - We found Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Said ibn Jumhan, from Abu Abd al-Rahman Safinah, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The caliphate is thirty years, then there will be kingship. Then Safinah said: Abu Bakr held two years, Umar ten years, Uthman twelve years, and Ali six years, may Allah be pleased with them. This hadith shows the years of prophetic caliphate are these thirty years comprising the durations of Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them. That the first two hadiths mention Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman as mentioned without Ali alongside them is only because what is in them concerns those three specifically - just as other reports mention Abu Bakr without Umar, Umar without Abu Bakr or Uthman, and Uthman without Abu Bakr or Umar. Likewise Ali in this sense has reports mentioning him without Abu Bakr, Umar or Uthman - for they, may Allah be pleased with them, are people of precedence and virtues, differing and excelling therein like Allah's prophets in their prophethood uniting them, though Allah said: And We favored some prophets over others. Safinah's hadith confined prophetic caliphate to a duration by which we understand it has its people till its end, namely these four, may Allah be pleased with them. We ask Allah for success."
"3844","Chapter 539 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the time when abandoning commanding right and forbidding wrong is allowed. 3350 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi and Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us, they said: al-Hakam ibn Musa al-Nasai Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Hafs, that is Ibn Ghaylan Abu Mabad, from Makhul, from Anas, he said: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, when is commanding right and forbidding wrong abandoned? He said: When there appears among you what appeared among the Children of Israel. It was said: What is that, O Messenger of Allah? He said: When flattery appears among your best, lewdness among your worst, rule passes to your young, and jurisprudence to your lowly. Abu Jafar said: We considered this hadith, first seeking the Prophet's intent in: when there appears among us what appeared among the Children of Israel - what had appeared among them? In our view - and Allah knows best - it is in the hadith we already narrated in this book from Ibn Masud and Abu Musa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that when one of the Children of Israel saw his fellow's sin he forbade him by way of reproof, but next day sat, ate and drank with him as if he had not seen him in sin yesterday. When Allah saw that of them, He struck some of their hearts upon others, then cursed them upon the tongue of their prophet Dawud and Isa son of Maryam, peace be upon them, for their disobedience and transgression. By Him in Whose Hand is Muhammad's soul, you shall command right and forbid wrong, seize the fool's tongue and bend him to truth, or Allah will strike some of your hearts upon others and curse you as He cursed them. Clear thereby that the time when a people are cursed - we seek refuge in Allah from such time - is when there is no meaning in their commanding right or forbidding wrong. Next we sought the flattery mentioned: flattery in Arab speech is softness toward one undeserving it, as al-Farra said, including Allah's saying: They wish you would soften so they would soften - i.e. you are lenient to them and they to you. Likewise the flattery of the wicked by the good here is softness to them, though what is obligatory upon them is its opposite as in the two hadiths of Ibn Masud and Abu Musa. Next we sought his intent in rule passing to the young: in our view - and Allah knows best - rule whose holders manage Islam's affairs - Friday and congregational prayers, struggle against the enemy, and all matters entrusted to imams to whom the عامة refer and follow - whose fulfillment is found in elders and absent in the young. Next we sought his saying: jurisprudence among your lowly - in our view - and Allah knows best - the jurisprudence he meant is that in Abu Hurayrah's narration from him."
"3845","3351 - As al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You find people are mines; the best of them in pre-Islamic ignorance are the best of them in Islam when they understand religion."
"3846","3352 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Amr al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Zaidah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, he said: Asim narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. And as Jabir ibn Abd Allah narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in agreement therewith."
"3847","3353 - As Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned it the like of it. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed us that the best of people in ignorance are the best in Islam when they understand religion. Their best in ignorance are those of noble lineage; when they understand Islam they are the best of its people. We understand thereby that if they do not understand Islam they are not so, and that others who understand, lacking their lineage, rise thereby and join those who held fast to it - an elevation to a high degree and lofty rank. They have therein virtue over the others, for what the others were honored with was not by their earning but a favor from Allah upon them, while what these others attained was by their earning, seeking and share therein. The like of this is unmistakable to its hearer. We ask Allah for success."
"3848","Chapter 540 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the due for destroying things neither weighed nor measured - what is due upon their destroyer in their place. 3354 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Abu al-Mutawakkil, from Umm Salamah, that she brought food in her bowl to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions. Then Aishah came wrapped in a cloak with a stone and split the bowl. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joined the two halves of the bowl and said: Eat; your mother is jealous - twice. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took Aishah's bowl and sent it to Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, and gave Umm Salamah's bowl to Aishah."
"3849","3355 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with one of his wives when one of the Mothers of the Believers sent a bowl of food. She struck the servant's hand and the bowl fell and split. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joined the two pieces, gathered the food therein, saying: Your mother is jealous, your mother is jealous. He said to the people: Eat. He detained the messenger till the other brought her bowl; then he gave the sound bowl to the messenger of the one whose bowl broke, and left the broken one for the one who broke it."
"3850","3356 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abd Allah informed us, from Qays ibn Wahb, from a man of Banu Suwah, he said: We said to Aishah: Narrate to us about the character of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She said: Do you not read the Quran? We said: Upon that, narrate to us about his character. She said: His companions were with him; Hafsah made food for him and I made food for him, but Hafsah beat me to him, sending a bowl with her slavegirl. I said to my slavegirl: If you catch her before she lowers it, throw it. She caught her as she lowered it and threw it; it fell upon the leather mat, the bowl broke and the food scattered. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gathered the food and they ate. Then my slavegirl set down the bowl of food, and he said to Hafsah's slavegirl: Take this food, eat, and take the platter in place of your vessel. She said: I saw no anger in his face, nor did he reproach me. An objector said: How could you abandon what is in these acceptable reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and oppose them to their contraries? Our answer, by Allah's aid: Had he pondered them, he would find us neither opposing nor averse to them. The two wives, one of whom did to the other's bowl what she did, were both wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), each in one of his houses under his maintenance. Both bowls mentioned belonged to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he moved the sound bowl of the wife who broke her companion's bowl to the house of the one whose bowl was broken, and moved the broken bowl to the house of the breaker - nothing of what this objector imagined. Proof of our view which he denied, counting us مخالف therein, is what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon which all scholars agree and state: about a slave owned by two men, when one frees him being wealthy, thereby destroying his partner's share, he owes his partner the value of his share, not half a like slave - which we shall mention with its narration later in this book, God willing. Their agreement thereon, alongside obliging likes, not values, for destroying measured and weighed likes, proves the due for destroying things without likes by measure or weight is their values, not their likes. He said: Yet you imposed a hundred camels upon camel-owners for accidental killing, and a slave or maidservant as compensation for a fetus cast dead from its mother's womb - proving animals are due for destroyed things. Our answer, by Allah's aid: What he cites is beside our point; for the soul for which a hundred camels are set - camels are not its likes - and the dead-cast fetus for which the Prophet set compensation - it is not its like. Rather that is worship Allah bound us with, which we observe without opposing. He said: Yet you narrated the Prophet permitted borrowing animals. Our answer, by Allah's aid: What is narrated thereof is as narrated, and in our view - and Allah knows best - it was before the prohibition of usury, before forbidding returning things to their measures with neither increase nor decrease. Proof: what is narrated of the Prophet borrowing animals is only of a camel he borrowed. Those who adopt that hadith, acting by it without deeming it abrogated, permitted it for borrowing male animals - showing the specific lifted and the ruling applied to what the Prophet applied it to and to all animals. Analogy was decisive and its use obligatory in matters without textual stipulation. Those who permitted what we mentioned forbade borrowing slavegirls, disallowing it; a borrowed slavegirl would exit her lender's ownership to her borrower's, as by sale from seller to buyer. So that showed prohibition falling upon borrowing a slavegirl falls upon borrowing all animals, and that were borrowing animals unrestricted, the borrower of a slavegirl would be permitted intercourse with her and returning her to her lender - unlike buying a slavegirl whose buyer may have intercourse and rescind to her seller. This speaker said: Yet you permitted animals as obligation in one sense, making them debt - as in marriage for a medium slavegirl, which you allow - so you must allow sale for a medium slavegirl for a house. Our answer, by Allah's aid: We permitted what we permitted and forbade what we forbade following what we found Muslims upon: they ruled compensation for a free woman's fetus and differently for a slavegirl's fetus - some: half a tenth of her mother's value if cast dead, among them Malik and al-Shafii; some: what diminished her mother, as with an animal's fetus cast dead by striking her belly - narrated from Abu Yusuf; others: if female, a tenth of her value had she been cast alive then died, if male, half a tenth - held by Abu Hanifah and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, well-known from Abu Yusuf. Since they set compensation for a free woman's fetus, which is not property, and value for a slavegirl's fetus, which is property, we understood what is property admits no use of animals therein, while what is not property admits it - proving marriage for animals lawful but purchase with animals as liabilities unlawful. We ask Allah for success."
"3851","Chapter 541 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about growing hair on heads, of jumam, and parting it and letting it hang. 3357 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Abd Allah ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to let his hair hang down. The idolaters used to part their heads, and the People of the Book used to let their heads hang. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) loved agreeing with the People of the Book in what he had not been commanded about. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) parted his head."
"3852","3358 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to let his hair hang down. The idolaters used to part their heads, and the People of the Book used to let their hair hang. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) parted his head."
"3853","3359 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi and Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, they said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The hair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was below the jummah and above the wafrah. So in Yahya ibn Salih's hadith; and in Yusuf's hadith, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had hair below the earlobe."
"3854","3360 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash ibn al-Walid al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yazid al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you has hair, let him honor it."
"3855","3361 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me that he heard Qatadah saying: I said to Anas: How was the hair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: It was wavy hair, neither curly nor straight, between his ear and his shoulder."
"3856","3362 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Dawud al-Marwazi al-Sharani narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Khalid al-Khayyat, from Malik ibn Anas, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) let his forelock hang, then parted."
"3857","3363 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Anas, he said: The hair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would reach his shoulders."
"3858","3364 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abu Ishaq: he heard al-Bara saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had hair to his earlobes."
"3859","3365 - Muhammad ibn al-Ward al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Amr al-Dabbi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has hair, let him honor it."
"3860","3366 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Iyad ibn Laqit narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Rimthah, he said: I set out with my father toward the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and there we were with him; he had a wafrah with traces of henna. An objector said: In what you narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his growing hair as you narrated, and his commanding people to honor hair, how could you abandon acting by it and turn to its opposite of removing hair? Our answer, by Allah's aid: We abandoned it for its opposite about which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed us it is better."
"3861","3367 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah Musa ibn Masud narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb al-Jarmi, from his father, from Wail ibn Hujr, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with long hair, so he said: Dhubab. I thought he meant me, so I went and cut it. Then I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: I did not mean you, but this is better."
"3862","3368 - And as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uqbah, brother of Qabisah, narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Said, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Wail ibn Hujr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. This hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shows cutting hair is better than growing it. What the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) deemed best, nothing is better than it. That best must be adhered to and its opposite abandoned. And it is accepted from him, since this is from him, and since he is worthiest of all beauties of all people besides him, that after this saying he moved to this best and abandoned what before it opposed it. We ask Allah for success."
"3863","Chapter 542 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the days meant in Allah's saying: And remember Allah in numbered days; whoever hastens in two days, no sin upon him, and whoever delays, no sin upon him, for whoever fears. 3369 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yala ibn Ubayd al-Tanafisi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Ata, from Abd al-Rahman al-Dayli, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing at Arafat when people of Najd came asking him about Hajj. He said: Hajj is the day of Arafah; whoever catches Jam before the dawn prayer has caught Hajj. The days of Mina are three, the days of Tashriq: Whoever hastens in two days, no sin upon him, and whoever delays, no sin upon him. Then he seated a man behind him proclaiming that."
"3864","3369M - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Shabab ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Ata, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yamar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it, without mentioning the Najd people's question nor his seating the man behind him. A questioner asked: What is the meaning of His saying: And whoever delays, no sin upon him - when the delayer has fulfilled the days Allah commanded staying in at Mina? One in such case is not rightly told: no sin upon him for what he did - just as one is not told: no sin upon one who prayed Zuhr or all five prayers. Rather no sin is said of one who fell short of something commanded, granted leave to abandon part or all of it. Our answer, by Allah's aid: It may be because Allah loves His dispensations to be taken as He loves His strict commands to be taken. So the one staying to the later departure abandons Allah's dispensation, and Allah lifts sin from him therein by His saying: And whoever delays, no sin upon him. We ask Allah for success."
"3865","Chapter 543 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: O Allah, so-and-so satirized me, knowing I am not a poet to satirize him back, so curse him to the number of his satires of me, or in place of his satires of me. Abu Jafar said: We already mentioned this hadith with its chain earlier in this book. An objector said: This hadith shows had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) been a poet he would have satirized that poet as he satirized him - how could you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the morals you narrate of him prove the contrary? Among what is mentioned thereof: 3370 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Sallam ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Aqil ibn Talhah narrated to us, from Abu Jurayy al-Hujaymi, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Jurayy, belittle nothing of good, even pouring from your bucket into the seeker's bucket, meeting your brother with a cheerful face. Beware trailing the waist-wrapper, for it is of arrogance, and Allah does not love the arrogant. I said: O Messenger of Allah, a man reviles me with what is in me - shall I revile him with what is in him? He said: No; its reward is yours, its sin and burden upon him. So this hadith commands pardon and abandoning reviling one who reviles, and poetry is among the greatest reviling - how could you narrate from him what contradicts these morals? Our answer, by Allah's aid: What he imagined in the first hadith is not as he imagined. The Prophet's saying: So-and-so satirized me, knowing I am not a poet to satirize him back, in our view - and Allah knows best - is denial of poetry of him; for his rank is grander than poets' ranks, the highest rank of prophethood and conveying the message from Allah. Since that was his lofty station, while his satirist's station was low, being of revilers - who moreover satirize only their equals when they satirize, and do not satirize those beneath them, deeming themselves above that - including Hassan ibn Thabit's satire of Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith when he satirized the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3866","As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi Abbad narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Saib ibn Barakah, from his mother, she said: I was with Aishah among women when Hassan ibn Thabit was mentioned; they fell upon and reviled him. Aishah said: Do not revile him; what Allah said has befallen him - he is blind. By Allah, I hope Allah admits him into Paradise for words he spoke about Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he says to Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith - followed by the verse about my honor. Abu Jafar said: Since the matter is as we mentioned, and satirical exchange among nobles is only with their equals, not others, the Prophet's saying we mentioned in the head of this chapter is for this meaning: informing people that his satirist is not his equal to need satirizing back had he been a poet. Then he followed his satire of him by asking Allah to curse him: And whomever Allah curses, you will find no helper for him. We ask Allah for success."
"3867","Chapter 544 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from Ibn Abbas, which we know he took only from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about the intent of Allah's saying: Allah has not made for a man two hearts in his interior. 3371 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih and Rawh ibn al-Faraj al-Qattan together narrated to us, they said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Qabus ibn Abi Zibyan narrated to us that his father narrated to him, he said: We said to Ibn Abbas: Have you considered Allah's saying: Allah has not made for a man two hearts in his interior - what is meant thereby? He said: One day the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was praying and had a passing thought. The hypocrites praying with him said: Do you not see he has two hearts, a heart with you and a heart with them? So Allah revealed: Allah has not made for a man two hearts in his interior. This hadith shows Allah's sending this verse upon His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is refutation of the hypocrites for what they said therein. Allah negated that of him and of others of His creation. Other than this interpretation is narrated from Mujahid, Abd Allah ibn Buraydah and al-Hasan in its interpretation."
"3868","3372 - As Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Warqa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid about: Allah has not made for a man two hearts in his interior. He said: A man of Banu Fihr said: In my belly are two hearts with each of which I understand better than Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) does - and he lied."
"3869","3373 - And as Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Abu Hilal narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, he said: In pre-Islamic ignorance there was a man called Dhu Qalbayn, the two-hearted. So Allah revealed: Allah has not made for a man two hearts in his interior."
"3870","3374 - And as Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Mubarak narrated to us, from al-Hasan, he said: A man would say: My soul commanded me so, and my soul commanded me so. So Allah revealed: Allah has not made for a man two hearts in his interior. Abu Jafar said: The first interpretation is worthiest of them, especially as it entered the supported chain by its narrators referring it to Ibn Abbas. We ask Allah for success."
"3871","Chapter 545 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the occasion for which was revealed: Those whom the angels take in death while wronging themselves. 3375 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Ibrahim ibn Munqidh together narrated to us, they said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal al-Asadi narrated to us, he said: A dispatch to Yemen was imposed upon the people of Madinah and I was among them. I met Ikrimah and he forbade me from that, then said: Ibn Abbas informed me that some Muslims used to swell the numbers of the idolaters; an arrow shot would strike one of them and kill him. So Allah revealed: Those whom the angels take in death while wronging themselves."
"3872","3376 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Lahiah, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that some Muslims were with the idolaters swelling their numbers against the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); an arrow shot would strike one of them and kill him, or he would be struck and killed. So Allah revealed: Those whom the angels take in death while wronging themselves, saying: In what were you - to the end of the verse."
"3873","3377 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Sahl al-Araj narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sharik al-Makki narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Some people of Makkah had become Muslim and concealed Islam. The idolaters brought them out on the day of Badr with them, some before others. The Muslims said: These companions of ours were Muslim and were coerced, so seek forgiveness for them. Then this verse came down: Those whom the angels take in death while wronging themselves - to the end of the verse. An objector said: What is the meaning of His saying joined to what you cited in this verse: Except the weak among men, women and children, unable to devise a plan nor guided to a way; for them perhaps Allah will pardon them - when they had no sins to be pardoned, for pardon is only for those deserving punishment for their sins, while these have no sins therein deserving punishment. Our answer, by Allah's aid: Pardon is two kinds: one is the pardon mentioned, and one is lifting of worship where it is lifted, so it becomes no worship entailing reward for performing and punishment for abandoning. Of that is the Messenger of Allah's saying: I have pardoned you charity of horses and slaves - not that something was due of them which he pardoned, but leaving it to them with no right upon them therein nor worship imposed. Of that is Ibn Abbas's saying: The pre-Islamic people ate some things and left some from disgust; when He sent His Prophet, He made lawful His lawful and unlawful His unlawful; what He forbade is unlawful, what He permitted is lawful, and what He was silent about is pardon. His meaning in: what He was silent about is pardon is not pardon of punishments for sins therein, but leaving what was pardoned for them thereof with no worship imposing reward for doing and punishment for abandoning. Likewise - and Allah knows best - His pardon mentioned in the verse for the weak men, women and children unable to devise a plan nor guided to a way, by His saying: For them perhaps Allah will pardon them - and His saying perhaps Allah will pardon them is upon making pardon certain for them, since they had no stratagem to move from their place to praiseworthy places. So Allah lifted that from them, not imposing therein what He imposed on others by His saying upon His Messenger's tongue: I am free of every Muslim with an idolater; their two fires shall not see each other - which we already mentioned with its chain in this book. What is in this hadith is stern threat, and Allah lifted its like from residents in those places unable to flee and move to praiseworthy places, lifting worship therein thereby. Allah knows best what He intended. Him we ask for success."
"3874","Chapter 546 - Explanation of the difficulty in what the reciters differed in of their recitations: There was for Saba - whether it admits inflection, as those who read: There was for Sabain in their dwelling - or contrary, without inflection, as those who read it uninflected. 3378 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Hubayrah narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Walah al-Sabai, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about Saba, what it is. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is a man who fathered ten tribes; six settled Yemen and four Syria. As for the Yemenis: Madhhij, Kindah, al-Azd, al-Ashariyyun, Anmar and Himyar, all Arabs. As for the Syrians: Lakhm, Judham, Amilah and Ghassan."
"3875","3379 - Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Hisham al-Khazzaz Abu Jafar narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah Hammad ibn Usamah narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn al-Hakam al-Nakhai, he said: Abu Safrah al-Nakhai narrated to us - so it is in my book, and so I memorized it from Muhammad ibn Sulayman, while people say: Abu Sabrah al-Nakhai - from Farwah ibn Musayk al-Ghatafani, so he narrated to us, while genealogists say: al-Ghutayfi, a clan of Murad. He said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I not fight those of my people who turn away with those who come forward? He said: Yes. Then it occurred to me and I said: O Messenger of Allah, nay, rather the people of Saba, for they are mightier and stronger. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me and permitted me to fight Saba. When I left him, Allah revealed about Saba what He revealed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What happened to the Ghatafani? He sent to my dwelling and found I had set out, so he brought me back. When I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions, he said: Call the people; whoever answers you of them, accept, and whoever does not, do not hasten against him till you report to me. A man of the people said: O Messenger of Allah, what is Saba? Is it land or woman? He said: It is neither land nor woman, but a man who fathered ten of the Arabs: six went right to Yemen, four went left to Syria. Those who went left: Lakhm, Judham, Ghassan and Amilah. Those who went right: al-Azd, Kindah, Himyar, al-Ashariyyun, Anmar and Madhhij. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, what is Anmar? He said: They from whom is Khatham. Abu Jafar said: When we considered that, we found in Muhammad ibn Sulayman's hadith: Nay, rather the people of Saba - so we knew intended by Saba is a land containing those ascribed to Saba. Above that is Allah's saying in His Book narrating the hoopoe's words to Sulayman: And I have come to you from Saba with certain news; indeed I found a woman ruling them - confirming they are dwellers of a land called Saba. It may have been named Saba as tribes name lands: Hamdan for the tribe Hamdan settling it, Murad for Murad settling it, Himyar for Himyar settling it, and the like. So Saba may be said for the tribe settled by those tracing lineage to Saba. If the name is for the land, it must be uninflected; if for its dwellers, since they trace to Saba the man who fathered them as a tribe, it must be inflected. So preference returned to the reading of one who read it: There was for Sabaa, not to the reading: There was for Sabain. Then we looked at who read it with inflection and who without."
"3876","We found Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: al-Amash read: From Sabain with lowering and nunation, and Asim likewise, Hamzah likewise, Nafi likewise, Ibn Muhaysin likewise."
"3877","And we found Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Khalaf narrated to us, he said: al-Khaffaf narrated to us, from Said, from Qatadah about: From Sabain likewise, making him a man. He said: And Ibn Kathir reads: From Sabaa with نصب, and Abu Amr likewise."
"3878","And we found Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Khalaf narrated to us, he said: al-Khaffaf narrated to us, from Ismail, from al-Hasan likewise, making it a land."
"3879","And we found Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Khalaf narrated to us, he said: al-Khaffaf narrated to us, meaning from Harun, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Ishaq, not inflecting it likewise."
"3880","And we found Wallad the grammarian narrated to us, he said: al-Musadiri narrated to us, from Abu Ubaydah about: There was for Sabaa in their dwellings - whoever nunates makes him father of the tribe, whoever does not makes it a land. And we found al-Farra mentioned from al-Ruasi that he asked Abu Amr ibn al-Ala: Why did you not inflect Saba? He said: I do not know what it is. Al-Farra said: He took a way in not knowing what it is, mentioning that Arabs when naming with an unknown name leave it uninflected. Abu Jafar said: Lost upon Abu Amr was what was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated from him by Ibn Abbas and Farwah ibn Musayk al-Ghatafani. As for our preference in recitation here, it is Abu Amr's reading and those agreeing with him of those we mentioned - for though he was a man, he became a tribe, as Thamud, a man, was left uninflected, referred to the tribe. Likewise Saba when referred to the tribe is likewise in absence of inflection. So held Abu Ubayd as Abd al-Aziz mentioned to us from him. We ask Allah for success."
"3881","Chapter 547 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about how they counted signs. 3380 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Israil ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: Abd Allah ibn Masud heard of a land-swallowing and said: We, the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), used to count signs as blessing, while you count them as fright. While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with no water, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek one with extra water. Water was brought and he poured it into a vessel, then placed his palm in it, and water gushed from between his fingers. Then he said: Come to the blessed purification, and blessing is from Allah. So we drank of it. Abd Allah said: And we used to hear the food glorifying Allah while we ate. Abu Jafar said: Abd Allah's saying: we counted them blessing and you count them fright, may mean: we counted them blessing because we feared thereby, increasing in faith and deeds, so that became blessing for us, while you count them fright without doing deeds bringing you blessing. What Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, said is not contrary to Allah's Book in His saying: We send signs only as fright - i.e. fright for you thereby, so you increase in deeds and faith, returning to you as blessing. We ask Allah for success."
"3882","Chapter 548 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about what he confided: may one to whom he confided reveal it in his lifetime or after his death? We already narrated in this book the hadith of Masruq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about the gathering of the Prophet's wives, the coming of Fatimah his daughter, peace be upon her, his confiding to her what made her weep, then confiding to her after that what made her laugh, Aishah asking her about it in the Prophet's lifetime and her refusal to tell, saying: I would not disclose the Messenger of Allah's secret; and that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, Aishah said to her: I adjure you by my right upon you to tell me - meaning what he had confided to her - and her saying: As for now, yes; when he confided to me first he said: Jibril used to review the Quran with me once every year, and now he reviewed it twice; I think my term has come, so fear Allah, for I am an excellent predecessor for you. So I wept my weeping you saw. Then he confided to me secondly: Are you not pleased to be mistress of the women of this ummah, or the believing women? So I laughed. So in this hadith is her concealing the Prophet's secret to her in his lifetime and telling it after his death. An objector said: How could you narrate this of her, peace be upon her, when you narrated of others what contradicts it? 3381 - He mentioned what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Thabit, he said: Anas narrated to us, he said: I served the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day till, seeing I had finished serving him, I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) will nap. So I left him and found boys playing; I stood watching their play. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to the boys, greeted them, then called me and sent me for his need, as if awaiting me till I return. I was late to my mother beyond my usual time. She said: What kept you? I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me for a need. She said: What is it? I said: It is the Messenger of Allah's secret. My mother said: Guard the Messenger of Allah's secret. So I never told anyone of that need; had I told anyone I would have told you."
"3883","3382 - And what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I was among boys when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon us and greeted us, then took my hand and sent me for a need of his, sitting in the garden or its shade till I returned to him. When I came to Umm Sulaym she said: What kept you? I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me with a message. She said: What is it? I said: It is a secret. She said: Then guard the Messenger of Allah's secret. So I never told anyone of it afterwards."
"3884","3383 - And what al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Yaqub narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Sad, freedman of al-Hasan ibn Ali, from Abd Allah ibn Jafar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mounted me behind him one day, then confided to me a hadith I shall tell no one."
"3885","3384 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when Hafsah became widowed from her husband, who had witnessed Badr and died at Madinah, Umar said: I met Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, and offered him Hafsah. He said: I shall consider. He tarried nights, then met me and said: It appears to me not to marry this day of mine. Then I met Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and offered her to him; Abu Bakr was silent and gave me no reply. I was angrier with him than with Uthman. I tarried nights, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) proposed to her and I married her to him. Then Abu Bakr met me and said: Perhaps you resented me when you offered me Hafsah and I gave you no reply? I said: Yes. He said: Nothing prevented me replying except that I knew the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had mentioned her, and I would not disclose the Messenger of Allah's secret; had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left her, I would have accepted her."
"3886","3385 - And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, he said: Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salim ibn Abd Allah informed me that he heard Abd Allah ibn Umar narrating that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when Hafsah daughter of Umar became widowed from Khunays ibn Hudhafah al-Sahmi, a Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who had witnessed Badr, Umar said: I met Uthman, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. Abu Jafar said: This speaker said: Since Abd Allah ibn Jafar and Anas ibn Malik concealed the Prophet's secret in his lifetime, saying they would never tell anyone, how could others mentioned in these reports disclose the Prophet's secret in any case, when you narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what requires that?"
"3887","3386 - He mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ata, from Abd al-Malik ibn Jabir ibn Atik, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a man narrates a narration then turns, it is a trust."
"3888","3387 - And what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3889","3388 - And what Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ata ibn ibnat Abi Labibah narrated to me, that Abd al-Malik ibn Jabir ibn Atik informed him, that Jabir ibn Abd Allah informed him, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When a man narrates a narration and the listener sees the speaker turning around, it is a trust. This speaker said: This hadith forbids disclosing secrets in its owner's lifetime and after his death. Our answer, by Allah's aid: What Fatimah did - concealing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his lifetime and telling after his death - was when what he confided to her became manifest, so when it left secrecy to its opposite she could narrate it of him. Likewise Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, in his excuse to Umar, since it became manifest and ceased to be secret, he could narrate it of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). As for Abd Allah ibn Jafar and Anas ibn Malik, it may concern something never manifest, so they did what is obligatory of concealing it. Weightiest of all is what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Jabir ibn Abd Allah's hadith: When a man narrates a narration then turns, it is a trust - i.e. a trust the listener is entrusted with, impermissible to betray and disclose, for therein may be loss of his blood or else corrupting his affairs. So by Allah's praise, what we narrated from the Prophet's Companions agrees with what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter. We ask Allah for success."
"3890","Chapter 549 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his household about the measure of Sadaqat al-Fitr of wheat and other than it. 3389 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Numan Arim Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Sadusi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us. 3390 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Sadaqat al-Fitr, for every young and old, free and slave, a sa of barley or a sa of dates. He said: So people equated it with two mudds of wheat."
"3891","3391 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, he said: Ayyub informed us, from Nafi, freedman of Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated Sadaqat al-Fitr, a sa of dates or barley. Ibn Umar said: Then people brought half a sa of wheat - or said: people equated half a sa of wheat with a sa of barley - and they brought it and it was accepted from them. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith from Ibn Umar, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) directed Sadaqat al-Fitr to these two kinds mentioned, to the exclusion of other kinds; and people after him equated that with two mudds of wheat. It is narrated from Abd Allah ibn Shawdhab, from Ayyub, this hadith with an added third kind alongside these two."
"3892","3392 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Ibn Shawdhab, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated Sadaqat al-Fitr upon the free and slave, young and old, male and female: a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, or a sa of wheat. He said: Then people equated half a sa of wheat with a sa of other than it. Abu Jafar said: We know none of Ayyub's companions following Ibn Shawdhab in adding this kind in this hadith, though each of Hammad ibn Zayd and Hammad ibn Salamah is proof against him therein, and he is no proof against them - how when both together oppose him therein? In his hadith is what proves his error: his saying: Then people equated half a sa of wheat with a sa of other than it - how could they equate an obligated kind with part of another obligated kind alongside it? An obligated thing is only equated with other than it of what is not obligated. Then this hadith is also narrated from Nafi by other than Ayyub, as Hammad and Hammad narrated from Ayyub, not as Ibn Shawdhab narrated from him. Among them: Ubayd Allah ibn Umar al-Umari."
"3893","3393 - As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, then he mentioned the like of Hammad ibn Zayd's hadith, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, with what is therein from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the people's equating after him. Among them: Malik ibn Anas."
"3894","3394 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik informed him. 3395 - And as Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Maslamah ibn Qanab narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then both mentioned the like of Hammad ibn Zayd's hadith, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, and Hammad ibn Salamah's, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), without mentioning the equating. Among them: Umar ibn Nafi."
"3895","3396 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan al-Basri informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jahdam narrated to us, he said: Ismail - meaning Ibn Jafar - narrated to us, from Umar ibn Nafi, from his father, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated Zakat al-Fitr: a sa of dates or a sa of barley, upon the free and slave, male and female, young and old. He did not mention the equating. Among them: al-Layth ibn Sad."
"3896","3397 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi and Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, they said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), mentioning as in Hammad ibn Zayd's and Hammad ibn Salamah's hadith, except that he did not mention the equating. Among them: Yunus ibn Yazid."
"3897","3398 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman and Tahir ibn Amr ibn al-Rabi ibn Tariq narrated to us, they said: Amr ibn al-Rabi ibn Tariq narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, from Yunus ibn Yazid, that Nafi informed him, he said: Abd Allah ibn Umar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated Zakat al-Fitr. Then he mentioned the like of the two Hammad hadiths, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: And Ibn Umar used to say: People made its equivalent two mudds of wheat. Abu Jafar said: This group by what they narrated from Nafi agree with what Ayyub narrated from him in the two Hammad hadiths - worthier than what Ibn Shawdhab narrated from Ayyub in excess thereof. Then we looked: is anything narrated about the measure of Sadaqat al-Fitr other than this hadith of Ibn Umar?"
"3898","3399 - We found Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Iyad ibn Abd Allah ibn Sad ibn Abi Sarh, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: We used to give Zakat al-Fitr of Ramadan: a sa of food, or a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, or a sa of dried yogurt."
"3899","3400 - And we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Iyad ibn Abd Allah, that he heard Abu Said saying: We used to pay Sadaqat al-Fitr: a sa of food, or a sa of barley, or a sa of dates, or a sa of dried yogurt."
"3900","3401 - And we found Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, from Iyad ibn Abd Allah ibn Sad, from Abu Said, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was among us, we used to pay Sadaqat al-Fitr: a sa of food, or a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, or a sa of raisins, or a sa of dried yogurt. We kept paying it till Muawiyah came as pilgrim or visitant; among what he addressed people with, he said: Pay two mudds of Syrian wheat as equivalent to a sa of barley."
"3901","3402 - And we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Nafi al-Madani narrated to me, from Dawud ibn Qays, from Iyad, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3902","3403 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, adding: Abu Said said: As for me, I pay only as I used to pay."
"3903","3404 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Iyad, from Abu Said, he said: In Ramadan charity, whoever brought a sa of barley it was accepted from him, whoever brought a sa of dates it was accepted, whoever brought a sa of dried yogurt it was accepted, whoever brought a sa of raisins it was accepted."
"3904","3405 - And we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, and we found al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, they said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abd Allah ibn Uthman, that Iyad ibn Abd Allah narrated to him that Abu Said said: We used to pay in the Prophet's time only a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, or a sa of dried yogurt, paying nothing else. When food grew abundant in Muawiyah's time, they made it two mudds of wheat."
"3905","3406 - And we found Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Uthman, from Iyad ibn Abd Allah, he said: I heard Abu Said, asked about Sadaqat al-Fitr, saying: I pay only what I paid in the Prophet's time: a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, or a sa of raisins, or a sa of dried yogurt. A man said to him: Or two mudds of wheat? He said: No; that is Muawiyah's valuation - I neither accept nor act by it. Abu Jafar said: In what we narrated in this chapter from Abu Said is mention of what they paid in the Prophet's time in Sadaqat al-Fitr: in some a sa of food or barley, in some a sa of barley without a sa of food, and in all mention of other kinds besides these two. The food mentioned therein may be wheat - yet if so, it is upon payment, possibly voluntary of the payers. Worthier is what Ibn Umar narrated of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated therein, and what people after him equated of other kinds. An objector said: Is Abu Said's rejection of the valuation mentioned therein? Our answer, by Allah's aid: Abu Said did not reject valuation, but the valuer. Valuation was not what Abu Said rejected; it was of people among the Prophet's Companions whose valuation stands, as Abd Allah ibn Umar said in his hadith - though whom Abu Said rejected in valuing is a man of the Prophet's companionship with understanding, so he with his followers is proof. Though we narrated from Abu Said that half a sa of wheat suffices in Sadaqat al-Fitr."
"3906","As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, that Marwan sent to Abu Said: Send me the zakat of your slaves. Abu Said said to the messenger: Marwan does not know; we must give for every slave head each Fitr a sa of dates or half a sa of wheat. What we narrated from Abu Said in this hadith proves our interpretation of his rejection above, though we found in what is narrated raised to the Prophet about wheat paid in Sadaqat al-Fitr in the Prophet's time, that it is half a sa."
"3907","3407 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us before I met him, then I met him and he narrated to me, as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to me from him, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Asma, she said: We used to pay Zakat al-Fitr in the Prophet's time: two mudds."
"3908","3408 - And as Fahd ibn Sulayman and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me: that Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to him, from his father, that Asma daughter of Abu Bakr informed him, that she used to pay in the Prophet's time, for her household free and enslaved, two mudds of wheat, or a sa of dates, with the mudd or sa they lived by."
"3909","3409 - And as al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, and as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Fatimah daughter of al-Mundhir, from Asma daughter of Abu Bakr, she said: She used to pay Zakat al-Fitr in the Prophet's time: two mudds of wheat."
"3910","3410 - And as Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Numan ibn Rashid, from al-Zuhri, from Thalabah ibn Abi Suayr, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pay Sadaqat al-Fitr: a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, or half a sa of wheat - or said: wheat - for every person, young or old, male or female, free or slave, rich or poor."
"3911","3411 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Numan ibn Rashid, from al-Zuhri, from Thalabah ibn Abi Suayr, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A sa of wheat, for every two, free or slave, male or female; as for your rich, Allah purifies him, and as for your poor, there is returned to him the like of what he gave. In what we narrated in this section from Asma is mention of what they paid in Zakat al-Fitr of wheat in the Prophet's time: half a sa. In Thalabah ibn Abi Suayr's hadith the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded paying that measure of wheat - confirming the half-sa of wheat, and showing what they paid beyond it of wheat then was voluntary increase in good, not obligation. An objector said: Bakr ibn Wail al-Kufi narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri, differing therein from al-Numan from al-Zuhri."
"3912","3412 - He mentioned what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Bakr al-Kufi narrated to us, that al-Zuhri narrated to him, from Abd Allah ibn Thalabah ibn Suayr, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood preaching and commanded Sadaqat al-Fitr: a sa of dates or a sa of barley, for every one - or said: for every head - young and old, free and slave."
"3913","3413 - And what Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Jannad narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he did not say: free and slave. He said: So Bakr differed from al-Numan from al-Zuhri in this hadith. Our answer, by Allah's aid: He did not differ, but fell short of him; one who adds is worthier than one who falls short. Thereby established is what al-Numan narrated. We found eminent Followers reporting the obligation in the Prophet's time in Zakat al-Fitr of wheat was two mudds."
"3914","3414 - And as al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, and as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, they said: al-Layth said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid and Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyab: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated Zakat al-Fitr: two mudds of wheat."
"3915","3415 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Azdi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zura Wahb Allah ibn Rashid narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Uqayl narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, that he heard Said ibn al-Musayyab, Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman and Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Zakat al-Fitr: a sa of dates, or two mudds of wheat."
"3916","3416 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, and Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah ibn Utbah, and al-Qasim, and Salim, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded concerning Sadaqat al-Fitr a sa of barley or two mudds of wheat."
"3917","3417 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Said, and Ubaydullah, and al-Qasim, and Salim, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"3918","3418 - And as Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abd al-Khaliq al-Shaybani - and he is Ibn Salamah - from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Charity used to be given in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, as half a sa of wheat. So in what we have narrated of this is what indicates that half a sa of wheat was in Sadaqat al-Fitr an origin among the origins which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated therein, and in that is what sufficed from valuation. Then an objector said: As for what you narrated of the hadith of Iyad ibn Abdillah, from Abu Said, of the people paying in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what they used to pay of what their payment of it was mentioned therein, you narrated it in what preceded of this chapter as payment, not as obligation, yet it has been narrated that that was as an obligation which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) imposed upon them."
"3919","3419 - Then he mentioned what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Harb informed me, he said: Muhriz ibn al-Waddah narrated to us, from Ismail - and he is Ibn Umayyah - from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Dhubab, from Iyad ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Sarh, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated Sadaqat al-Fitr as a sa of barley, or a sa of dates, or a sa of dried curd. This objector said: And in this hadith, from other than the narration of this shaykh, is what you mentioned in this chapter: their payment of a sa of food therein, and food means wheat, so in that is what indicates that the sa of wheat had been obligated therein. Our answer to him in that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that the obligation mentioned in this hadith was mentioned only in the hadith of al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, and Zayd ibn Aslam opposed him in that, as did those whose opposition to him we mentioned in this chapter, of Dawud ibn Qays, and Ibn Ajlan also opposed him in that."
"3920","3420 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Mansur informed us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ajlan narrated to us, he said: I heard Iyad ibn Abdillah reporting from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: We did not give out in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, or a sa of raisins, or a sa of flour, or a sa of dried curd, or a sa of sult. Then Sufyan doubted, saying: flour or sult. That indicates the consecutiveness of the narration from Iyad ibn Abdillah, contrary to what al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated from him, and the group in that is more worthy than the single one."
"3921","3421 - Al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, he said: Ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: We would not give out in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, or a sa of dried curd. Abu Jafar said: What we mentioned has also confirmed that. Then we returned to what the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Rightly-Guided rightly-guided caliphs, may Allah be pleased with them, held in that. So among what we mentioned in the hadith of Ahmad ibn Dawud, from Sulayman ibn Harb, was mention of what they used to give in the time of Abu Bakr and Umar in that, and that it was half a sa of wheat."
"3922","And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Darir, and Hilal ibn Yahya narrated to us, they said: Abu Awanah informed us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abu Qilabah, he said: One who handed to Abu Bakr a sa of wheat shared between two informed me."
"3923","Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, he said: I and al-Hakam ibn Utaybah went to Ziyad ibn al-Nadr, and he narrated to us from Abdillah ibn Nafi, that his father asked Umar ibn al-Khattab, saying: I am a slave man, so is there zakat in my wealth? Umar said: Your zakat is only upon your master, that he pay on your behalf at every Fitr a sa of barley, or a sa of dates, or half a sa of wheat."
"3924","And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Suayr, he said: We used to give out Zakat al-Fitr in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab as half a sa."
"3925","And Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ashath, he said: Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed us and said: Pay Zakat al-Fitr as two mudds of wheat. Abu Jafar said: Thus Abd al-Rahman narrated it to us from his memory."
"3926","As for Ibn Abi Dawud, he narrated it to us from his book, he said: Al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ashath, he said: Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed us and said in his sermon: Pay Sadaqat al-Fitr as a sa of dates or a sa of barley, on behalf of every young and old, free and slave, male and female, and he did not mention therein two mudds of wheat."
"3927","And Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Isa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I commanded the people of Basra, when I was among them, to give on behalf of the young and the old, the free and the slave, two mudds of wheat."
"3928","Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, that Humayd al-Tawil informed them from al-Hasan, he said: Abdillah ibn Abbas addressed upon the pulpit of Basra, saying: O people of Basra, why do you not pay the zakat of your month, then he said: Whoever is here from the people of Medina, stand to your brothers and teach them, and he commanded them with a sa of barley, or dates, or half a sa of wheat. Then when Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, came, he said: O people of Basra, your prices are cheap; if only you made it a sa of wheat. So in what we have narrated of half a sa of wheat, that it applies in Sadaqat al-Fitr, is what establishes the proof for whoever holds that view against his opponents therein. And that has also been narrated from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and from Mujahid and Ibrahim."
"3929","As Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Humran narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Umar ibn Artah a letter which was read upon the pulpit of Basra: To proceed, command those under you of the Muslims to give out Sadaqat al-Fitr as a sa of dates or half a sa of wheat."
"3930","And as Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibrahim, the like of it."
"3931","And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Mujahid concerning Zakat al-Fitr: a sa of everything except wheat, and wheat is half a sa. So in what we mentioned is what indicates that the half sa of wheat is the obligated amount in Zakat al-Fitr, not what is besides it, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"3932","550 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated concerning Sadaqat al-Fitr of what was intended thereby toward the Muslims. 3422 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him. 3423 - And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he obligated Zakat al-Fitr of Ramadan upon the people, a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, upon every free or slave, male or female, among the Muslims. Then an objector said: Did anyone among those who narrated it from Nafi follow Malik upon this wording - meaning among the Muslims? Our answer to him in that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that Ubaydullah ibn Umar, and Umar ibn Nafi, and Yunus ibn Yazid followed him upon that."
"3933","3424 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Hashimi narrated to us, from Said ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jumahi, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated Zakat al-Fitr of Ramadan till Ramadan as a sa of dates, or a sa of barley upon every free or slave, male or female, among the Muslims."
"3934","3425 - And Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ayan, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Maqabiri narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jumahi narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he did not say: till Ramadan."
"3935","3426 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan al-Basri informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jahdam narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Umar ibn Nafi, from his father, from Abdillah ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated Zakat al-Fitr as a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, upon the slave and the free, the male and the female, the young and the old among the Muslims, and he commanded that it be paid before the people go out to the prayer."
"3936","3427 - And Tahir ibn Amr ibn al-Rabi ibn Tariq narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Yunus ibn Yazid, that Nafi informed him, he said: Abdillah ibn Umar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated upon the people Zakat al-Fitr of Ramadan as a sa of dates, or a sa of barley, upon every person, male or female, or free or slave among the Muslims. Thus it became clear by what we mentioned that this meaning is established in the hadith, meaning among the Muslims. Then an objector said: Is there an obligation upon the slave along with his inability for the obligated amount mentioned in this hadith? Our answer to him in that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that there is no obligation upon the slave in himself, since he has no wealth; rather the obligation is upon his master concerning him. And when that is so, his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): among the Muslims, refers to the masters, not to the slaves. And in that is what indicates that there is no proof in this meaning of this hadith for whoever says that the Muslim man is not required to pay Zakat al-Fitr on behalf of his Christian slave against whoever says that it is required of him therein. And it has been narrated from more than one of the predecessors what agrees with the saying of whoever says that the Muslim pays it on behalf of his Christian slave just as he pays it on behalf of his Muslim slave. And we shall mention that in the sitting which follows this sitting as an addition in this chapter, if Allah wills, and Allah we ask for success."
"3937","3428 - And what Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih, and Abd al-Wahhab ibn Khalaf ibn Umar Abu Ayyub narrated to us, they said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Jafar, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: He used to give out Zakat al-Fitr on behalf of every person, saying: of young or old, or free or slave, even if he was a Christian, two mudds of wheat, or a sa of dates."
"3938","And what Yahya and Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, they said: Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, he said: If you have Christian slaves not managed for trade, then pay zakat on their behalf on the day of Fitr."
"3939","And what Yahya and Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, they said: Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Muhajir narrated to us, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, he said: A man pays on behalf of his slave, even if he is a Christian, Zakat al-Fitr. Abu Jafar said: So here are Abu Hurayrah, and Ata ibn Abi Rabah, and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz who held in this chapter what we mentioned, and it is the view with us in that; for since the Muslim man pays zakat on behalf of his Christian slaves due to his Islam, and that is not dropped from him concerning them due to their disbelief, likewise he pays Zakat al-Fitr on their behalf due to his Islam, and that is not dropped from him concerning them due to their disbelief. And thus Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad used to say in that, and Allah we ask for success."
"3940","551 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what contains the denial of the nullification of his ablution by his sleep in the state in which the ablution of others of his nation is nullified by sleeping likewise. 3429 - Ismail ibn Ishaq ibn Sahl al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Abu Khalid Yazid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dalani, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray the two rakahs of Fajr, then he slept while prostrating or sitting until he snored or breathed heavily, then he stood for the prayer, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, you have slept. He said: Ablution is only obligatory upon one who sleeps lying down, for when he does that his joints relax. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith and found in it the saying of Ibn Abbas to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what was mentioned of his saying to him therein, and that was with us - and Allah knows best - upon that Ibn Abbas held at that time that the sleep of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which he witnessed of him had nullified his ablution, until he said to him on account of that: O Messenger of Allah, you have slept. And when the ablution of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with him nullified by that, the sleep of others by the like of it was more deserving of being nullified. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him at that, answering him and teaching him: Ablution is only obligatory upon one who sleeps lying down. And he informed him of the cause for which ablution is obligatory upon him for that, which is the relaxing of his joints. And that was from him - and Allah knows best - teaching him the ruling of all other people in that besides himself, because it is what he needs so that he acts upon it himself and teaches it to people besides him. As for the ruling of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that concerning himself, it is contrary to that, and that has been narrated from him, from Ibn Abbas in a hadith other than this hadith."
"3941","3430 - And it is what Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, that he spent the night with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the night of his aunt Maymunah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and performed ablution from a hanging water-skin, he said: And he described his ablution, making it brief with his hand. Then Ibn Abbas stood and did the like of what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did, he said: Then I came and stood on his left, and he moved me and placed me on his right, and he prayed, then he lay down and slept until he breathed heavily. Then Bilal came and informed him of the dawn prayer, so he prayed and did not perform ablution. Then an objector said: Ibn Abbas only addressed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his saying to him: You have slept, so how is it permissible that his answer to him be about other than that of what was mentioned in the hadith in which that was mentioned. Our answer to him in that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that that was - and Allah knows best - so that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would teach him that that is the ruling of the sleep which he needs to know concerning himself and concerning all other people besides the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and besides him, and that his need for that is what he has no need for knowing the ruling of the sleep of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that. So he taught him what he needs to know, and deferred what is besides that of what he has no need for, to teach it to him after that, either by a saying from him to him therein, or by an act he does in his presence of that kind, then prays without performing ablution, so that we know thereby from him that his ruling in that is contrary to the ruling of others of his nation. And in that is what carries with it that his sleep may have been in the state in which he slept with his witnessing of him in the hadith of Kurayb from Ibn Abbas, of what was mentioned therein of his prayer after that sleep in the state of lying down without a new ablution, so that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined by his saying to him in the hadith of Abu al-Aliyah and by his act with his witnessing of him in which that was mentioned in the hadith of Kurayb the answer to what he asked him about. And perhaps all of that was in one night, until Ibn Abbas stood upon the distinction of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the rest of his nation in that sleep in that state: that it nullifies the ablution of others of his nation, and that it does not nullify his ablution (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then we sought the meaning by which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, distinguished him thereby from the rest of his nation until his ruling and their rulings differed in that - what it is."
"3942","3431 - Then we found that Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik ibn Anas narrated to him, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he informed him, that he asked Aishah, Mother of the Believers: How was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Ramadan? She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not exceed in Ramadan nor in other than it eleven rakahs; he would pray four, so do not ask about their beauty and length, then he would pray four, so do not ask about their beauty and length, then he would pray three. Aishah said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, do you sleep before you pray witr? He said: O Aishah, my eyes sleep but my heart does not sleep. So we established by what is in this hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), even if his eyes slept, his heart did not sleep. And when his heart does not sleep even if his eyes sleep, his joints do not relax, and when his joints do not relax by that sleep, his ablution is not nullified by it. And we understood thereby that the nullification of the ablution of others by the like of that sleep was only due to the relaxing of their joints. So by the praise of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His blessing, all the meanings of these reports which we narrated in this chapter became clear, and the meaning by which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, distinguished His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by what He distinguished him thereby from the rest of his nation besides him, so that his ablution remained with his sleep, while the ablution of others of his nation besides him was nullified by the like of that sleep, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"3943","552 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the sleep by which the ablution of others of his nation besides him is nullified. 3432 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Hakim ibn Sayf narrated to us. And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, they said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Wadin ibn Ata, from Mahfuz ibn Alqamah al-Hadrami, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Aidh al-Asdi, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The eye is the stopper of the anus, so whoever sleeps, let him perform ablution. Abu Jafar said: Thus every one of the people of hadith whom we met narrates this hadith, saying: it is the stopper of al-satt, while the people of Arabic differ with them in that, saying stopper of al-sah, and likewise Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz mentioned to us, from Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam. Abu Ubayd said: His saying: al-sah means the ring of the anus, and al-wika in origin is the thread, or the strap with which the mouth of the water-skin is tied. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made in the hadith narrated from him in that - meaning the hadith of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, which we mentioned - wakefulness of the eye like the stopper of the water-skin, saying: When it sleeps, that stopper relaxes, so impurity occurs from it. He said: And the poet said concerning al-sah - and Abu Ubayd said: Nasr is a tribe of Banu Asad - he said: And another said. Abu Jafar said: As for what is in the hadith: whoever sleeps, let him perform ablution, it carries that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant thereby the sleep with which the stopper relaxes and the joints relax with it, like what is in the hadith of Ibn Abbas which Abu al-Aliyah narrates from him which we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter, and it is the worthiest interpretation to carry it upon until its meaning agrees with the meaning of that hadith of Ibn Abbas. And this meaning has also been indicated."
"3944","3433 - What al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us. 3434 - And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh al-Hadrami, and Sulayman ibn Abdillah al-Raqqi narrated to us, they said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam - al-Rabi said in his hadith: He said: Atiyyah ibn Qays al-Kilabi narrated to me, and Abu Umayyah said in his hadith: from Atiyyah ibn Qays, then they agreed, saying: from Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The two eyes are only the stopper of the anus, so when the two eyes sleep, the stopper loosens. Abu Jafar said: And that has also been indicated."
"3945","3435 - What Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik ibn Anas narrated to him, and what al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you dozes in his prayer, let him lie down until sleep goes away from him, for when one of you prays while drowsy, perhaps he goes to seek forgiveness but curses himself."
"3946","3436 - And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"3947","3437 - And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: And Yahya ibn Abdillah ibn Salim narrated to me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"3948","3438 - And what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Hilal informed us, he said: Abd al-Warith - meaning Ibn Said al-Tannuri - narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you dozes while praying, let him leave, lest he supplicate against himself without knowing. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is what indicates that a man may pray while drowsy, and the like of that also."
"3949","3439 - What Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Humayd al-Tawil, that he heard Anas ibn Malik say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a rope stretched between two pillars in the mosque and said: What is this rope? They said: So-and-so woman prays; when she fears being overcome, she holds onto it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let her pray as long as she understands; when she is overcome, let her sleep. So in his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): let her pray as long as she understands, is what indicates that she may pray while sleep has mingled with her, even if of what does not overcome her. That indicates that nothing of sleep nullifies ablution except what is accompanied by relaxing of the joints, as in the hadith of Ibn Abbas which Abu al-Aliyah narrated from him, which is the hadith we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter. Then an objector said: Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts that."
"3950","3440 - Then he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, he said: I said to Safwan ibn Assal: There occurred in my self, or in my chest, wiping over the two leather socks after stool and urine, so did you hear anything from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, he used to command us when we were traveling to not remove them for three days and their nights except from janabah, but from stool and urine and sleep."
"3951","3441 - And what al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Sufyan and Hammad ibn Zayd and Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Safwan ibn Assal, he said: When we were traveling with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he commanded us not to remove our leather socks for three days and their nights except from janabah, but from stool and sleep and urine. He said: In this hadith is what indicates that sleep nullifies ablution in whatever state it is. Our answer to him in that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that it carries that that sleep may be that with which is the loosening of the stopper and the relaxing of the joints, until this report and the reports we mentioned before it agree and none contradicts another. And the proof of the correctness of this interpretation is what the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held in his time and after him in that."
"3952","3442 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Abdillah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed the last Isha prayer one night until the people slept, then they woke, and Umar came and said: O Messenger of Allah, the prayer, the prayer. He said: Then they prayed, and he did not mention that they performed ablution."
"3953","3443 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The last Isha prayer was established, and a man stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have a need. So he stood with him conversing with him until some of the people dozed, then he came and prayed, and he did not mention that they performed ablution."
"3954","3444 - Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Hilal narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: We used to come to the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) awaiting the prayer; among us were those who doze and sleep, or doze, then pray without performing ablution."
"3955","3445 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: The Isha prayer was established, and a man stood to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and kept speaking to him until some of the people or the people dozed, then they prayed and did not perform ablution."
"3956","3446 - And as Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, he said: The Isha prayer was established one night, and a man intercepted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and spoke to him concerning a need for a long part of the night, until some of the people dozed, then he came and led them in prayer."
"3957","3447 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Harami ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: al-Furat ibn Abi al-Furat narrated to us, he said: Ata ibn Abi Rabah narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I slept and woke, then slept and woke. Then a man of the Muslims stood and said: The prayer, the prayer. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us with his head dripping - he said: And I think the man was Umar, may Allah be pleased with him - and led us in prayer, saying: Were it not that I would burden my nation, I would have loved that they pray this prayer at this hour."
"3958","3448 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, he said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sleep, then stand and pray without performing ablution."
"3959","And as Salih narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn informed us, from Mujahid, he said: Ibn Umar, when dawn rose, would pray two rakahs, then sit with his knees drawn up while we were around him; if one of us saw him doze, he would move him. And he would doze while sitting with knees drawn, then the prayer would be established and he would rise and pray."
"3960","And as Salih narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he used to say: Whoever sleeps while sitting, there is no ablution upon him."
"3961","And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar, when he slept sitting, would not perform ablution, and when he slept lying down, would perform ablution."
"3962","And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Ilyas narrated to us, from Muhammad and Abu Bakr, the two sons of al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: Whoever sleeps while sitting, there is no ablution upon him, and whoever sleeps lying down, upon him is ablution. He said: So these Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his life and after his death held concerning sleep what we mentioned from them in these reports in word and deed without disagreement among them therein: that their ablution is nullified only in a specific kind of sleep. And the worthiest view in that is that that specific kind is what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) specified thereof, describing it by the relaxing of the joints with which the sleeper has no control of himself against the causes which nullify his ablution. And it is understood with that that the standing, the sitting, and the prostrating are devoid of that, while the lying down has that in it. And when that is so, ablution is not nullified except in that state, so as not to depart from anything of what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then from his Companions in this chapter, may Allah's pleasure be upon them. Then an objector said: Something has been narrated from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, which contradicts what you narrated in this chapter. Then he mentioned what had."
"3963","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Jurayri. And what Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: al-Jurayri informed us, then they agreed, saying: from Khalid ibn Ghallaq, from Abu Hurayrah, that he said: Whoever is overcome by sleep, ablution has become obligatory upon him. And what we preserve concerning this Khalid, from all who narrated to us this hadith as we mentioned it, is Ibn Allaq with ayn, while al-Bukhari and Muhammad ibn Sad mentioned that he is Ghallaq, and Muhammad in particular mentioned that he is Ayshi, and Allah knows best the reality of his name. Our answer to him in that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that what Abu Hurayrah said of what we mentioned from him in this hadith does not contradict what is in the first hadiths; for what is therein from him is his saying: Whoever is overcome by sleep, ablution has become obligatory upon him. So it is permissible that being overcome by sleep with him is that with which is the relaxing of the joints, and that is the worthiest interpretation to carry it upon so that his saying therein agrees with the sayings of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides him. And among what confirms what he mentioned of the relaxing of the joints is that falling occurs with that, and what falling does not occur with is contrary to that. And whatever is of what falling to the ground occurs with, its person is in the ruling of one sleeping upon the ground, so it is understood that upon him is ablution, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"3964","553 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the clinging of Abdillah ibn al-Mughaffal to the bag of fat which was lowered on the day of Khaybar, and his saying with that: I shall give nothing of it after today, and the smiling of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at that. 3449 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdillah ibn Mughaffal, he said: We were besieging Khaybar when a man threw a bag containing fat, so I leapt to take it. Then I turned, and there was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I felt shy of him. Abu Jafar said: We brought this hadith, even though it does not contain the meaning with which we titled this chapter, so that no one thinks it escaped us of the hadith of Shubah."
"3965","3450 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Humayd ibn Hilal narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Mughaffal, he said: I found a bag of fat on the day of Khaybar and clung to it, saying: I shall give no one anything of this today. Then I turned, and there was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiling."
"3966","3451 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdillah ibn Mughaffal, he said: A bag of fat was lowered on the day of Khaybar, so I clung to it and said: I shall give no one anything of this today. Then I turned toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he smiled at me. Then an objector said: How do you narrate the like of this, when you have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts this."
"3967","3452 - Then he mentioned what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Budayl ibn Maysarah al-Uqayli, from Abdillah ibn Shaqiq, from a man of Bulqayn, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at Wadi al-Qura, and said: O Messenger of Allah, to whom belongs the spoils? He said: To Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is a share, and to these are four shares. I said: Is anyone more entitled to anything of the spoils than anyone? He said: No, not even the arrow which one of you takes from his side; he is not more entitled to it than his brother. He said: In this hadith is that all the Muslims are partners in the booty, and that none of them is more entitled to anything of it than the rest of them, while the hadith of Ibn al-Mughaffal which you narrated contradicts this. Our answer to him in that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that his arguing against us with this hadith shows his ignorance of the sound hadith from the corrupt, and that he is of those with no distinction between them; for this hadith, even if Hammad ibn Salamah narrated it from Budayl ibn Maysarah, from Abdillah ibn Shaqiq, from a man of Bulqayn, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so that was possible, even though its narrator is unnamed, as to his meeting the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and taking from him - yet Ibn al-Mubarak narrated it from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abdillah ibn Shaqiq, from a man, from a man of Bulqayn, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3968","3453 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Shaqiq, from a man, from a man of Bulqayn, then he mentioned this hadith. So the hadith returned to an unknown man between this Companion and Abdillah ibn Shaqiq, so it became obligatory that the like of it not be argued with. And after this, what Ibn al-Mughaffal did was only in food of the booty, while the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the food of the booty were upon."
"3969","3454 - What Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Abu Yusuf, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi al-Mujalid, from Abdillah ibn Abi Awfa, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Khaybar; one of us would come to the food of the booty and take from it his need."
"3970","3455 - And what Ahmad ibn Khalid ibn Yazid al-Farisi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: We would obtain in our raids - then he mentioned grapes and honey - and eat it without raising it. Abu Jafar said: And when it was wide taking what the booty of the Muslims preceded them to, until they appropriate it for their need thereof, and until they eat it apart from others of the people of the booty of those with no need thereof, or of those who appropriated the like of it for their need thereof, what Ibn al-Mughaffal did, which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not disapprove, of his taking with his hand and his saying with his tongue, was wider, and the permissibility for him in that was greater. As for what is besides that of what enters into the hadith of al-Balqayni, it is of what the cast one has no need for; but if he needs it to throw with it at whoever threw it at him or others of his enemy, his keeping it for that is free for him. So it became clear, by the praise of Allah and His blessing, that there is no contradiction in these two hadiths, nor disagreement, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"3971","554 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to Abu al-Darda: Taffa al-sa. 3456 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn al-Harith al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ihab narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from his father, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from his father, from Salim ibn Abi Salim al-Jayshani, from Abu al-Darda, he said: A brother of mine died leaving his wife, and a brother of his by his mother proposed to me for her. So I came to her and said: Do not marry so-and-so. That reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he passed by me and said: O Abu al-Darda, O son of the water of the sky, taffa al-sa."
"3972","3457 - And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Yaqub narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Salih, and Amr ibn al-Harith narrated, from his father, from Abu Salim al-Jayshani, he said: A brother of Abu al-Darda by his father died, leaving a brother by his mother, who married his wife. Abu al-Darda became angry when he heard that, so he came to her and stood over her saying: Did you marry the son of the slave woman?! He repeated that to her, and she said - may Allah set you right - he was the brother of my husband, and he was most entitled to me to shelter me and his child. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard of that, so he came to him until he stopped him, then struck upon his shoulder and said: O Abu al-Darda, O son of the water of the sky, taffa al-sa, taffa al-sa, taffa al-sa. Abu Jafar said: The correction of these two chains for this hadith was that there enter into its chain by the narration of Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman thereof with the chain by which he narrated it, Salim ibn Abi Salim, and that there enter into it by the narration of Ishaq ibn Ibrahim thereof with the chain by which Abu Salim narrated it, so that its chain returns to Salim ibn Abi Salim, from Abu Salim, from Abu al-Darda. Then we examined what therein of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu al-Darda on account of it what he said to him therein, and found that Abu al-Darda had before that shown of anger toward the wife of his deceased brother what he had shown to her when she married his brother by his mother whose mother was a slave woman, what the people of pre-Islamic ignorance counted as deficiency in whoever was such, counting whoever was contrary to him above him, and of his threatening her at that with what he threatened her of what Islam has forbidden; since Islam commanded abandoning boasting with lineages which the people of pre-Islamic ignorance used to boast with and excel over one another on account of them, informing them of the equality of people in that, and that none excels over another except by righteous deed. And there was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that."
"3973","3458 - What Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Said al-Maqburi, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has removed from you the pride of pre-Islamic ignorance and its boasting: a believing God-fearing one or a wicked wretched one. You are the children of Adam, and Adam is from dust. Men shall abandon their boasting of peoples who are but coals of the coals of Hell, or they shall be lighter upon Allah, Mighty and Majestic, than the dung beetles which push filth with their noses. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned the boasting which the children of Adam have of some being higher thereby over others to the piety which is in their believer, by which he is higher than their wicked one with whom is wretchedness due to his wickedness. And his saying to Abu al-Darda at that: taffa al-sa, was of this meaning; for taffa al-sa intends falling short of filling the sa and equality therein and gathering it for all people, while they differ therein by what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, differentiated them thereby of the righteous deeds by which He raised ranks for their people, making them thereby contrary to their opposites of those with evil deeds and ugly choices. And there was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that of what Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani narrated from him a hadith additional to the hadith we narrated in this meaning in this chapter."
"3974","3459 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Abdillah ibn Lahiah informed me, from al-Harith ibn Yazid, from Ulayy ibn Rabah, from Uqbah ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These lineages of yours are not disgraces upon anyone. You are but the children of Adam; taffu al-sa which you did not fill. No one has excellence over anyone except by religion or righteous deed. It suffices a man to be obscene, foul-tongued, miserly, cowardly. Abu Jafar said: The taff mentioned in the hadith of Abu al-Darda was deficiency, and from it is the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: Woe to the defrauders, meaning those deficient in measure. So from that was the belittling of Abu al-Darda of the brother of his brother by his mother with what he belittled him thereby, that he is the son of a slave woman, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed him on account of it with what he addressed him thereby in the hadith we mentioned. And Wallad al-Nahwi narrated to us, from al-Masadiri, from Abu Ubaydah, he said: The defrauder is one who does not fulfill to people from people. That is proof of what we mentioned. And Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam mentioned in his book on Gharib al-Hadith which Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz permitted us from him: al-taff is that the vessel nearly fills without filling. It is said: This is taffu al-mikyal and its tifaf, when it nearly fills it, and from it is tatfif in measure, which is only its deficiency. Abu Jafar said: Then the utmost of honor after that in which the people of praiseworthy deeds and lofty choices excel, their excelling therein by their places with these deeds with the best of the creation of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His elect of His servants, and His choice for His message and conveying from Him, so that he is with his earning for himself praiseworthy matters better than others of those with the like of that due to the place with which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, described him and rewarded him thereby apart from others of the possessors of those deeds. And from it is his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The best of you in pre-Islamic ignorance are the best of you in Islam when they understand. And we mentioned that with its chains in what preceded of us in this book of ours, and in that is what was understood from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the loftiness of the rank of understanding and the majesty of the measures of its people and their loftiness over others of those lagging behind it, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"3975","555 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the six whom he cursed, including among them the one who dominates with tyranny. 3460 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: And Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Mawali informed me, from Ubaydullah ibn Mawhab, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Abu Bakr ibn Hazm, while he was the governor of Medina at that time: Write to me of the hadith of Amrah daughter of Abd al-Rahman, who was in the care of Aishah, Mother of the Believers. Ibn Mawhab said: Abu Bakr ibn Hazm sent me to Amrah daughter of Abd al-Rahman, and among what she dictated to me she said: Aishah narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Six I curse; Allah curses them, and every prophet whose supplication is answered: the adder to the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and the denier of the decree of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and the one who dominates with tyranny, humbling thereby whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, honored and honoring thereby whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, humbled, and the abandoner of my sunnah, and the violator of the sanctities of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and the violator of what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, forbade of my kindred."
"3976","3461 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Mawal narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mawhab, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Amrah daughter of Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: In the hadith of Yunus from Ibn Wahb was the hearing of Ibn Mawhab of this hadith from Amrah, while in the hadith of Ibn Abi Dawud from al-Farwi was his hearing it from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad from Amrah. And the hadith of Yunus was worthier of the two with us, for therein is mention of the dictation of Amrah thereof to him upon his coming to her with the message of Abu Bakr to her in that."
"3977","3462 - And Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mawhab, he said: I heard Ali ibn al-Husayn say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Six I cursed. Then he mentioned the six mentioned in the first two hadiths. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith was the taking of Ibn Mawhab thereof from Ali ibn al-Husayn, not from Amrah nor from others. And al-Thawri was the proof in that, worthiest that his narration therein from Ibn Mawhab be accepted due to his age, precision, and memory. Yet Ibn Abi al-Mawal mentioned the story which he mentioned therein of the sending of Abu Bakr ibn Hazm of him to Amrah in that, and the dictation of Amrah thereof to him from Aishah, so it strengthened in hearts on account of that. And it carries that Ibn Mawhab took it from Amrah as he narrated it from her, and took it along with that from Ali ibn al-Husayn as he narrated it from him of what al-Thawri mentioned from him. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, knows best the reality of the matter in that. Then we examined the text of this hadith, and what therein of mention of tyranny was derivation of that from jabriyyah, as they derived malakut from mulk. And what therein of violating the sanctuary of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is that it be made like what is besides it of His lands which He did not forbid; since He distinguished it by forbidding it from all His other lands besides it, by forbidding His servants from entering it except in ihram either for Hajj or for Umrah, and by forbidding its game, and by its granting safety to whoever enters it by His saying, Mighty and Majestic: And whoever enters it is safe, and by forbidding its thorny trees the sanctity which He did not make like the thorny trees of others, and by forbidding fighting therein one whose fighting is not obligatory; for He informed us, Mighty and Majestic, upon the tongue of His Messenger that Mecca shall not be raided after the year in which it was raided, and that no Qurashi shall be killed after that year of his in patience - meaning: its people shall not disbelieve after that year so as to be raided as they were raided in that year, nor shall a Qurashi disbelieve after that year the disbelief which made lawful the blood of its Qurashi people in that year. So whoever lowers the sanctuary to contrary of that station is thereby cursed. And His saying: and the violator of what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, forbade of my kindred. And his kindred are the people of his house who are upon his religion and upon holding to his command, like what we mentioned in what preceded of us in this book of ours of what was from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Ghadir Khumm of his saying to people: I am leaving among you the two weighty things: the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and my kindred. And of what was narrated from him in that of what we had not mentioned there."
"3978","3463 - What Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail al-Nahdi narrated to us, he said: Israil ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Mughirah, from Ali ibn Rabiah al-Asadi, he said: I met Zayd ibn Arqam while he was entering upon al-Mukhtar or leaving, and said: What hadith reached me from you: you heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I am leaving among you the two weighty things: the Book of Allah, and my kindred? He said: Yes."
"3979","3464 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Numayr al-Hamdani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl ibn Ghazwan narrated to us, he said: Abu Hayyan Yahya ibn Said ibn Hayyan al-Taymi narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Hayyan, he said: I and Husayn ibn Uqbah set out to Zayd ibn Arqam, and Husayn said to him: Allah has honored you, O Zayd; you saw much good, you saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), raided with him, and heard from him. You have attained much good, O Zayd, so narrate to us what you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Zayd said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us at a water called Khumm between Mecca and Medina, and praised Allah, Mighty and Majestic, extolled Him, and reminded. Then he said: To proceed, O people, I only await that a messenger from my Lord, Mighty and Majestic, come to me and I answer. And I am leaving among you the two weighty things: the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in it is guidance and light, so hold fast to the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and take with it. So he inclined toward the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and urged upon it. Then he said: And my household; I remind you of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, concerning my household. Abu Jafar said: We sought who narrated from Yazid ibn Hayyan besides Abu Hayyan al-Taymi, so that there be one who narrated from him besides Abu Hayyan who is like Abu Hayyan in uprightness, so that two upright ones narrated from him. Then we found that al-Amash narrated from him."
"3980","As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Hayyan, he said: Anbas ibn Uqbah would prostrate until the sparrows would land upon his back and descend, thinking it only the stump of a wall."
"3981","And what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us. Then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So al-Amash and Abu Hayyan joined in narrating from him. So whoever removes the kindred of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and upon them from the place in which Allah placed them upon the tongue of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what we mentioned in these reports, making them like others of those not of his kindred, is cursed; since he opposed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he did of that. And the rest of what is in this hadith besides that is of uncovered meanings which its hearers know what is intended thereby with knowledge which suffices us from explaining it, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"3982","556 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the hyena, of the lawfulness of eating its meat and of its unlawfulness. 3465 - Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, he said: Ismail ibn Umayyah, and Ibn Jurayj, and Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me, that Abdillah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr narrated to them, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Ammar informed me, that he asked Jabir ibn Abdillah about the hyena, saying: May I eat it? He said: Yes. I said: Is it game? He said: Yes. I said: Did you hear that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith was the taking of Yahya ibn Ayyub thereof from these three persons mentioned of his taking thereof from them therein. So we examined the reality of their narrations thereof from other than the hadith of Yahya ibn Ayyub: whether it agrees with the narration of Yahya thereof from them, or contradicts it."
"3983","3466 - Then we found that Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Abdillah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, from Ibn Abi Ammar, he said: I asked Jabir about the hyena, saying: Is it game? He said: Yes. I said: May I eat it? He said: Yes. I said: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. Abu Jafar said: So the narration of al-Thawri and Yahya of this hadith from Ismail ibn Umayyah agreed."
"3984","3467 - And we found that Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Abdillah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr narrating from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Ammar, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the hyena, and said: It is game, and he assigned therein when the pilgrim in ihram hits it a ram."
"3985","3468 - And we found that Yazid narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal, and Shayban ibn Farrukh, and Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, they said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us. Then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3986","3469 - And we found that Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3987","3470 - And we found that Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So in the narration of these of this hadith from Jarir was, apart from what is in the narration of Yahya ibn Ayyub thereof from him, mention of the permissibility of eating it; that is not in the hadiths of these. Rather in the hadiths of these is only: it is game, while it may be game yet uneaten."
"3988","3471 - And we found that Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abdillah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr informed me, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Ammar informed him, he said: I asked Jabir ibn Abdillah about the hyena, saying: May I eat it? He said: Yes. I said: Is it game? He said: Yes. I said: Did you hear that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. Abu Jafar said: What al-Bursani narrated of this hadith upon him from Ibn Jurayj agreed with what Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated from him. And we know no one who narrated this hadith from Abdillah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr from Ibn Abi Ammar besides these three persons mentioned in the hadith of Yahya ibn Ayyub. And we found Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan, in what Harun ibn Muhammad al-Asqalani permitted us from al-Alai from him, denied this hadith, saying: He used to narrate it from Jabir, from Umar, then he made it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - a denial from him thereof against Ibn Abi Ammar. And the place of Yahya in this matter is his place therein. And we examined this hadith whether anyone besides Ibn Abi Ammar narrated it."
"3989","3472 - Then we found that Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from Umar, that he judged concerning the hyena a ram."
"3990","And we found that Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik informed him from Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, decreed therein with that."
"3991","And we found that Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Awn narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from Umar, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: What Abu al-Zubayr narrated upon him of this hadith strengthened what Yahya ibn Said said therein. Then an objector said: We found it from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not from Umar; so in that was support for what Ibn Abi Ammar narrated upon him."
"3992","And he mentioned what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, and what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Saigh, from Ata, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the hyena and said: It is of game, and he assigned therein when the pilgrim in ihram hits it an aged ram, and it is eaten. So of the proof upon him for his opposition in this hadith was that Ibrahim al-Saigh - even though his place in knowledge is the place which is his place therein - was opposed in this chain by two men not below him, namely Mansur ibn Zadhdhan and Abd al-Karim ibn Malik al-Jazari."
"3993","As Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Zadhdhan, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: It was decreed concerning the hyena, when the pilgrim in ihram kills it, a ram."
"3994","And as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim ibn Malik, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: Concerning the hyena, when the pilgrim in ihram hits it, a ram. He said: In what we narrated was the opposition of Mansur ibn Zadhdhan and Abd al-Karim ibn Malik to Ibrahim al-Saigh in this hadith from Ata, their returning it to other than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his Companions. And two are worthier of memory than one, so thereby rejection of this hadith to one below the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became obligatory, not to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Ibn Abi Ammar had no concurring one upon him, while there befell him from Yahya al-Qattan what befell him. Along with that we know not that anyone narrated from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Ammar besides Abdillah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, so he was not thereby like those whom Ata opposed therein, nor Abu al-Zubayr, due to the place of Ata in knowledge and the place of Abu al-Zubayr in memory. Then we considered: has anything been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the hyena indicating its ruling in permissibility of its meat or in its forbidding."
"3995","3473 - Then we found that al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Nasr ibn Marzuq both narrated to us, they said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Majid ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade every fanged beast of prey, and every taloned bird."
"3996","3474 - And we found that Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade every fanged beast of prey, and every taloned bird."
"3997","3475 - And we found that Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya - meaning Ibn Hassan - narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"3998","3476 - And we found that Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, and from Jafar ibn Iyas, both from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he mentioned the like of it."
"3999","3477 - And we found that Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Shubah informed us, from al-Hakam, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he forbade eating every fanged beast of prey and every taloned bird. And al-Hakam raised it. Shubah said: And I dislike narrating its raising."
"4000","3478 - And we found that Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Hatim ibn Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Hibban narrated to us, he said: Abdillah informed us, from Shubah, from al-Hakam, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he forbade every fanged of beasts of prey, and every taloned of birds. He said: Then al-Hakam raised it."
"4001","3479 - And we found that Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Hakam, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating every fanged beast of prey and every taloned bird. So Ali ibn al-Hakam inserted in the chain of this hadith between Ibn Abbas and Maymun ibn Mihran Said ibn Jubayr."
"4002","3480 - And we found that Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu Thalabah al-Khushani, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade every fanged beast of prey."
"4003","3481 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik informed him from Ibn Shihab. Then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"4004","3482 - And we found that Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik narrated to him from Ismail ibn Abi Hakim, from Ubaydah ibn Sufyan al-Hadrami, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Eating every fanged beast of prey is unlawful."
"4005","3483 - And we found that Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Biraki narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade every fanged beast of prey."
"4006","3484 - And we found that Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Shababab ibn Sawwar al-Madaini narrated to us, he said: Abu Zabr Abdillah ibn al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Mishkam, scribe of Abu al-Darda, narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Thalabah al-Khushani say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: The domestic donkey is not eaten, nor every fanged beast of prey. And this was an established apparent sunnah in the hands of the scholars. The imams of the regions upon whom fatwa turns held to the forbidding of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of every fanged beast of prey without differing therein. And the hyena is fanged, so it entered into that, and it is not permissible for anyone to remove it from it. Then an objector said: How is it permissible that you accept this hadith from Ibn Abbas, when what is widespread in the hands of the scholars from Ibn Abbas is contrary to that."
"4007","3485 - And he mentioned what al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I said to Jabir ibn Zayd: They claim that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating the meat of domestic donkeys. He said: Al-Hakam ibn Amr al-Ghifari used to say that with us from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but al-Bahr - meaning Ibn Abbas - refused that, reciting: Say, I do not find in what was revealed to me forbidden to an eater who eats it. He said: In this hadith is what indicates that whatever is outside what is in this verse of what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, mentioned His forbidding thereof, its eating is lawful. Our answer in that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that the matter in that is as mentioned by the apparent verse, except that Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, when he stood upon the forbidding of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, upon the tongue of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what He forbade of the fanged of beasts of prey and the taloned of birds, knew that it is excepted from what was permitted by this verse, attached to what was forbidden by it. And likewise whoever was besides him of those below him - namely al-Zuhri - said in what Abu Idris narrated to him from Abu Thalabah of the forbidding of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of every fanged beast of prey: We had not heard of this until we entered Sham - meaning: then we heard it and took with it. So this was of what was with Ibn Shihab in Medina, yet knowledge of it escaped him, as we mentioned from Malik, from Ismail ibn Abi Hakim from Ubaydah ibn Sufyan, from Abu Hurayrah. And those besides them stood upon the forbidding of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) along with that of every taloned bird, so they took with that. And every party of them in what they were upon thereof was praiseworthy for holding to the Book of Allah, the Exalted, and to what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed them of what He excepted of what is in His Book in general."
"4008","3486 - As Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Idris, from Abu Thalabah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade every fanged of beasts. Al-Zuhri said: I had not heard this hadith until we came to Sham, and Allah we ask for success."
"4009","557 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as proof of what is intended by the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: And forbidden to you is the game of the land as long as you are in ihram. Abu Jafar said: We mentioned in the chapter before this chapter the hadith of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Ammar which we mentioned therein, and mentioned with that what befell it of what Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan said therein, and what was narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and from Jabir ibn Abdillah concerning the hyena that therein is a sheep, and mentioned with that the entering of the hyena into what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade of the fanged of beasts of prey, and that thereby it became obligatory that it is uneaten, and in what we mentioned of that that it is forbidden. Our need for what we mention in this chapter, if Allah wills, was what the people of knowledge differed in of what is intended by the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: And forbidden to you is the game of the land as long as you are in ihram. Al-Muzani narrated to us in that from al-Shafii that this verse indicated to him that what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, forbade His servants in their ihram of game is what was lawful for them to eat in their state of non-ihram. And Ibn Abi Imran would narrate to us in that of what he mentions from his companions, and of what he selected of their saying: that what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, forbade people in their ihram of game is what they would hunt to eat, and what they would hunt thereof with their hunting animals of dogs and what is besides them of what they feed them thereof, and of what eating it is unlawful upon them like wolves and what resembles them of fanged beasts of prey and taloned birds. And he says: This entered into what was forbidden for the pilgrim in ihram to hunt in his ihram. What Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us of that was with us worthier for interpreting the verse we recited; for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said therein: And forbidden to you is the game of the land as long as you are in ihram, so He generalized thereby all game, eaten and uneaten. Yet Ibn Abi Imran had followed that with a proof he argued therewith, saying: We saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Five of beasts are killed in the sanctuary and in ihram: the crow, the kite, the scorpion, the mouse, and the vicious dog. The narrations in that are of what we are sufficed from mentioning their chains due to the agreement of the two parties we mentioned upon them. Ibn Abi Imran said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) limited that with a known number, we understood thereby that there is nothing in what he permitted the pilgrim in ihram to kill in his ihram which goes beyond that number to others. Abu Jafar said: This proof was with us unsound; for it is permissible that these five be of what was permitted for the pilgrim in ihram to kill in his ihram, while with them is what was permitted for him to kill in his ihram of their kinds besides them; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only mentioned in that hadith a number for what he mentioned thereby, and did not say therein that nothing enters into what was permitted for the pilgrim in ihram to kill in his ihram of game besides that number. So it is permissible that that number entered into it, and numbers besides it of its kinds entered into it. And we found the like of that of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned with a meaning whose mention was repeated thereby, then he mentioned in another hadith of that kind with a meaning other than that number. 3487 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Shayban - meaning al-Nahwi - narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Sulayman ibn Mushir, from Kharashah ibn al-Hurr, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three Allah shall not speak to on the Day of Resurrection, nor purify them, and for them is a painful punishment: one who gives nothing except with reminder of favor, and one who trails his lower garment dragging it, and one who promotes his goods with false oath. He said: He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in this hadith these three with what he mentioned them thereby, then we found him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mention three others with that meaning in another hadith."
"4010","3488 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath al-Nakhai narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three Allah shall not look at on the Day of Resurrection, nor purify them, and for them is a painful punishment - I do not know with which of them to begin: a man upon surplus water on the road withholding it from the traveler, and a man who swore over goods after Asr that he took it for such and such, so the one who sold it to him believed him and he took it while lying, and a man who pledged to a leader pledging him only for worldly gain; if he gives him he fulfills, and if he does not give him he does not fulfill. Then he recited the verse in Al Imran. Abu Jafar said: His mentioning the three whom he mentioned in the first hadith, and limiting them with the number by which he limited them therein, does not negate that there be there three besides them of the people of the meaning by which he mentioned them therein. And we found him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mention three others also, that they are of the people of the meaning by which he mentioned these three whom he mentioned in this hadith, apart from the three whom he mentioned in the hadith we mentioned before it."
"4011","3489 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shayban informed us, from al-Amash, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three Allah, Mighty and Majestic, shall not speak to on the Day of Resurrection, nor purify them, and for them is a painful punishment: an old adulterer, and a lying king, and a poor arrogant one. Abu Jafar said: And this Abu Hazim is al-Ashjai; his wala is to a woman of Ashja called Azzah. And all of those from whom hadith is narrated of those with this kunyah: this Abu Hazim whose name is Salman, counted among the Kufans; and Abu Hazim Salamah ibn Dinar, freedman of Abdillah ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabiah, counted among the Medinans; and Abu Hazim al-Tammar from whom Yahya ibn Said al-Ansari narrates, a freedman of Banu Ghifar, counted among the Medinans."
"4012","3490 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three Allah shall not look at: the old adulterer, and the lying leader, and the poor vain one."
"4013","3491 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three Allah shall not look at on the Day of Resurrection: the old adulterer, and the lying king, and the poor vain one. So what was mentioned in each hadith of these hadiths, that whoever was mentioned therein is of the kind mentioned therein that he is of its people, even though limited therein with a known number, does not negate that there be in that kind others like what the five which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in the hadith with which Ibn Abi Imran argued do not prevent that there be there of what enters into that meaning along with those five others. Yet there enters for him in that upon us that he say: I attached to each three of the threes mentioned in these hadiths others of what was mentioned in the rest of these hadiths, due to the mentioning of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thereof. Had I found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mention of others than the five mentioned in the hadith with which I argued, I would have attached them to them, but I did not find it, so I attached nothing to them. Then we say to him: What was your need to negate thereby others of what was not known that they negated. Then we say, arguing for his school in that: We found Allah, the Exalted, said in His Book: And forbidden to you is the game of the land as long as you are in ihram. So the apparent of this verse was upon the entering of all land game, and that it generalized it all with forbidding in the state of ihram. And it is not permissible that anything of what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, generalized with the like of this be removed except by what obligates its removal thereby, of a written verse, or a transmitted sunnah, or a consensus of the nation, that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, did not intend thereby that thing, but intended what is besides it. And when we lack that, we remove not from what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, forbade with that verse except what was agreed upon its removal thereof, namely the five in the hadith with which Ibn Abi Imran argued, nothing besides them, and Allah we ask for success."
"4014","558 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of the sound of what the people of knowledge differ in its time of the day of sacrifice in which the Jamrat al-Aqabah is stoned whose stoning therein suffices: whether it is before sunrise or after its sunrise, with what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that. 3492 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith informed us, from Shubah, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not stone the jamrah until the sun rises."
"4015","3493 - And Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Harun al-Burdi narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-Amash, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us with the weak of Banu Hashim upon donkeys, saying: O my sons, pour forth, and do not stone the jamrah until the sun rises."
"4016","3494 - And Yahya narrated to us, he said: al-Burdi informed us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4017","3495 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated to us, from al-Numan ibn Thabit Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent the weak of his family by night from Jam, saying to them: Do not stone the jamrah until the sun rises."
"4018","3496 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-Amash, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by us on the night of sacrifice while darkness of night was upon us, striking our thighs and saying: O my little sons, pour forth, and do not stone the jamrah until the sun rises."
"4019","3497 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Amash, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Banu Hashim: O sons of my brother, hasten before the crowding of people, and do not stone the jamrah until the sun rises."
"4020","3498 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Mahmud ibn Ghaylan narrated to us. 3499 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Mahmud ibn Ghaylan informed us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent his family ahead, commanding them not to stone the jamrah until the sun rises."
"4021","3500 - And al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us. 3501 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Misar ibn Kidam, then they agreed, saying: from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from al-Hasan al-Urani - in the hadith of Husayn, from Ibn Abbas, and in the hadith of Rawh he said: Ibn Abbas said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) carried us, the young boys of Banu Abd al-Muttalib, upon donkeys, then he began striking our thighs. In the hadith of Rawh: O my sons. And in the hadith of Husayn: Ubayniyya, do not stone Jamrat al-Aqabah until the sun rises."
"4022","3502 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Yazid informed us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from al-Hasan al-Urani, from Ibn Abbas, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Husayn exactly. Abu Jafar said: All these reports are of uncovered meanings, with the forbidding of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to those he hastened from Jam: that they not stone the jamrah until the sun rises. And when this is the ruling of one who has the concession to hasten from there, one who has no concession in that is worthier of that forbidding."
"4023","3503 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Sulayman al-Numayri narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Kurayb informed us, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command his women and his baggage on the morning of Jam to pour forth with the first dawn in darkness, and not to stone the jamrah except in the morning. Abu Jafar said: The correction of this hadith and what we mentioned before it of the hadiths in this chapter is upon forbidding stoning Jamrat al-Aqabah on the day of sacrifice until the sun rises. Then an objector said: We know no one of the people of knowledge upon whom fatwa turns except he departed from this hadith, holding that whoever stones Jamrat al-Aqabah on the day of sacrifice before sunrise, his stoning suffices, and he need not repeat it after that when the sun rises - among them Abu Hanifah among his companions, among them Malik among his companions, and among them al-Shafii among his companions; indeed he exceeded them, mentioning that whoever stones it on the day of sacrifice after midnight, his stoning suffices. He said: So this hadith is of what the scholars received with rejection, so there was no meaning to your mentioning it. Our answer to him in that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that the scholars did not receive this hadith with rejection as he mentioned; rather those we mentioned of them opposed it, while among them are those who clung to it and held it, namely al-Awzai and al-Thawri, both of imamate in knowledge and place therein like those who opposed that of them."
"4024","As Muhammad ibn Sinan permitted us, from Mahmud ibn Khalid, from Umar ibn Abd al-Wahid, he said: I heard al-Awzai say concerning a man who departed after alighting at Muzdalifah by night, proceeding as he was until he stoned the jamrah and sacrificed: he said: As for the command, he should not sacrifice until the sun rises; but if he does, it suffices him. He said: As for his saying: as for the command, he should not sacrifice until the sun rises, it is as he said. As for his saying: but if he does, it suffices him, he is required therein with the like of what those we mentioned before him are required therein."
"4025","And as Muhammad ibn Jafar known as Ibn al-Imam narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Musa al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan, asked about one who stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah before sunrise, saying: He repeats the stoning. So what Sufyan said of this was worthier than what was said in this chapter; for no one may depart from what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, nor from what he did, nor from what he timed. And when he timed in sacrifice on the day of sacrifice a specified time, so that whoever precedes it his sacrifice does not suffice him and he is commanded with repetition, likewise in his commanding the pilgrim with stoning therein for a specified time, he may not depart from it by preceding it to another; and if he precedes it and stones before it, he is commanded with repeating the stoning therein. This is the view with us in this chapter, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"4026","559 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from Ibn Abbas and from Jabir in their saying: We do not know with how many the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned the jamrah of pebbles, then what others besides them narrated of what contains mention of the number of what he stoned it thereby. Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: 3504 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, he said: I heard Abu Mijlaz say: I asked Ibn Abbas about something of the matter of the jamarat, and he said: I do not know; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned it with six or seven."
"4027","3505 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Shubah, from Qatadah, from Abu Mijlaz, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about stoning the jamarat, and he said: By Allah, I do not know with how many the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned, with six or with seven."
"4028","3506 - And al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Salim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdillah say: I do not know with how many the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned."
"4029","3507 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir say: I do not know with how many the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned. Abu Jafar said: We examined the hadith of Ibn Abbas in that, whether a known number was narrated of the pebbles with which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned the jamrah."
"4030","3508 - Then we found that Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Manazil al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from his grandfather Ali ibn Husayn, from Abdillah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas, he said: I was riding behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah with seven pebbles, saying takbir with each pebble of them. So we understood thereby that Ibn Abbas only reported that in the first hadith from his own knowledge, then reported in the second hadith the reality of the number of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned it thereby, namely seven pebbles."
"4031","3509 - And we found that al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, that he heard Jabir ibn Abdillah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Pilgrimage came to the jamrah by the tree and stoned it with seven pebbles, saying takbir with each pebble, like the pebbles of khadhf, stoning from the bottom of the valley, then departed. So it carried concerning Jabir ibn Abdillah in what we narrated from him the like of what we established in what we narrated from Ibn Abbas, of what he did not stand upon the reality of its number, while he stood upon it through others. And a people clung to the two hadiths of Ibn Abbas and Jabir which we narrated at the beginning of this chapter, permitting thereby the pilgrim to stone the jamrah with whatever pebbles he wishes without a number intended, short of seven or beyond it. And they mentioned in that two men of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4032","What Fahd narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf, freedman of Amr ibn Uthman, informed me, that Abdillah ibn Amr ibn Uthman informed him, that he heard Abu Hibbah al-Ansari say: There is no harm in whatever pebbles a man stones the jamrah thereby, meaning of their number. Then Abdillah ibn Amr - they claimed - came to Abdillah ibn Umar and said: Abu Hibbah al-Ansari gives fatwa to people that there is no harm in whatever pebbles a man stones therewith of the pebbles of the jamrah, meaning of their number. Ibn Umar said: Abu Hibbah spoke the truth. And Abu Hibbah is of the people of Badr. And they mentioned in that also."
"4033","3510 - What Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from Hajjaj, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We came with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his pilgrimage; among us were those who stoned with seven and more and less, and he did not criticize that upon us."
"4034","3511 - And what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Musa informed us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, he said: Mujahid said: Sad said: We returned in the pilgrimage with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), some of us saying: I stoned with seven, and some of us saying: I stoned with six, and they did not criticize one another. Abu Jafar said: What is in this hadith contradicts what is in the hadith before it; for in the hadith before it is what necessitates joining it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while what is in this hadith does not necessitate that. And this hadith is firmer than the first hadith; for the one who narrated the first hadith from Ibn Abi Najih is al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, and he did not mention it as hearing, while what al-Hajjaj does not mention as hearing, they impugn it. As for the second hadith, it is of the hadith of Ibn Uyaynah, the firmest of people concerning Ibn Abi Najih. Then we examined what was narrated of the pebbles with which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned the jamarat from others than Sad and Ibn Abbas and Jabir."
"4035","3512 - Then we found that Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he came to the first jamrah by the mosque of Mina, would stone it with seven pebbles, saying takbir whenever he threw a pebble. Then he would advance before it, stand facing the House raising his hands supplicating, prolonging the standing. Then he would come to the second jamrah and stone it with seven pebbles, saying takbir whenever he threw a pebble. Then he would descend to the left of what adjoins the valley, stand at al-Aqabah, and stone it with seven pebbles, saying takbir whenever he threw a pebble, then depart without standing at it. Al-Zuhri said: I heard Salim ibn Abdillah narrating this from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4036","3513 - And we found that Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, from his brother, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, would stone the lowest jamrah with seven pebbles, saying takbir after each pebble. Then he would advance, go to level ground, stand facing the qiblah a long standing, supplicating Allah, Mighty and Majestic, raising his hands. Then he would stone the middle likewise, taking to the left, going to level ground, standing facing the qiblah a long standing, supplicating Allah and raising his hands. Then he would stone the Jamrat al-Aqabah from the bottom of the valley without standing at it, saying: Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do."
"4037","3514 - And we found that Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Humayd and Abdillah ibn Said al-Ashajj narrated to us, they said: Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) poured forth from the end of his day, then came to Mina, remaining there the nights of Mina, the days of tashriq, stoning the jamarat when the sun declined, each jamrah with seven pebbles saying takbir with each pebble, standing at the first and the second prolonging the standing and supplicating humbly, then stoning the third - meaning Jamrat al-Aqabah - without standing at it."
"4038","3515 - And we found that Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Sulayman ibn Amr ibn al-Ahwas, from his mother, she said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stone the jamrah with seven pebbles, then depart. Abu Jafar said: In these reports was the stoning of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of each jamrah of these three jamarat of pebbles with a known number, as his circumambulation of the House in his pilgrimage was known circuits, and as his running between al-Safa and al-Marwah was known circuits. And he said with that: Let my nation take their rites; for I do not know, perhaps I shall not meet you after this year of mine."
"4039","3516 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to me that he heard Jabir say: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of sacrifice sacrifice upon his mount, saying: Take your rites; for I do not know, perhaps I shall not perform Hajj after this pilgrimage of mine. And that was from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) so that they follow his traces, being in what they do in their pilgrimage followers complying with his acts, not departing from them to addition upon them nor to deficiency from them. And as the circuits we mentioned do not admit exceeding them nor falling short of them in their number, likewise the pebbles with which the jamarat are stoned in Hajj in their number do not admit exceeding their count with which he stoned them nor falling short of it to what is below it, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"4040","560 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what was from him in his pilgrimage of his commanding Umm Salamah his wife to meet with him the dawn prayer on the day of sacrifice at Mecca. 3517 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus al-Thalabi known as al-Susi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah Muhammad ibn Khazim al-Darir narrated to us, from Hisham - meaning Ibn Urwah - from his father, from Zaynab, from Umm Salamah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her to meet the forenoon with him at Mecca on the day of sacrifice. Abu Jafar said: Al-Shafii argued, as al-Muzani narrated to us from him, with this hadith, saying therein what indicates that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted her to depart from Jam before dawn; for it is not possible that that be from her, along with her meeting Mecca in the forenoon, except that she departed from Jam before dawn, due to the distance between Mecca and Jam. And in that is what indicates that she stoned the jamrah before dawn. Abu Jafar said: This is a saying we know no one of the people of knowledge besides him said, nor held. All of them are contrary to him therein, upon that no pilgrim may stone Jamrat al-Aqabah in the night before dawn. So we examined this hadith and found it only turned with this meaning upon Abu Muawiyah, and found that Abu Muawiyah was inconsistent therein, narrating it once as we mentioned, and narrating it another time."
"4041","3518 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Zaynab bint Abi Salamah, from Umm Salamah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her on the day of sacrifice to meet with him the dawn prayer at Mecca. Abu Jafar said: This is contrary to what is in the hadith of Muhammad ibn Amr from Abu Muawiyah; for in this is his commanding her on the day of sacrifice to meet with him the dawn prayer at Mecca, so this is upon that he commanded her on the day of sacrifice with this for the day after the day of sacrifice."
"4042","3519 - And Abdillah ibn Suwayd al-Baghdadi mentioned to me from al-Athram, from Ahmad ibn Hanbal in a book he handed to me and permitted me from al-Athram - and he narrated to me that al-Athram corrected it for him and permitted it for whoever copied it from him, so I copied it - and therein was: from Ahmad ibn Hanbal, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, from Zaynab, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her to meet him on the day of sacrifice at Mecca. Abu Jafar said: In that book joined to this hadith is: Abu Abdillah Ahmad ibn Hanbal said: None connected it besides him - meaning Abu Muawiyah - and it is an error. He said: And Waki said: from Hisham, from his father, mursal: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her to meet the dawn prayer on the day of sacrifice at Mecca, or the like of this. Abu Abdillah said: This too is strange; what would the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do at Mecca on the day of sacrifice?! - denying that. Abu Abdillah said: So I came to Yahya ibn Said and asked him. He said: From Hisham, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her to meet, not to meet him. He said: Between these two is a difference; on the day of sacrifice the dawn prayer is at al-Abtah. He said: And Yahya said to me: Ask Abd al-Rahman. So I asked him, and he said: Thus from Sufyan, from Hisham, from his father: tuwafi. Al-Athram said: Then Abu Abdillah said to me: May Allah have mercy on Yahya, how precise he was and how scrutinizing; he was a muhaddith - and he praised him with fine praise. Abu Jafar said: This is sound speech by which the corruption of this hadith becomes obligatory. Then we sought it from other than the hadith of Abu Muawiyah."
"4043","3520 - Then we found that Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her to pray Fajr at Mecca on the day of sacrifice. Abu Jafar said: He did not mention therein between Urwah and Umm Salamah anyone, and this is disconnected; for we know no hearing of Urwah from Umm Salamah. And this too is other than what is in the hadith of Abu Muawiyah; for what is therein is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her to pray Fajr at Mecca on the day of sacrifice, not with him, but alone."
"4044","3521 - And we found that Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Urwah, that the day of Umm Salamah came around to the day of sacrifice, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her on the night of Jam to pour forth, and she stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah and prayed Fajr at Mecca."
"4045","3522 - And we found that Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that the day of Umm Salamah came around to the day of sacrifice, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her, and she stoned the jamrah and prayed Fajr at Mecca. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is its disconnection after Urwah, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her on the night of Jam to pour forth, and she stoned the jamrah and prayed Fajr at Mecca. So it carries that her stoning the jamrah in the time in which she stoned it may have been without his commanding her therewith. And what he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended from her in her stoning Jamrat al-Aqabah may be what he intended from others of the weak of his family: that they stone it after sunrise, as we narrated from him before this chapter in that. Then we examined this hadith also."
"4046","3523 - Then we found that Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Umm Salamah to pray Subh on the day of nafr at Mecca, it being her day, wanting that she meet him."
"4047","3524 - And we found that Jabr ibn Said al-Hadrami wrote to me narrating to me from Muhammad ibn Khallad al-Iskandarani, that he narrated to him, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Umm Salamah to meet him on the day of nafr at Mecca. Abu Jafar said: In this is contrary to what is in what preceded of this story in the chain and in the text together; for this in its chain returns to Aishah, not to Umm Salamah, and because its text intends the time in which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Umm Salamah to meet him therein at Mecca as the day of nafr, not the day of sacrifice. And we mentioned in the chapter of the number of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned of pebbles in his stoning Jamrat al-Aqabah, in what preceded of us in this book of ours, that the pouring forth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Mecca was only at the end of the day of sacrifice. So in that is what indicates contrary to what is in this hadith with which we began mentioning, of the hadith of Abu Muawiyah in the story of Umm Salamah."
"4048","3525 - And what Yazid ibn Sinan also narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Tariq narrated to me, from Tawus. And Abu al-Zubayr, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and Ibn Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed Tawaf al-Ziyarah to the night. So in this is what indicates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had no need to meet Umm Salamah with him on the day of sacrifice at Mecca. And in that is what indicates the corruption of the hadith of Abu Muawiyah which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"4049","561 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the slap: is there retaliation therein or not? 3526 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Israil ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abd al-Ala, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas: that a man of the Ansar reviled a father of al-Abbas who was in pre-Islamic ignorance, so al-Abbas slapped him. His people came and said: By Allah, we shall slap him as he slapped him. They wore arms. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he ascended the pulpit and said: O people, which people of the earth are most honored with Allah? They said: You. He said: Al-Abbas is from me and I am from him, so do not revile our dead lest you harm our living. The people came and said: O Messenger of Allah, we seek refuge with Allah from your anger; seek forgiveness for us. Then an objector said: In this hadith is that the people of the slapped sought retaliation from the slap which was from al-Abbas to their companion, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not disapprove that of them. So in that is what indicates the obligatoriness of retaliation in the slap, while you do not say that in your entirety, nor the people of Medina besides you."
"4050","And he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik said: There is no retaliation in the slap, for its limit is not known. He said: In that is what indicates your departing from this hadith not to a like hadith. Our answer to him in that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that we neither departed from this hadith nor abandoned it. It is not a proof upon us in our repelling retaliation from the slap; rather it is a proof for us in that. For had retaliation therein been obligatory, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have permitted its taking from whom it was due upon for whom it was due, nor would the majesty of the station of whom it was due upon have prevented him from that, just as it did not prevent him from Fatimah - who is closer to him than al-Abbas - by his saying: By Allah, had Fatimah stolen, I would have cut her hand. But he did not see the slap which was necessitating anything, so he therefore abandoned taking anything thereby from al-Abbas for the one to whom it was from him. And it is understood in jurisprudence itself that whoever takes something deliberately whose taking necessitates something upon him, when he takes it undeliberately there becomes obligatory upon him in his taking thereof something, either its like or another: of that, if a man destroys for a man wealth by mistake from him, upon him for him is its like if it has a like, or its value if it has no like; and had he killed him deliberately, retaliation would be obligatory upon him, and had he killed him by mistake, blood-money would be obligatory upon him. So likewise what we mentioned of the slap which neither wounded nor left a trace in the face of the slapped: there is nothing therein when that is by mistake; likewise when it is deliberate, there is nothing therein. And for this meaning - and Allah knows best - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) abandoned taking for the one whom al-Abbas slapped from al-Abbas anything of retaliation or others for his slapping him. Then he said: You have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4051","3527 - Then he mentioned what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya informed us, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Wahid al-Makki, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, from Abdillah ibn Unays, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says on the Day of Resurrection: It is not fitting for anyone of the people of Paradise to enter Paradise while anyone of the people of Fire has a wrong with him, nor is it fitting for anyone of the people of Fire to enter Fire while anyone of the people of Paradise has a wrong with him, until I retaliate him from him, even the slap. We said: And how, when we only come to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, naked, uncircumcised, voiceless? He said: With good deeds and evil deeds. He said: In this hadith is that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, takes in the Hereafter the slap for whoever was slapped it in the world from whoever slapped him it therein. And in that is what indicates the obligatoriness of that upon him for him in the world. Our answer to him in that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that there is no proof upon us in this hadith either; since it carries that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, may have lifted from the slapper in the world that there be upon him in his slap in the world anything of retaliation or others for the one whom he slapped it; since its limit is unachievable, and the valuation therein is unachievable, so He lifted that from him in the world. And He, Mighty and Majestic, in the Hereafter is capable of standing upon its limit; since in the Hereafter He undertakes judgment therein, while the undertaker of judgment therein is others of His servants of those uncapable of the like of that thereof. Then an objector said: We found from more than one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what necessitates retaliation in the slap."
"4052","Then he mentioned what Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Husayn al-Ahmasi, he said: I heard Tariq ibn Shihab say: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, slapped a man. They said: By Allah, we never saw like today at all; he was not content to withhold him until he slapped him. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: He came to me asking me to carry him, so I carried him. Then he came to me asking me to carry him, so I carried him. Then he came to me asking me to carry him, so I carried him, and then he sells it. So I swore not to carry him. Then he said: By Allah, I shall carry him, then by Allah, I shall carry him, then by Allah, I shall carry him. Then he said: Retaliate from me, and the other pardoned him."
"4053","And what Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, from Shubah, from Yahya ibn Husayn al-Ahmasi, he said: I heard Tariq ibn Shihab say: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq slapped a man. They said: He was not content to withhold him until he slapped him. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said to the man: Retaliate from me, and the man pardoned him. Our answer in that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that it carries that Abu Bakr permitted that of himself not as obligatory upon him, but as humility from him and dislike of what was from him of exalting himself over others by slapping him."
"4054","And he mentioned what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Mukhariq, from Tariq, he said: Khalid ibn al-Walid was in al-Jazirah when a nephew of his slapped a man. The uncle of the man said: Allah only favored Quraysh with prophethood. So Khalid ibn al-Walid retaliated him from him, and he pardoned him. Abu Jafar said: This too may have been from Khalid as humility and discipline from him for his nephew, deterring him from repeating that. And there was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then from Umar after him."
"4055","3528 - What Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Yahya al-Hamdani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: al-Jurayri informed us, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Firas, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: By Allah, I do not send my workers to you to strike your skins and take your wealth; rather I only sent them to teach you your religion and your sunnah. So whoever is done otherwise, let him raise it to me; by Allah, I shall retaliate him from him. Amr ibn al-As said: O Commander of the Believers, if a man is over a group and disciplines some of his subjects, do you retaliate from him? He said: By Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Umar, I shall retaliate from him. I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) retaliate from himself. Then he said: Do not strike the Muslims lest you humiliate them, nor withhold their rights lest you disbelieve them, nor keep them long in campaigns lest you tempt them, nor settle them in thickets lest you waste them. Abu Jafar said: This was with us also from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as humility from him, not as obligatory. And what was of Umar was discipline for whoever he threatened therewith and warning him from doing what he takes from him as discipline what he threatened him with taking it from him, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"4056","562 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning what was from the bedouin to him in pulling his cloak upon his neck until it reddened it, and his seeking retaliation therein. 3529 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Maslamah ibn Qanab narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Hilal narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We would sit with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque; when he stood, we stood. One day he stood and we stood with him, until when he reached the middle of the mosque a bedouin caught him and pulled with his cloak from behind. His cloak was coarse, so it reddened his neck. He said: O Muhammad, load for me upon these two camels of mine, for you load not for me from your wealth nor from the wealth of your father. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall not load for you until you retaliate me for what you pulled with my neck. The bedouin said: By Allah, I shall not retaliate you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that to him three times, each time saying: By Allah, I shall not retaliate you. When we heard the saying of the bedouin, we hastened to him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned and said: I adjure whoever heard my speech not to leave his place until I permit him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man of the people: Load for him barley upon a camel, and dates upon a camel. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Disperse. Abu Jafar said: An objector said: Whence was retaliation in the like of what was mentioned in this hadith wide for you, until you all opposed it not to a like hadith. Our answer to him in that, by the guidance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, was that it carries that the retaliation which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought from that bedouin was not upon what he imagined of qisas; rather it was upon returning humble by offering to him of himself the like of what he did, until he be thereby upon the like of what the people of Islam are upon in humility at the like of this - as was of the humility of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Umar which we mentioned, then of the humility of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, which we narrated in the chapter before this chapter. And his mentioning retaliation was metaphorical, as the Arabs borrow the word for the meaning which is therein of what they borrowed it thereof. Of that is their saying: So-and-so shed the lifeblood of so-and-so - not that the lifeblood is shed; rather the shed is blood, and that is much in the speech of the Arabs, until it rose to the coming of the Quran therewith, namely what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, described in the story of Musa and his companion, blessings of Allah upon them both, of His saying: They found therein a wall wanting to collapse, so he set it upright. So He mentioned it with will, while the wall has no will; rather there was from it what is from those with will at their willing to cast themselves to the ground. So likewise what he intended from the bedouin was that he offer to him of himself the like of what is offered by retaliation. And in what we mentioned is what indicates that there is no proof for this interpreter upon us in what he argued with us thereof of this interpretation of his, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for success."
"4057","563 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying concerning the mawali: They shall fight you upon this religion in return as you fought them upon it at the beginning. 3530 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah informed us, from Sulayman - meaning al-Amash - from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Abbad ibn Abdillah, he said: Ali, upon him be peace, addressed us while Sasaah ibn Suhan was present upon a pulpit of bricks. A man came stepping over the necks of people until he spoke to him of something. He repelled him, and I do not know what he said to him. Then al-Ashath ibn Qays came stepping over the necks of people until he drew near him and said: O Commander of the Believers, these red ones have overcome us before you - meaning the mawali. Sasaah ibn Suhan struck upon my back and said: He shall disclose of the affair of the Arabs a matter he had concealed. Then he said: Who excuses me from these potbellied ones: one of them turns upon his bedding, while people abandon for the remembrance of Allah; you command me to drive them away, so I would be of the wrongdoers. By Him Who split the grain and created the souls, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: They shall strike you upon religion in return as you struck them at the beginning."
"4058","3531 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath al-Nakhai narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, he said: al-Minhal narrated to me, from Abbad al-Asadi, that he narrated to him, he said: While Ali, upon him be peace, was addressing us one Friday upon a pulpit of bricks, with Zayd ibn Suhan behind me, a man was seen stepping over the necks of people until he drew near and spoke something. Ali, upon him be peace, became severely angry until it was seen in his face. Then al-Ashath ibn Qays came stepping over the necks of people until he drew near and said: These red ones have overcome us before you. Ali became angry, his anger intensified. Then he said: Who excuses me from these potbellied ones: they recline upon their beds, while people go to the remembrance of Allah, Mighty and Majestic; they command me to drive them away, so I would be of the ignorant. By Him Who split the grain and created the souls, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: They shall strike you upon religion in return as you struck them upon it at the beginning. Then Zayd struck upon my shoulder and said: The Commander of the Believers shall disclose upon the Arabs today a matter he concealed. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith to stand upon what is intended by what is therein, if Allah wills. What therein of mention of the red ones intends thereby the mawali, and of it is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4059","3532 - Of what al-Muzani narrated to me, he said: Al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was given five which none before me was given: the earth was made for me a mosque and purification, I was aided with terror, spoils were made lawful for me, I was sent to the red and the white, and I was given intercession. Al-Muzani said to us: Al-Shafii said: Then I sat to Sufyan, and he mentioned this hadith; then al-Zuhri said from Abu Salamah or Said, from Abu Hurayrah, then he mentioned it. And therein of the dayatirah mentioned therein is that intended thereby are those who attend markets with no wealth with them, attending therewith the markets and benefiting thereby in their attending them. Whoever attends them likewise is as one who did not attend them; so like him is whoever attends others without benefit in his attending what he attends. And the singular of al-dayatirah is daytar. Then we examined what is in this hadith of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which we mentioned therein from him. The Arabs at the beginning are those who fought the non-Arabs until they admitted them into Islam, as was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that."
"4060","3533 - Of what al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Kathir ibn Abi al-Ayan, he said: Abu al-Tufayl narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed until he was astonished, saying: Will you not ask me what I laughed from? They said: What did you laugh from, O Messenger of Allah? He said: I marvel at people led to Paradise in chains while reluctant from it; what makes it hateful to them. They said: And how, O Messenger of Allah? He said: People of the non-Arabs whom the Emigrants take captive to admit them into Islam while hating."
"4061","3534 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: al-Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Kathir ibn A'yan Abu Muhammad, he said: Abu al-Tufayl narrated to me at Makkah in the year 107, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed until he laughed loudly and heartily, then he mentioned the like thereof."
"4062","3535 - And what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, he said: Kathir Abu Muhammad informed me, Abu al-Tufayl narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed, then said: Will you not ask Me from what I laughed? Then he mentioned the like thereof. So the Arabs were those who brought the non-Arabs into Islam until they became of its people, and until there came to be among them those who knew and understood from Allah Almighty and from His Messenger the laws of His religion, until there passed to them the calling of whoever departed from what he was upon of it to its opposite to return to what he had left of it; so that was their fighting them for return, that they return to what they had abandoned of it, like the Arabs had fought them at first over what they fought them until they brought them by that into what they brought them into. And it is possible that he meant by the non-Arabs those whom he described with seeking knowledge, such that he said concerning them: If religion were at the Pleiades, or if knowledge were at the Pleiades, men from the sons of Persia would attain it. So we looked: has there been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that or not?"
"4063","3536 - So we found Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Qaza'ah narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Sulayman al-Numayri narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Yahya al-Aslami narrated to us, from al-Abbas ibn Sahl ibn Sa'd, from his father Sahl ibn Sa'd, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Day of the Trench; he took the pickaxe and dug with it, and struck a rock, and laughed, and was asked: What made you laugh, O Messenger of Allah? He said: From people who will be brought from the direction of the East in fetters, driven to Paradise while they are unwilling. So we understood by that that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only meant by the non-Arabs in what he said in the hadith before this, the non-Arabs who were in the direction of the East, and they are the sons of Persia who entered into the description he described in the other hadith concerning seeking knowledge and religion, and they entered into the saying of Allah Almighty: And others of them who have not yet joined them, i.e. they will join those mentioned at the beginning of the surah, and it is His Almighty saying: He it is Who sent among the unlettered a Messenger from among them. And with Allah Almighty is success."
"4064","564 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the she-camel whose owner cursed her, from his saying to her: Leave her, for she is cursed. 3537 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed me, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a woman cursed her she-camel, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take your goods from her, for she is cursed. Imran said: So it is as if I am looking at her, a grey she-camel. So a questioner asked about the meaning by which the owner of this she-camel was commanded to abandon her due to her cursing her."
"4065","3538 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Taymi informed us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Abu Barzah: that a slave girl, while she was upon a camel or a riding mount upon which was some of the people's goods, came to a mountain and the mountain narrowed upon her; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon her and she saw him, and she began saying: Hal, O Allah curse him; Hal, O Allah curse him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is the owner of the slave girl? Let no camel or riding mount upon which is a curse from Allah accompany us, or as he said. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah Almighty and His aid, was: that cursing in the speech of the Arabs is expulsion and distancing, and from it is the saying of Allah Almighty: Those are they whom Allah curses and the cursers curse them. So the curse of Allah Almighty upon them was His expelling them from Him and distancing them from Him."
"4066","As Wallad the grammarian narrated to us, he said: al-Masadiri narrated to us, from Abu Ubaydah Ma'mar ibn al-Muthanna: May Allah curse them, i.e. may Allah expel them and distance them; it is said: an outcast wolf, i.e. expelled. Shammakh ibn Dirar said. So her saying that, meaning may Allah curse her she-camel, i.e. may Allah expel her and distance her by way of supplication from her against her by that; so it is possible that that coincided from her with a time in which Allah Almighty grants His giving, so when that woman asked Him that concerning her she-camel, He answered her in her, so she became by it cursed, i.e. expelled and distanced, not for a meaning of punishment that befell the she-camel, since she had no sin in what her owner did concerning her; and the punishment in that and the blame for it returned upon the woman from whom the curse came. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prevented a she-camel which Allah Almighty had made expelled from accompanying him; and in that was prevention of her owner from benefiting by her henceforth due to Allah Almighty answering her in her by what she supplicated against her; and when she became expelled by Allah Almighty, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prevented her accompanying him, because her accompanying him is opposite to the expulsion which Allah Almighty had cast upon her and brought her to. And what we mentioned of cursing, that it is supplication, is indicated."
"4067","3539 - What al-Husayn ibn Nasr al-Baghdadi and Sa'id ibn Marwan al-Azdi Abu Uthman narrated to us, they said: Mahdi ibn Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Abu Hazrah al-Madani Ya'qub ibn Mujahid, from Ubadah ibn al-Walid ibn Ubadah ibn al-Samit, he said: We came to Jabir ibn Abdullah, and he narrated to us, he said: We marched with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the raid of Buwat, while he was seeking al-Majdi ibn Amr al-Juhani; and the water-drawing camel was shared among us, five and six and seven taking turns upon it. Then the turn of a man from the Ansar came upon a water camel of his; so he mounted it, then drove it, and it became stubborn with him somewhat, so he said: Sha! May Allah curse you. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is this one cursing his camel? He said: I, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Get down from it; let no cursed one accompany us. Do not supplicate against yourselves, nor supplicate against your children, nor supplicate against your wealth, lest it coincide with a time of granting from Allah Almighty in which there is giving, and He answers you. Abu Ja'far said: So he returned what is in this hadith to supplication; so that indicated that the cursing which was from the woman against her she-camel in the hadith of Imran was a supplication from her against her, in which she coincided with a time in which Allah Almighty grants His giving to whoever asks Him therein, so He answered her in her supplication against her she-camel in what she supplicated against her. And in the hadith of Jabir is the like of that concerning the man who cursed his camel. And the she-camel in the hadith of Imran, and the water camel in the hadith of Jabir, remained in the state they were in before there was from their owners concerning them what was, since they had no sin in that; and the punishment for what was from their owners returned upon their owners."
"4068","3540 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id informed us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was among people of his Companions, a man among them cursed his camel, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is the one cursing his camel? A man said: I, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Then put him behind us, for it has become binding. So in this hadith was the informing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the curser of his camel mentioned therein that it had become binding; and that was in the meaning that there was from him the supplication which was answered therein, so the curse became binding, and it is expulsion, in the camel which he cursed. So the meaning of this hadith returned to the meaning of the hadith of Imran, and it added upon it the binding which the hadith of Jabir which we mentioned indicated. And from Allah we ask success."
"4069","565 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the protected area of the palm tree. 3541 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Mus'ab al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Yahya narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Two men disputed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning a small palm tree; so he cut a frond from it, then measured the palm tree with it, and it was five cubits, so he made it its protected area."
"4070","3542 - And Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abu Tuwalah Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Ma'mar, and from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini, from his father, from Abu Sa'id, he said: Two men disputed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the protected area of a palm tree; so he said in the hadith of Amr ibn Yahya: he found it five cubits, and Abu Tuwalah said: seven cubits, so he judged by that. Abd al-Aziz said: meaning the measure of a frond from its fronds."
"4071","3543 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Yahya informed me, from his father: that two men disputed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the fallen fruit of a palm tree; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a frond from its fronds and measured it, and it was five cubits, so he judged that its protected area is five cubits. And he did not mention Abu Sa'id in the chain of his hadith. Abu Ja'far said: So we considered this hadith; and the best that presented itself to us therein is that what is meant by it is the palm tree which its owner plants in the place which is of dead lands, so he owns it by what dead land is owned by, of the command of the Imam by that upon the school of whoever says: dead land is not owned except by the Imam granting it to whomever he grants it of the people, and they are Abu Hanifah; and of his reviving it and removing deadness from it, even if the Imam did not grant it to him, so he owns it by that, as Malik ibn Anas and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan and al-Shafi'i say, and most of the people of knowledge besides Abu Hanifah therein. So when he planted it as we mentioned, he deserved by that what stands only by it, and it is the protected area which was made for it in what we narrated in this chapter, as the wells taken in dead lands are of the protected area which stands only by them. Among them is the watering well; it has of protected area forty cubits from every side of its sides. And among them is the water-drawing well; it has of protected area sixty cubits from every side of its sides. And Muhammad ibn al-Hasan used to say concerning these two wells: the protected area of each one of them is the cubits which we mentioned that they are its protected area."
"4072","3544 - And it is what Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Salt ibn Mas'ud al-Jahdari narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Sulayman al-Numayri narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn al-Walid ibn Ubadah ibn al-Samit, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, that of the judgment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is that he judged concerning the sole palms among palms, and that is that there be a palm or two palms or three among palms and they differ concerning the rights of that, so he judged that for each of those palms is the reach of its frond as its domain, and they were called al-araya. Abu Ja'far said: So the aspect of what is in the hadith in our view, and Allah knows best, is concerning the palm or two palms or three which are among a man's palms, and he and the owner of the palms differ concerning the rights of what each one of them has of the palms; so what belongs to the owner of the palm or two palms or three is what that which is his thereof stands only by; this is the aspect of this hadith in our view, and Allah knows best."
"4073","566 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the door which he excepted from the doors that were toward his mosque, and he commanded closing them except that door. 3545 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: I heard Ya'la ibn Hakim narrating from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in his illness in which he died: Close from me every small door in the mosque except the small door of Abu Bakr."
"4074","3546 - And Abu Umayyah and Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud together narrated to us, they said: Mu'alla ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Close these doors except the door of Abu Bakr, for if I were taking a close friend, I would have taken Abu Bakr as a close friend, but the brotherhood of Islam is better."
"4075","3547 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Hasan al-Nasa'i narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in his illness: Close these doors opening out except the door of Abu Bakr, for there is none of my Companions greater with me in favor, nor better in service, than him."
"4076","3548 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ayyub ibn Bashir al-Ansari informed me, from one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Close these doors opening into the mosque except the door of Abu Bakr, for I know no man better with me in companionship in favor than Abu Bakr."
"4077","3549 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof."
"4078","3550 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Close these doors except the door of Abu Bakr, for I have seen upon every door of them darkness. Abu Ja'far said: So I mentioned this hadith to Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud, and I said to him: Fahd has been corroborated therein by Hasan ibn Sulayman; did you hear it yourself from Abdullah ibn Salih? He said: He narrated it on a day I was not present, then I attended him the next day and he mentioned it and retracted it. Abu Ja'far said: So in what we narrated of these hadiths, the excepted door was the door of Abu Bakr; and it has been narrated that the excepted door was the door of Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4079","3551 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Aslam narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Suhayl ibn Abi Salih narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, was given qualities, that one of them be in me is dearer to me than to be given red camels. They said: And what are they, O Commander of the Believers? He said: He married Fatimah the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and his dwelling in the mosque with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), lawful for him therein what is lawful for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and the banner on the Day of Khaybar. Abu Ja'far said: And Abdullah ibn Ja'far to whom this hadith returns, if he is al-Mukharrimi, then he is of those praised in his hadith; and if he is Ibn Najih Abu Ali ibn al-Madini, then his hadith is not like the hadith of Abdullah ibn Ja'far al-Mukharrimi, but he is not dropped; the people narrated from him, and of those who narrated from him is his son, and he is the imam of the people of hadith. Then we looked: did anyone besides him narrate this hadith from Suhayl?"
"4080","3552 - So we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri informed me, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, and he did not mention Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Ali ibn Abi Talib was given three things, that I be given them is dearer to me than to be given red camels: neighboring the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque; and the banner on the Day of Khaybar; and the third Suhayl forgot."
"4081","3553 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Jarrah al-Quhistani narrated to us, he said: Zafir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Abdullah ibn Sharik, from al-Harith ibn Tha'labah, he said: I said to Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him: Did you witness any of the virtues of Ali, peace be upon him? He said: I witnessed four virtues for him, and the fifth I witnessed; that the last of them be mine is dearer to me than the world and what is in it: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) closed the doors of the mosque and left the door of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him; so he was asked about that, and he said: It is not I who closed them, nor I who left it. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married him to Fatimah, peace be upon her, so she bore him children; and he gave him the banner on the Day of Khaybar."
"4082","3554 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Sufi informed us, he said: Ali, and he is Ibn Qadim, narrated to us, from Fitr, and he is Ibn Khalifah, from Abdullah ibn Sharik, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Ruqaym, from Sa'd, that al-Abbas came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: You closed our doors except the door of Ali. So he said: It is not I who opened them, nor I who closed them."
"4083","3555 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Wahb ibn Abi Karimah al-Harrani informed me, he said: Miskin ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Balj, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the doors of the mosque and they were closed, except the door of Ali, peace be upon him."
"4084","3556 - Ahmad informed us, he said: And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: al-Waddah, and he is Abu Awanah, narrated to us, he said: Yahya, and he is Ibn Abi Sulaym Abu Balj, narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abbas said: And he closed the doors of the mosque, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except the door of Ali, peace be upon him; so he used to enter the mosque while in major ritual impurity, and it was his path, he had no path besides it."
"4085","3557 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Balj, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Close the doors of the mosque except the door of Ali."
"4086","3558 - And Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Salih al-Nakhkhas narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr al-Raqqi narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Ayzar ibn Hurayth, he said: I was with Ibn Umar, and a man asked him about Ali and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them both; so he said to him: As for Ali, do not ask us about him, but look at his station with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): he closed our doors in the mosque except his door. And as for Uthman, he committed a great sin on the day the two hosts met, which Allah Almighty pardoned, and he committed a small sin and you killed him."
"4087","3559 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us. 3560 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, they said: Hisham ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Asid, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: We used to speak in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of people is Abu Bakr, then Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. And Ali, peace be upon him, was given three virtues, that one of them be mine is dearer to me than red camels: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married him to Fatimah and she bore him children; and he gave him the banner on the Day of Khaybar; and he closed all the doors of the mosque except the door of Ali."
"4088","3561 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from Maymun Abu Abdullah, from Zayd ibn Arqam, he said: Some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had doors opening into the mosque; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Close these doors except the door of Ali. People spoke about that, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: To proceed: I was commanded to close these doors except the door of Ali, and your speaker said concerning it; by Allah, I neither closed nor opened, but I was commanded with something and I followed it. Abu Ja'far said: So a speaker said: This is severe confusion and distant disagreement; how do you accept this and attribute it in its entirety to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah Almighty and His aid, was: that he did not clarify to us in that what he claimed of disagreement; and he only came to that from his little knowledge of the breadth of the language with which the Arabs addressed one another and understood one another's intent by what they address one another with of it. So it is possible that there was from him what is in each one of these two kinds of these hadiths in two different sayings: the first of them was a command to close those doors except the door which he excepted of them, either the door of Abu Bakr or the door of Ali; then he commanded after that to close the doors which he commanded to close by his first saying, and there was not among them the door which he excepted of them except the door which he excepted, either the door of Abu Bakr or the door of Ali."
"4089","3562 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Majid al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Talhah narrated to me, he said: I entered upon Mu'awiyah, and when I came out he called me and said: O son of my brother, shall I not place with you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I said: Yes. He said: I bear witness I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Talhah has fulfilled his vow. Abu Ja'far said: And this is of what we do not know was said unrestrictedly of anyone besides him. And the like of that is what was from him concerning al-Zubayr."
"4090","3563 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the people on the Day of the Trench, and al-Zubayr volunteered; then he called them, and al-Zubayr volunteered; then he called them, and al-Zubayr volunteered. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every Prophet has a disciple, and my disciple is al-Zubayr. Yunus said: Sufyan said: The disciple is the helper; and we do not know this was said unrestrictedly of anyone besides him. And the like of that is what was from him concerning Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4091","3564 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had say: I heard Ali, peace be upon him, say: I never heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combine both his parents for anyone except Sa'd ibn Malik; for on the Day of Uhud he kept saying: Shoot, may my father and mother be ransom for you. And the like of that is what was from him concerning Sa'id ibn Zayd, in entering him among the ten whom he testified are in Paradise. And the like of that is what was narrated concerning Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, from Uthman, of what we know with certainty he did not say except by divine instruction."
"4092","3565 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd, from his father, he said: al-Miswar said: While I was traveling in a company between Uthman and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, and Abd al-Rahman was ahead of me, upon him a black woolen cloak, Uthman said: Who is the owner of the cloak? They said: Abd al-Rahman. So he called me: O Miswar. I said: At your service, O Commander of the Believers. He said: Whoever claims he is better than your maternal uncle in the first emigration and in the latter emigration has lied."
"4093","3566 - And as Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Umm Bakr, meaning the daughter of al-Miswar, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf sold a land of his to Uthman ibn Affan for forty thousand dinars, and distributed it among the poor of Banu Zuhrah and among the Mothers of the Believers and among the needy of the people. Al-Miswar said: So I entered upon Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, with her share of that, and she said: Who sent this? I said: Abd al-Rahman. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None will be kind to you after me except the patient. May Allah Almighty give Ibn Awf to drink from Salsabil of Paradise. And this is what we have not known was said of anyone besides him. And the like of that is what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said concerning Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, of what we mentioned of what preceded from us in this book of ours: Every nation has a trustee, and the trustee of this nation is Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah. So these were distinctions from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for those whom he distinguished of his Companions, may Allah's pleasure be upon them. And above that is what the Book of Allah Almighty brought, of the saying of Allah Almighty: Not equal among you are those who spent before the conquest and fought; those are greater in degree than those who spent afterwards and fought. And all those we mentioned entered into this meaning, and their loftiness above people became clear, and the majesty of their station, and that no one of the people who was not as they were is like them. Then He Almighty said connected to that: And to each Allah promised the best. So the preferred entered by what we mentioned into the first meaning, and those besides them of those who accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered into the second meaning. So it is established by that that whoever accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has virtue over all people, and that those who accompanied him excel by what was from them of what Allah mentioned them by in the verse we recited. And from Allah Almighty we ask success."
"4094","567 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his forbidding his messengers to the disbelievers in fighting them from bringing down the people of a fortress of the fortresses they besiege upon the judgment of Allah Almighty. 3567 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me, from Shu'bah ibn al-Hajjaj, from Alqamah ibn Marthad al-Hadrami, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was of what he commanded a man when he appointed him over a detachment: If you besiege the people of a fortress, and they want you to bring them down upon the judgment of Allah Almighty, do not bring them down upon the judgment of Allah; for you do not know whether you will hit the judgment of Allah Almighty concerning them."
"4095","3568 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us. 3569 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, each one of them said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith is the forbidding of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to his messengers from bringing the disbelievers down upon the judgment of Allah, and his informing them of the reason for which he prevented them from that, and it is that they do not know whether they will hit the judgment of Allah concerning them or not. And we did not find in the hadith of Jarir, from Shu'bah, from Alqamah, in this meaning of this hadith, addition over what we mentioned from him therein; and we found in the hadiths of others from Shu'bah addition over that."
"4096","3570 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Hafs ibn Abdullah informed me, he said: my father narrated to me, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to me, from Shu'bah ibn al-Hajjaj. Then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof, and added: But bring them down upon your judgment."
"4097","3571 - And as Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Mahmud ibn Ghaylan informed us, he said: Abd al-Samad, meaning Ibn Abd al-Warith, narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof, and agreed with Ibrahim upon the addition which he added over Jarir in his hadith. Then we sought in this hadith from other than the hadith of Shu'bah this addition, and we found more than one narrated it from Alqamah with this addition. Among them is Abu Hanifah."
"4098","3572 - As Ja'far ibn Ahmad ibn al-Walid al-Aslami narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Walid informed us, he said: I heard Abu Yusuf, he said: Abu Hanifah informed us, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like thereof, and in it is the addition which was added over Jarir. And among them is Sufyan ibn Sa'id al-Thawri."
"4099","3573 - As Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Yahya al-Hamdani narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, he said: al-Ashja'i narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Alqamah ibn Marthad al-Hadrami, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah al-Aslami, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like thereof, and in it is mention of that addition."
"4100","3574 - And as Abu Bishr Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Alqamah, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like thereof, and in it is that addition. Alqamah said: So I narrated it to Muqatil ibn Hayyan; he said: Muslim ibn Haysam narrated to me, from al-Nu'man ibn Muqarrin, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like thereof. Abu Ja'far said: And we did not find this addition in the hadith of anyone of al-Thawri's companions besides al-Firyabi, and besides Ishaq ibn Yusuf al-Azraq."
"4101","3575 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Sallam informed us, he said: Ishaq al-Azraq narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like thereof, and in it: And if you besiege the people of a fortress, and they ask you to bring them down upon the judgment of Allah Almighty, do not bring them down upon the judgment of Allah, but bring them down upon your judgment; for you do not know whether you will hit the judgment of Allah concerning them or not. And in it: Alqamah said: So I narrated this hadith to Muqatil ibn Hayyan, and he said: Muslim ibn Haysam narrated to me, from al-Nu'man ibn Muqarrin, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like thereof. Abu Ja'far said: So this addition in this hadith which returns to al-Nu'man ibn Muqarrin came from al-Firyabi, and from Ishaq ibn Yusuf together, from al-Thawri. And among them is Idris al-Awdi."
"4102","3576 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Sulayman al-Rahawi informed us, he said: Ya'la ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Idris al-Awdi narrated to us, from Alqamah, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like thereof, and in it is mention of the addition which was added over Jarir, from Shu'bah; and in it there is no mention of Alqamah's mentioning it to Muqatil ibn Hayyan to the end of the hadith. Then we looked into this addition which al-Firyabi and Ishaq ibn Yusuf added, which returns to al-Nu'man ibn Muqarrin: do we find it in the hadith of other than al-Thawri from Alqamah or not?"
"4103","3577 - So we found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki al-Sa'igh narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Umar al-Allaf narrated to us, he said: Jarir, meaning Ibn Abd al-Hamid, narrated to us, from Hamzah al-Zayyat, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from Muslim ibn Haysam al-Abdi, from al-Nu'man ibn Muqarrin al-Muzani, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like thereof, and in it is mention of the addition which was added over Jarir, from Shu'bah. Except that Hamzah and al-Thawri differed concerning who narrates this hadith from Muslim ibn Haysam: Hamzah said in his hadith: from Alqamah, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from Muslim ibn Haysam; so the narrator thereof in this hadith from Muslim ibn Haysam is Sulayman ibn Buraydah. And al-Thawri said in his hadith: Alqamah said: So I narrated it to Muqatil ibn Hayyan, then he mentioned the hadith; so the hadith became from Alqamah, from Muqatil, from Muslim ibn Haysam, from al-Nu'man. And Allah knows best what is correct therein. Abu Ja'far said: So we considered these reports and stood upon the forbidding of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to his messengers from bringing anyone of the people of the fortresses down upon the judgment of Allah concerning them if they ask them that, and his informing them that his forbidding them from that is only because they do not know what the judgment of Allah Almighty concerning them is. And we found in most of them his permitting them to bring them down upon their judgment. So we understood by that that the judgments of Allah Almighty in things which we have not known as written down, revealed in His Book, or a transmitted sunnah conveyed upon the tongue of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or by consensus of the nation upon the judgment of Allah Almighty therein, when they do not agree except from where they may agree upon what they agree upon thereof, and since Allah does not gather them upon misguidance, when we lack them, since we have not been tasked with them nor worshipped by them, because Allah Almighty has not tasked us with what we cannot bear, nor worshipped us by what we are incapable of, that we return in new events to our ijtihad therein, and to seeking what our ijtihad leads us to therein after we are of the people of the instruments for whose people is ijtihad in seeking the like of this. So when that leads us to a meaning, while we are such, acting by it is wide for us, even if we do not know whether it is with Allah Almighty as our ijtihad led us to therein or not. And we understood by that that what is obligated upon us therein is the ijtihad by which correctness therein may be attained, and which may fall short of it, not the hitting of correctness therein itself. And the like of that is what was in the affair of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh when Qurayzah came down upon his judgment and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him judgment concerning them."
"4104","3578 - As al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr, meaning Ibn Alqamah ibn Waqqas al-Laythi, narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: Aishah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besieged Banu Qurayzah; and when the siege pressed them, they said: We will come down upon the judgment of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes. So he sent to Sa'd. Abu Sa'id al-Khudri said: When he appeared to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Stand to your chief, or to your best. He said: Judge concerning them. He said: I judge that their fighters be killed, and their offspring be taken captive, and their wealth be divided. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have judged among them by the judgment of Allah and by the judgment of His Messenger."
"4105","3579 - And as al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, al-Rabi' said: Shu'ayb narrated to us, and Muhammad said: my father and Shu'ayb narrated to us, then they both together said: from al-Layth, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that he said: On the Day of the Confederates Sa'd ibn Mu'adh was shot, and they severed his jugular vein; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cauterized him with fire, and his hand swelled, so he left him, and the blood bled; so he cauterized him again, and his hand swelled. When he saw that, he said: O Allah, do not take my soul until You cool my eye from Banu Qurayzah. So his vein held, and not a drop dripped until they came down upon the judgment of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh. So he sent to him, and he judged that their men be killed, and their women and offspring be spared that the Muslims may benefit by them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You hit the judgment of Allah concerning them, and they were four hundred. When killing them was finished, his vein burst and he died. Abu Ja'far said: Do you not see that Sa'd judged concerning Banu Qurayzah by what he judged therein before knowing what the judgment of Allah concerning them is, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praised that of him. So that indicated that likewise judgments in new events: whoever has judgment therein employs his opinion by his ijtihad therein seeking what is obligated upon him therein, and he is not upon him hitting their realities; upon him is only ijtihad in that, even if he may fall short of it. And when that is wide in blood and in private parts, it is wider in wealth. And from Allah we ask success."
"4106","568 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning judges, who of them is in the Fire and who of them is in Paradise. Abu Ja'far said: We mentioned in what preceded from us in this book of ours, in explanation of the difficulty of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying to Abu Dharr: Do not judge between two, the chains of these reports; so we were sufficed by that from repeating them in this chapter. As for their texts, they are that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Judges are three: two judges in the Fire, and a judge in Paradise. As for the one in Paradise, a man who knew the truth and judged by it, so he is in Paradise; and a man who knew the truth and did not judge by it, and was unjust in judgment, so he is in the Fire; and a man who did not know the truth and judged for people upon ignorance, so he is in the Fire. So we considered this hadith and found therein that the judge who is in Paradise is the judge by the truth. So a speaker said: The judge by the truth is he who has stood upon the judgment with Allah in what he judged; and in that is what negates employing ijtihad which may be with it hitting that, and may be with it falling short of it. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah Almighty and His aid, was: that the matter therein is contrary to what was mentioned; for Allah Almighty has not tasked us with what we cannot bear. And He informed us upon the tongue of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in the two hadiths of Amr ibn al-As and Abu Hurayrah which we mentioned in that chapter, what the judge has of reward when he hits the truth by his ijtihad, and what he has of reward when he misses it after his ijtihad. So in that is what indicated that he exercises ijtihad in what he does not find in the Book of Allah textually, nor in the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) transmitted, nor in the consensus of the nation upon it established. And since he may judge by his ijtihad in which may be with it hitting the truth with Allah Almighty, and may be with it falling short of that, and what he judges by, by the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him to judge by it, is truth, we understood by that that the truth by which the judge is in Paradise is that truth, until these reports are sound and do not contradict. And we found the like of that was from two prophets of Allah's prophets, and they are Dawud and Sulayman: they judged concerning the crops and differed; so Allah said concerning them: And We gave Sulayman understanding of it, and to each We gave judgment and knowledge. And in that is what indicated that they judged by the ijtihad of their opinions without Allah revealing to them what they judge by; so that indicated that likewise are judges besides them. And it was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that Sulayman asked his Lord to give him a judgment coinciding with His judgment, and He gave it to him. And we knew he was before his asking Him that, to him was judgment by right of prophethood; so that indicated that it was permissible he judge a judgment contradicting His judgment; and were it not so, his asking Allah that would have no meaning, since He had given it to him before that. 3580 - al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us of that, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, he said: Rabi'ah ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn al-Daylami, he said: I entered upon Abdullah ibn Amr at Ta'if and heard him say: We heard, meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), say: Sulayman, peace be upon him, asked his Lord to give him a judgment coinciding with His judgment, and He gave it to him."
"4107","3581 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir narrated to us, he said: I heard Sa'id ibn Abd al-Aziz narrating from Rabi'ah ibn Yazid, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Ibn al-Daylami, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like thereof, except that he said in place of so He gave him: so He granted him. And there was of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his praise of Mu'adh ibn Jabal when he asked him about what he judges by, when he sent him as judge to Yemen, upon this meaning."
"4108","3582 - As al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us. 3583 - And as Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn A'yan narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, from Abu Awn al-Thaqafi, from al-Harith ibn Amr, nephew of al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, from men of the people of Hims of the companions of Mu'adh, from Mu'adh, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he sent him to Yemen, said: How will you judge when a case presents itself to you? He said: I will judge by what is in the Book of Allah Almighty. He said: And if it is not in the Book of Allah Almighty? I said: By the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: And if it is not in the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah? He said: I will exercise my opinion and not fall short. He said: So he struck my chest with his hand and said: Praise be to Allah Who guided the messenger of the Messenger of Allah to what pleases the Messenger of Allah. He said: Then likewise were the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after him in this meaning."
"4109","As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from al-Shaybani Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote a judgment to an official of his, and the scribe wrote: This is what Allah showed Umar. So he said: Erase it, and write: This is what Umar saw; if it is correct, it is from Allah Almighty, and if it is error, it is from Umar. And the like of that is what was from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud when he was asked about the man who married a woman and did not consummate with her nor name her a dowry until he died: I say therein by my opinion; if it is error, it is from me, and if it is correct, it is from Allah Almighty. And we shall mention that with its chains in its place later in this book of ours, if Allah wills. And in that is what indicated that their school, may Allah's pleasure be upon them both, was in this meaning, as we established these reports upon in this chapter. And from Allah we ask success."
"4110","569 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the pilgrimage before his pilgrimage, of the appointment therein, and of the recitation of Bara'ah upon the people therein, and who was its commander therein, and who was the conveyer from him therein, of Abu Bakr and of Ali. 3584 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb informed us, he said: al-Abbas ibn Muhammad, meaning al-Duri, informed us, he said: Abu Nuh Qurad narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq, from Abu Ishaq, from Zayd ibn Yuthay', from Ali, peace be upon him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Bara'ah to Makkah with Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, then followed him with Ali, and said to him: Take the document and go to the people of Makkah. So I caught up with him and took the document from him, and Abu Bakr turned back grieved, and said: O Messenger of Allah, has anything come down concerning me? He said: No, except that I was commanded to convey it, I or a man of my household."
"4111","3585 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Abbad, meaning Ibn al-Awwam, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and commanded him to call with these words, then followed him with Ali. While Abu Bakr was on part of the road, he heard the bellowing of the she-camel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), al-Qaswa. So Abu Bakr came out, thinking it was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and behold Ali, peace be upon him. So he handed him the document of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so he appointed him over the season, and commanded Ali to call with these words. So they set out; Ali stood in the days of Tashriq and said: The covenant of Allah Almighty and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is free from every polytheist; so travel in the land four months; and no polytheist shall perform pilgrimage after this year; and no naked one shall circumambulate the House; and none shall enter Paradise except a believer. He said: So Ali used to call with them, and when his voice grew hoarse, Abu Hurayrah stood and called with them."
"4112","3586 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: al-Waddah, and he is Abu Awanah, narrated to us, he said: Abu Balj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: I was sitting with Ibn Abbas when nine men came to him, and he mentioned a story, and said therein: And he sent, meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, with Surah al-Tawbah, and sent Ali, peace be upon him, behind him, and he took it from him, and said: None shall go with it except a man who is of me and I am of him."
"4113","3587 - And Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Imran al-Akhnasi narrated to us; and Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, he said: Salim ibn Abi Hafsah narrated to us, from Jumay' ibn Umayr al-Taymi, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar said to me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, with Bara'ah, until when they were of the road of Makkah at such and such, there was a rider, and behold it was Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. So he said: O Abu Bakr, give the document which is with you. He said: What is with me, O Ali? He said: By Allah, I knew only good. So Abu Bakr returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, what is with me? He said: Good, but I was commanded that none convey from me except I or a man of my household; thus in the hadith of Muhammad ibn Ali; and in the hadith of Fahd: or a man of my household, Ali ibn Abi Talib."
"4114","3588 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he sent Bara'ah to the people of Makkah with Abu Bakr, then sent Ali, and said: None shall convey it except a man of my household."
"4115","3589 - And al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Hibri narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof."
"4116","3590 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us, he said: I read to Abu Qurrah Musa ibn Tariq, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to me, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he returned from the Umrah of al-Ji'ranah, sent Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, over the pilgrimage. Until when we were at al-Arj, he called the dawn prayer, then straightened to say the takbir, and heard the grunting behind his back, so he stopped from the takbir and said: This is the grunting of the she-camel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has changed his mind concerning the pilgrimage, and perhaps it is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) so we pray with him. And behold Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, upon her. So Abu Bakr said to him: A commander or a messenger? He said: No, but a messenger; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me with Bara'ah to recite it to the people in the stations of the pilgrimage. So we came to Makkah; and when it was a day before al-Tarwiyah, Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, stood and addressed the people, and told them of their rites, until when he finished, Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, stood and recited Bara'ah to the people until he completed it. Then we went out with him until when it was the Day of Arafah, Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, stood and addressed the people and told them of their rites, until when he finished, Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, stood and recited Bara'ah to the people until he completed it. Then it was the Day of Sacrifice and we poured forth; when Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, returned, he addressed the people and told them of their pouring forth and of their sacrifice and of their rites; when he finished, Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, stood and recited Bara'ah to the people until he completed it. When it was the first day of departure, Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, stood and addressed the people, and told them how they depart and how they stone, and taught them their rites; when he finished, Ali stood and recited Bara'ah to the people until he completed it. Abu Ja'far said: So a speaker said: There has been narrated from Abu Hurayrah what indicated that the call with these things which in what you narrated are attributed to Ali was by the command of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4117","3591 - So he mentioned what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sent me among those who call on the Day of Sacrifice at Mina: that no polytheist shall perform pilgrimage after this year, and no naked one shall circumambulate the House."
"4118","3592 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Muhammad ibn Shihab al-Zuhri, he said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Abu Bakr sent me in that pilgrimage among criers he sent on the Day of Sacrifice calling at Mina: No polytheist shall perform pilgrimage after this year, and no naked one shall circumambulate the House. This speaker said: So the hadith of Abu Hurayrah indicated that the conveying of these things was only from Abu Bakr, not from Ali; and this is severe confusion in these reports. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah Almighty and His aid, was: that there is no confusion in that, as he mentioned; for the command in that pilgrimage was only to Abu Bakr specially, with no partner for him therein, and obedience in the command and prohibition therein was to Abu Bakr, not to another. For that reason he sent Abu Hurayrah among the criers who were with him to carry out what Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, commands them in what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him for. And that is indicated."
"4119","3593 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from al-Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Muharrar ibn Abi Hurayrah, from his father, he said: I was with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him with Bara'ah to the people of Makkah; so I used to call until my voice grew hoarse. It was said: With what did you call? He said: He commanded us to call: that none shall enter Paradise except a believer; and whoever had between him and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned a word as if it were a covenant, his term is to four months; and when the months pass, Allah is free from the polytheists and His Messenger; and no naked one shall circumambulate the House; and no polytheist shall perform pilgrimage after this year. Abu Ja'far said: So that indicated that the call of Abu Hurayrah was only by what Ali cast to him, and that his resorting to Ali was by the command of Abu Bakr; for the command was to him, since he was the commander in that pilgrimage until he returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turning from it. And in what we clarified of that is loftiness of rank for Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, in his command over the conveyer from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what none but he is fit to be the conveyer thereof from him. And in it also is loftiness of rank of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, in the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singling him out with what he singled him out of conveying from him. And in that is what the people of knowledge must stand upon the station of each one of them until they give him what Allah made for him, and do not diminish him anything thereof. And from Allah we ask success."
"4120","570 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his sending Abu Bakr over the pilgrimage in that pilgrimage which we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter, of his hastening to Dhu al-Majaz. As was narrated from Ibn Abbas of what contradicts the hadith of Jabir which we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter. 3594 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Sulayman al-Numayri narrated to us, he said: Musa, meaning Ibn Uqbah, narrated to us, he said: Kurayb informed me, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Abu Bakr over the pilgrimage, and he did not approach the Ka'bah, but hastened to Dhu al-Majaz informing the people of their rites, and conveying to them from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he came to Arafah from the direction of Dhu al-Majaz; and that is because they had not combined the pilgrimage with the Umrah. So a speaker said: How do you accept this, and in it is abandonment of things of the causes of pilgrimage? And they are the circumambulation of arrival, and the sermon in Makkah on the day before the Day of Tarwiyah, and staying at Mina the time the pilgrim stays therein, then going from it to Arafah. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah Almighty and His aid, was: that what was from Abu Bakr of what is in this hadith was for a meaning which must be stood upon and known; for the market of Dhu al-Majaz was one of the markets in which the Arabs gathered for selling and trades; among them are those who perform pilgrimage, and among them are those who turn to their abode without pilgrimage. So Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, wanted them to gather in the season of pilgrimage to hear what is recited to them therein of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, for. And of what was narrated concerning the market of Dhu al-Majaz is that it was such."
"4121","3595 - What Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Ukaz and Dhu al-Majaz and Majannah were the markets in the pre-Islamic time; and when Islam came, it is as if they deemed it sinful to trade, so Allah revealed: There is no blame upon you to seek bounty from your Lord. In the seasons of pilgrimage."
"4122","And what Ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, he said: I heard Ibn al-Zubayr say: There is no blame upon you to seek bounty from your Lord. In the seasons of pilgrimage. Thus Ibn Abi Aqil narrated it from Ibn Uyaynah; and others narrated it from him contrary to that."
"4123","3596 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Amr, from Ibn Abbas, and from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, from Ibn al-Zubayr, he said: Ukaz and Majannah and Dhu al-Majaz were markets in the pre-Islamic time in which they traded; and when Islam came, it is as if they deemed them sinful, so they asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and there came down: There is no blame upon you to seek bounty from your Lord. In the seasons of pilgrimage. Abu Ja'far said: So what was from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, of his hastening to Dhu al-Majaz, was to command all the people to attend the season to hear what is recited there of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent whom he sent therein for; and perhaps the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had commanded him with that. Then he went to Arafah with the people and stood there, and it is the connection of the pilgrimage which is indispensable. Then he returned to Makkah after going to al-Muzdalifah, and after stoning and shaving, until he circumambulated the House the circumambulation of the Day of Sacrifice, and it is the circumambulation of visitation without which the pilgrimage is not complete. And there is no disagreement among the people of knowledge that whoever circumambulated and had not circumambulated at his arrival, that he trots in the first three circuits thereof if he did not trot them in the circumambulation in which trotting is done, and it is the circumambulation of arrival; and that he runs thereafter between al-Safa and al-Marwah, as he runs after the circumambulation of arrival; unlike what he does who circumambulated the House on the Day of Sacrifice and had circumambulated the circumambulation of arrival, of abandoning trotting therein and abandoning running between al-Safa and al-Marwah. And Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, did not neglect the matter of the sermon before the Day of Tarwiyah at Makkah; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had an official of his over Makkah then, and he is Attab ibn Asid al-Umawi; so he addressed the people at Makkah on that day, then joined Abu Bakr with the people at Arafah until he completed with them the rest of their pilgrimage. So what was from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, in his pilgrimage, of what he undertook for the people, since he was their commander in their pilgrimage, had no deficiency from what the commander of the pilgrims must do in his pilgrimage with the people. And it is a pilgrimage before which there was in Islam no pilgrimage except one pilgrimage which Attab ibn Asid performed with the people in the year eight; and it is said: it was in other than Dhu al-Hijjah; for time also turned to Dhu al-Hijjah in the pilgrimage which Abu Bakr performed with the people, and he established the pilgrimage therein; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed pilgrimage with the people in the year after it in Dhu al-Hijjah; and the matter continued upon that to the Day of Resurrection. And from Allah we ask success."
"4124","571 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the Mufassal of the Qur'an, of his prostrating therein and of his abandoning prostration therein. 3597 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Ubayd Abu Qudamah al-Iyadi narrated to us, from Matar al-Warraq, from a man, from Ibn Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not prostrate in anything of the Mufassal when he moved to Madinah. Abu Ja'far said: So in the chain of this hadith was a man whose name was left unmentioned; so we wanted to know who he is."
"4125","3598 - So we found Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Khalaf narrated to us, he said: Azhar ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Ubayd, from Matar al-Warraq, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated in al-Najm while in Makkah; and when he emigrated to Madinah, he abandoned it. Abu Ja'far said: So we stood by that upon that he is Ikrimah the freedman of Ibn Abbas; and acceptance of this hadith became straight for us thereby, and considering it and looking into the conditions of its narrators, and whether Ibn Abbas has an opponent of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what was mentioned from him therein or not. So we found the one upon whom al-Harith ibn Ubayd turned; and al-Bukhari mentioned that Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi was asked about him, and said: He is one of our shaykhs, and we saw only good. So this was from Abd al-Rahman informing of the majesty of his standing with him. And he strengthened what is from Ibn Abbas in this hadith."
"4126","What Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata that he asked Ibn Abbas about the prostration of the Qur'an, and he did not count for him anything in the Mufassal. Then we considered what is in the text of this hadith: has there been narrated what repels it or not?"
"4127","3599 - So we found al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Nu'aym al-Mujmir, that he said: I prayed with Abu Hurayrah above this mosque; so he recited: When the heaven splits asunder. And prostrated therein, and said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrate therein."
"4128","3600 - And we found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: al-Thawri and Ibn Jurayj and Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Ata ibn Mina, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: We prostrated with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in: When the heaven splits asunder."
"4129","3601 - And we found Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Ata ibn Mina, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: We prostrated with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in: When the heaven splits asunder, and: Read in the name of your Lord."
"4130","3602 - And we found Abd al-Ghani narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Abu Hurayrah, that he prostrated with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in one of these two."
"4131","3603 - And we found al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Abu Bakr Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We prostrated with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in: When the heaven splits asunder."
"4132","3604 - And we found Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Rawh narrated to us, and the wording is Abu Dawud's, they said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah: that he saw him prostrate in: When the heaven splits asunder, and said: Had I not seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrate therein, I would not have prostrated."
"4133","3605 - And we found Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof."
"4134","3606 - And we found Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us. 3607 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, they said: Malik narrated to us. 3608 - And we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Abu Salamah, that Abu Hurayrah recited to them: When the heaven splits asunder, and prostrated therein; and when he finished, he told them: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated therein."
"4135","3609 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, he said: I saw Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, prostrate in: When the heaven splits asunder. It was said to him, and he said: I saw Abu al-Qasim, or the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), prostrate therein."
"4136","3610 - And we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he saw Abu Hurayrah prostrating in: When the heaven splits asunder. Abu Salamah said: So I said to him when he finished: You prostrated in a surah in which I never saw people prostrate! He said: Had I not seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrate therein, I would not have prostrated."
"4137","3611 - And we found al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Had narrated to us, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, then he mentioned the like thereof exactly."
"4138","3612 - And we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Qurrah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab and Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'd, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I prostrated with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in: When the heaven splits asunder, and Read in the name of your Lord: two prostrations. Abu Ja'far said: So we stood by what we narrated from Abu Hurayrah upon his prostration with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he mentioned his prostration with him therein of the Mufassal; and his prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companionship of him was at Madinah, not at Makkah."
"4139","3613 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: We came to Abu Hurayrah and said: Narrate to us. So he said: I accompanied the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) three years."
"4140","3614 - And as al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard Irak ibn Malik say: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: I came to Madinah while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at Khaybar, and a man of Banu Ghifar was leading the people; so I heard him recite in the dawn prayer, in the first rak'ah: Surah Maryam, and in the second: Woe to the defrauders. There was a man among us who had two measures, taking with one and giving with the other; so I said: Woe to so-and-so. So what we narrated from Abu Hurayrah of this contradicts what we narrated from Ibn Abbas therein; for what we narrated from Ibn Abbas therein is his informing of the abandonment by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of prostration in the Mufassal after he came to Madinah, and in this is his prostration therein after he came to Madinah. And this was with us worthier; for affirmation of things is worthier than their negation. And it is possible that Ibn Abbas said of that what we narrated from him because he did not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do it after he came to Madinah, and whoever mentioned that he did it after he came to it is worthier. So a speaker said: What Ibn Abbas said in that has been strengthened by what was narrated from Zayd ibn Thabit therein."
"4141","3615 - So he mentioned what Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Abi Fudayk informed us; and what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us; Ibn Abd al-Hakam said: from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, and Bakkar said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn Qusayt, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Zayd ibn Thabit: that he recited to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) al-Najm and he did not prostrate therein."
"4142","3616 - And what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Kathir, meaning Ibn Ja'far, narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Qusayt, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Zayd ibn Thabit, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like thereof."
"4143","3617 - And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abu Sakhr narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Qusayt, from Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, from his father, he said: I presented al-Najm to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and none of us prostrated."
"4144","3618 - And what al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, he said: Haywah narrated to us, he said: Abu Sakhr narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah Almighty and His aid, was: that he has no proof in what he mentioned either upon negating prostration from the Mufassal, even if what was from Zayd ibn Thabit was also at Madinah; for it is possible that the abandonment by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of prostration therein then was for a meaning preventing him from that: either because it was in a time in which prostration is not proper, of the times in which he forbade prayer therein; or because he was without purification from a ritual impurity that was from him; or because the reciter of a prostration verse had prostration therein and abandoning it, as was upon that more than one of his Companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Among them: Salman al-Farisi."
"4145","3619 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, he said: Salman passed by people who had recited the prostration verse, and it was said: Will you not prostrate? So he said: We did not intend it."
"4146","And among them: Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr. Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Abi Saghirrah narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, he said: Ibn al-Zubayr recited the prostration verse while I was witness, and did not prostrate; so al-Harith ibn Abdullah stood and prostrated, then said: O Commander of the Believers, what prevented you from prostrating when you recited the prostration verse? So he said: When I am in prayer, I prostrate; and when I am not in prayer, I do not prostrate. And when it is possible that the abandonment by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of prostration therein was for a meaning of these meanings we mentioned, there is in this hadith of Zayd ibn Thabit no proof for whoever abandons prostration therein, nor repelling that there is a prostration therein; and what we narrated from Abu Hurayrah, established thereby the prostration of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he mentioned his prostration therein at Madinah, is worthier than it and than the hadith of Ibn Abbas which we mentioned from him before it. And from Allah we ask success."
"4147","572 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in his saying to Ubayy ibn Ka'b: I was commanded to recite the Qur'an to you, or: I was commanded to make you recite the Qur'an. 3620 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: al-Ajlah informed me, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was commanded to recite the Qur'an to you. He said: I said: Did your Lord Almighty name me to you? He said: Yes. So he recited to me: Say: In the bounty of Allah and in His mercy, in that let them rejoice; it is better than what they gather. With ta in both. He said: So in this hadith was the informing of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Ubayy, may Allah be pleased with him, that he was commanded to recite the Qur'an to him; and it has been narrated that what he said to him was contrary to what is in this hadith."
"4148","3621 - al-Hasan ibn Abdullah ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof."
"4149","3622 - Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Aslam al-Minqari, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A surah was revealed to me, and I was commanded to make you recite it. He said: I said to him: So did you rejoice? He said: And what prevents me, while He says: In the bounty of Allah and in His mercy, in that let them rejoice. He said: So in this hadith was that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to make him recite a surah of the Qur'an revealed to him. And the chain of this hadith was better in chain than the hadith before it, due to the majesty of Aslam al-Minqari and his high standing in narration over the standing of al-Ajlah therein, and due to the loftiness of Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman therein over Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman, and his fame, and the multitude of his narrations."
"4150","3623 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ubayy and said: Allah commanded me to recite the Qur'an to you. He said: Did Allah Almighty name me to you? So he said: Allah Almighty named you to me. So he began weeping. Qatadah said: And I was informed that he recited to him: Those who disbelieved were not. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith was of the recitation of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon Ubayy; so it agreed with the first hadith. And in it was that what he recited to him was a surah of the Qur'an, and it is: Those who disbelieved were not. So he was thereby a reciter of the Qur'an to him."
"4151","3624 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us also, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ammar ibn Abi Ammar, he said: I heard Abu Habbah al-Badri say: When: Those who disbelieved were not, to its end, came down, Jibril (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Messenger of Allah, your Lord Almighty commands you to make Ubayy recite it. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ubayy: Jibril commanded me to make you recite this surah. Ubayy said: And was I mentioned there, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes. So Ubayy wept. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith was that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to make Ubayy recite of the Qur'an is only a surah thereof of the Qur'an. And this is permissible in the language that the name Qur'an be applied to it, existing in the Book of Allah: among it His Almighty saying: And when you recite the Qur'an, We place between you and those who do not believe in the Hereafter a concealed veil. And His saying: So when you recite the Qur'an, seek refuge in Allah from the accursed Satan. And His saying: And when We turned to you a company of the jinn listening to the Qur'an. And that was only upon what they heard of it, not upon all of it. And we narrated from Ibn Abbas in what preceded from us in this book of ours that what they heard of it is what he used to recite in the dawn prayer; and if we did not mention it, we shall mention it later in this book of ours, if Allah wills. And we carried it upon mentioning what we brought in this chapter only because Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam mentioned to us from al-Shafi'i that he said to him: The most indicative thing that there is no prostration in the Mufassal of the Qur'an is the hadith of Ubayy in his answer to Ata ibn Yasar when he asked him about prostration in the Mufassal, and he informed him that there is no prostration therein."
"4152","And what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays al-Farra narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, that he asked Ubayy ibn Ka'b: Is there a prostration in anything of the Mufassal? He said: No. He said: So Ubayy: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited the Qur'an to him, and passed the places of prostration, and stood upon what he prostrated therein of it and upon what he did not prostrate therein of it; so his negating that there be prostration in the Mufassal indicated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not prostrated therein in his recitation of it to him. So we transmitted that to Ibn Abi Imran, and he said: This is corrupt speech; for were what he related from Ubayy to negate that there be prostration in the Mufassal, what was narrated from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud of prostration in the Mufassal would be more indicative that therein is prostration than that; for Ubayy, even if the Qur'an was recited to him or he was made to recite the Qur'an upon what was said, in what he recited to him or in what he made him recite thereof of what necessitates that it is part of the Qur'an, not all of it, since Ibn Mas'ud attended the presentation by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the Qur'an to Jibril (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it is the last presentation he presented to him."
"4153","3625 - And he mentioned what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abdullah al-Nakha'i and Abu Mu'awiyah and Waki' narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Zibyan, he said: Abdullah ibn Abbas said to me: Which of the two readings do you read? I said: The first reading, the reading of Ibn Umm Abd. So he said to me: Rather it is the latter: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to present the Qur'an to Jibril (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) every year once; and when it was the year in which he was taken, he presented it to him twice; and Abdullah ibn Mas'ud attended that, and knew what was abrogated and what was altered. So what we had in Ibn Mas'ud, in his attending the recitation by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the whole Qur'an to Jibril (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and what Abu Abdullah, meaning Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam, had, in what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited to Ubayy or in what he made him recite thereof of the Qur'an, is what was narrated that it is part of it, not all of it. And it is possible, had he recited the Qur'an to him and not prostrated, that he did not prostrate while he may prostrate; so how, when he only mentioned that he recited to him thereof what has no prostration therein. And we found from Ibn Mas'ud prostration in the Mufassal, or in what was narrated from him of prostration therein."
"4154","So he mentioned what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab and Abdullah ibn Mas'ud prostrate in: When the heaven splits asunder. So in this was the prostration of Abdullah in the Mufassal; and it is not permissible to prostrate in other than its place of prostration, while it is permissible to abandon prostration in the place of prostration. So if in the hadith of Ubayy, in negating prostration in the Mufassal, Abu Abdullah has indication that there is no prostration therein, then what we have from Ibn Mas'ud, of what is therein affirming prostration therein, is more indicative that it is the place of prostration, for what we mentioned. Abu Ja'far said: And we did not find from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning prostration of recitation, of what was established with us from him, except in what is in the Mufassal thereof, not in what is besides it of the Qur'an. And we were sufficed from bringing what is from Ibn Mas'ud and Ibn Umar of the prostration of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Makkah; for Ibn Abbas said therein what we mentioned from him in this chapter. And from Allah Almighty we ask success."
"4155","573 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: It is not proper, or it is not lawful, for a man believing in Allah and the Last Day to spend two nights except his will is written with him. 3626 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Ubaydullah al-Umari, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not the right of a man who spends the night while he has wealth except his will is written with him."
"4156","3627 - And Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a Muslim man who has wealth concerning which he bequeaths to spend two nights except his will is written."
"4157","3628 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not the right of a man who has wealth concerning which he wants to bequeath except his will is written with him."
"4158","3629 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rashid narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa narrated to us, that Nafi' narrated to him from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not proper for anyone who has wealth concerning which he bequeaths that two nights pass him except his will is with him."
"4159","3630 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik and Yunus informed me, that Nafi' narrated to them from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not the right of a Muslim man who has something concerning which he bequeaths, spending two nights, except his will is written with him."
"4160","3631 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Shabbabah narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn al-Ghaz narrated to us, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not proper for anyone who has wealth concerning which he bequeaths that two nights pass him except his will is with him."
"4161","3632 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to me, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abdullah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like thereof. Abdullah said: No night passed me since I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say that except my will is with me. Abu Ja'far said: So in these reports is what was mentioned therein of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded of the will, and urged upon it. And the people spoke concerning what is meant by that. Al-Shafi'i, in what al-Muzani related to us from him, used to say: Its meaning is: It is not prudence for a man to spend two nights except his will is written with him. He said: And it bears: It is not the known in morals except this, not from the aspect of obligation. Abu Ja'far said: And it is possible that it be upon a meaning worthier for its interpretation than these two meanings, and it is that Allah Almighty had His judgment upon His servants what He revealed to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of His saying: Prescribed for you when death presents itself to one of you, if he leaves good, is the bequest to parents and near relatives. So that was from Him Almighty before inheritances were obligated in estates. Then He obligated them therein after that, and abrogated the bequest to the heir upon the tongue of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by his saying: Allah Almighty has given every possessor of right his right, so no bequest to an heir, even if that was not narrated except from one direction."
"4162","3633 - And it is what al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Shurahbil ibn Muslim, from Abu Umamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that. Except that the people of knowledge accepted that and argued by it, so that sufficed from seeking chains therein. And since a man's father and his relatives deserve of his wealth after his death only what he bequeaths to them thereof, while they are worthier of it after his death than others of strangers, what was obligatory upon him was the bequest to him and to them until they deserve that to the exclusion of others; until Allah Almighty abrogated that in whoever inherits him, and there remained whoever besides him of his near ones for whom what is in the verse of the command of bequest to him was not abrogated. So we found no meaning for the interpretation of this hadith worthier of it than this meaning. And from Allah we ask success."
"4163","574 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he used to love agreeing with the People of the Book in what he was not commanded anything therein. Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Khalifah al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: 3634 - Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to let his hair hang down, while the polytheists used to part their heads; and the People of the Book used to let their heads hang down. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to love agreeing with the People of the Book in what he was not commanded anything therein; then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) parted his head."
"4164","3635 - And Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah informed me, from Ibn Abbas, then he mentioned the like thereof. So a speaker said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and describe him with loving agreement with the People of the Book, despite their altering their book and distorting it from its places, and purchasing with it a small price, despite your narrating from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4165","3636 - So he mentioned what Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Ibn Akhi Ibn Shihab narrated to us, from his uncle, he said: Ibn Abi Namlah al-Ansari informed me, that Abu Namlah al-Ansari informed him that: While he was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man of the Jews came to him and said: O Muhammad, does this bier speak? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah knows best. The Jew said: It speaks. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever the People of the Book tell you, neither believe them nor deny them; and say: We believe in Allah Almighty, and His messengers, and His books; for if it is truth, you did not deny them, and if it is falsehood, you did not believe them."
"4166","3637 - And Muhammad ibn Uzayz narrated to us, he said: Salamah narrated to us, from Uqayl, he said: Ibn Shihab said: And Ibn Abi Namlah narrated to me, that Abu Namlah al-Ansari informed him, then he mentioned the like thereof exactly. He said: And since the People of the Book, their reports are not accepted for what may be therein of lying upon Allah Almighty and upon His messengers, their deeds were likewise. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah Almighty and His aid, was: that what is in the hadith of Ibn Abbas, of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) agreed with the People of the Book upon of what they were upon thereof, indicated to us the things which he used to love agreeing with the People of the Book upon in what he was not commanded anything therein, and it is their letting their hair hang down: it was only in what was wide for him to shave his head, and was wide for him what he did of letting his hair hang down; since each one of them had no command from Allah Almighty therein. So it was wide for him to do what he wished thereof to do. And the People of the Book, in what they did therein, was possibly that that was from them for something they were commanded in their book. And those besides them of the Arabs were only people of idols and worship of statues. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) loved, in what he did of what was mentioned in this hadith, what the People of the Book did therein; since it is possible that it was from them for what we mentioned. And as for the hadith of Abu Namlah, it is not of this meaning in anything; for what is therein is informing of a thing itself, either it be truth or it be falsehood. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught his nation to say at that, and at its likes of what the People of the Book inform them of what he taught them to say in the hadith of Abu Namlah, so they neither believe it if it is falsehood, nor deny it if it is truth. So it became clear, by the praise of Allah and His favor, that there is no contradiction in anything of these two meanings mentioned in these two hadiths. And from Allah Almighty we ask success."
"4167","575 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his permitting al-Furay'ah to move in her waiting period from her husband's death, from the house in which his death news came to her suddenly, and of his commanding her after that to remain therein until the decree reaches its term. 3638 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad informed me, he said: Sa'd ibn Ishaq ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah al-Ansari informed me, from Zaynab the daughter of Ka'b, she said: al-Furay'ah the daughter of Malik ibn Sinan, and she is the sister of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, informed me that the death news of her husband came to her; he had gone out seeking runaway slaves of his, and overtook them at the edge of al-Qadum, and they killed him. So I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, the death news of my husband came to me, while I am in a house of the houses of the Ansar far from the houses of my family; and I dislike staying therein; and he left us neither in a dwelling, nor wealth he owned, nor maintenance to be spent upon me. So if you see that I join my brother, and our affair be together, for it is more uniting in my affair and dearer to me. He said: If you wish, join your family. So I went out rejoicing with that, until when I was in the chamber or in the mosque, he called me, or I was called to him. So he said: How did you claim? So I repeated the account to him from its beginning. So he said: Remain in the house in which the death news of your husband came to you until the decree reaches its term. So she observed waiting therein four months and ten days. Then Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, sent to her and asked her, and she informed him, and he judged by it. Abu Ja'far said: And this is a hadith of momentous weight turning upon Sa'd ibn Ishaq, from whom Anas narrated it; and it was narrated by more than one of the eminent people of knowledge older than him from him; of those who narrated it from him, of those who are such, is Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri."
"4168","3639 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from one who informed him, from Zaynab the daughter of Ka'b ibn Ujrah, and she was under Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from Furay'ah the daughter of Malik, sister of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, then he mentioned this hadith with all its meanings. Except that al-Zuhri did not mention in this hadith of his to Yunus ibn Yazid who narrated it to him from Zaynab the daughter of Ka'b. So we sought that to know: is he Sa'd ibn Ishaq or not?"
"4169","3640 - So we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Nasr al-Marwazi informed us, he said: Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr, meaning Ibn Abi Uways, narrated to us, from Sulayman, meaning Ibn Bilal, from Ibn Abi Atiq and Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah, that his paternal aunt informed him, from Furay'ah the daughter of Malik, she informed her that she was with a man of Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj, then he mentioned it with all its meanings except what was from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, therein, for he did not mention it. Abu Ja'far said: So we stood by that upon that the man by whom Ibn Shihab narrated to Yunus ibn Yazid this hadith without naming him to him is this Sa'd ibn Ishaq. And among them: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari."
"4170","3641 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof, and mentioned therein what Anas mentioned in his hadith of what was from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, therein."
"4171","3642 - And as Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof. And among them: Yazid ibn Muhammad al-Qurashi."
"4172","3643 - As al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Yazid ibn Muhammad, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, then he mentioned with his chain and with the story of Uthman therein the like thereof. And among them: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughirah ibn Abi Dhi'b."
"4173","3644 - As Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, he said: Sa'd ibn Ishaq ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof except the story of Uthman which he did not mention. And among them: Malik ibn Anas."
"4174","3645 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof. And among them: Shu'bah and Rawh ibn al-Qasim."
"4175","3646 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah and Rawh ibn al-Qasim together narrated to me, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof. And among them: Sufyan al-Thawri."
"4176","3647 - As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof, except that he did not mention the story of Uthman therein. And among them: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah."
"4177","3648 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, or Ishaq ibn Sa'd, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof; and I do not know whether he mentioned the story of Uthman therein or did not mention it. And among them: Muhammad ibn Ishaq."
"4178","3649 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, and he mentioned with his chain the like thereof, and mentioned therein the story of Uthman, except that he said in place of al-Furay'ah: al-Fara'ah. And among them: Ibn Jurayj."
"4179","3650 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Ala, meaning Abu Kurayb, informed us, he said: Ibn Idris narrated to us, from Shu'bah and Ibn Jurayj and Yahya ibn Sa'id and Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, that he mentioned with his chain the like thereof, except that he did not mention the story of Uthman therein, and said in place of al-Furay'ah: al-Fari'ah the daughter of Malik. And among them: Hammad ibn Zayd."
"4180","3651 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id informed us, he said: Hammad, and he is Ibn Zayd, narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof, and said therein from Furay'ah, and did not mention therein the story of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. And among them: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Salim al-Umari."
"4181","3652 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Salim informed me, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof. And among them: Wuhayb ibn Khalid."
"4182","3653 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn al-Khalil narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Ishaq, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof. And among them: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah al-Fazari."
"4183","3654 - As Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Mani' narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof, and did not mention therein the story of Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith was the permitting by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to al-Furay'ah to join her brother and move to him from the house in which the death news of her husband came to her. So it is possible that that was from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) due to her mentioning to him that he left her nothing to dwell in nor anything to spend upon her thereof; so he permitted her the move and joining her brother for that. And it is possible that he permitted her that because she has no dwelling in an abode her husband left, nor maintenance for her from wealth had he left it, since his wealth or his dwelling had gone out of his ownership by his death to whom they went. And Allah knows best what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended thereby. Then we considered his commanding her after that to remain in the house in which the death news of her husband came to her until the decree reaches its term, after her affair was contrary to that: what is it? So it is possible that that was from him because Jibril (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was present at that of his answer, and informed him of what he commanded for whose sake to al-Furay'ah of what he commanded her thereof; since she had informed him that she is in a house from which her husband's family did not dislodge her, though they may dislodge her thereof, since it was theirs to the exclusion of her husband, for he did not own it; but it was of their right to keep her chaste where they wished to keep her chaste, as precaution for her husband against a child being attached to him from her. And more than one of the people of knowledge said with this; among them al-Shafi'i, along with their schools that the widow has no maintenance nor dwelling in her waiting period. So they said: her husband's guardians may keep her chaste in her waiting period, guarding for her husband, whose guardians they are, against a child she brings not from him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her, since they had not expelled her from that abode and approved it for her, to return to it and be therein until the decree reaches its term, as Jibril (peace and blessings of Allah be upon them both) informed him that it is of their rights which they may seek. And what was from Jibril (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein was like what was from him in the hadith of Abu Qatadah to the one who asked him and said: If I am killed in the path of Allah, patient, seeking reward, advancing not retreating, will Allah expiate my sins from me? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes. When the man turned, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him, or commanded with him and he was called. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How did you say? So he repeated to him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, except debt; thus Jibril (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me."
"4184","3655 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed me. 3656 - And al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, then they both together said: from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah al-Ansari, from his father, then he mentioned this hadith as we mentioned."
"4185","3657 - And al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Muhammad ibn Qays, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, then he mentioned the like thereof. So the like of this is possible to be in the hadith of al-Furay'ah. And the meaning we mentioned of the rights of the dead man's guardians in his wife widowed from him, Abu Ja'far said: al-Muzani related it to us from al-Shafi'i, and it is a good saying. And from Allah Almighty we ask success."
"4186","576 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning al-ghayl: of dislike of it, and of intending to forbid it, and of forbidding it, and of what besides that was from him therein. 3658 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Amr ibn Muhajir, from his father, from Asma the daughter of Yazid ibn al-Sakan al-Ansariyyah, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Do not kill your children secretly; for al-ghayl overtakes the horseman upon the back of his horse."
"4187","3659 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Ghaniyyah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Humayd, from Muhammad ibn Muhajir al-Ansari, from his father, from Asma the daughter of Yazid al-Ansariyyah, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Do not kill your children secretly; for killing by al-ghayl overtakes the horseman and throws him off the back of his horse. Abu Ja'far said: So we considered these two hadiths and found therein of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to his nation: Do not kill your children secretly. Then he mentioned the meaning he mentioned therein; so that was upon warning them thereof, and informing them that there may be from it throwing the horseman off his horse. And that was from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Allah knows best, upon what the Arabs used to say therein. So he warned of that, even though there had not come down to him therein from Allah Almighty confirmation of them nor denial of them in what they said thereof, upon compassion for their children, not upon other than that of forbidding to them what is a cause of that al-ghayl feared for their children."
"4188","3660 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us; and Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us; and as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, they said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed us, he said: al-Rukayn ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Hassan, from his uncle Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to dislike ten: yellowness, and altering grey hair, and wearing a gold ring, and trailing the waist-wrapper, and displaying adornment to other than its place, and striking with dice, and isolating water from its place, and spoiling the child without forbidding it, and tying amulets, and incantations except with the two refuge surahs."
"4189","3661 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-Rukayn ibn al-Rabi' ibn Umaylah al-Fazari, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof."
"4190","3662 - And as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-A'la informed us, he said: al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Rukayn narrating, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof. And in this hadith is the dislike by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of spoiling the child, and it is by the al-ghayl we mentioned, without forbidding it. So that indicated that his dislike (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he disliked thereof was a dislike with no prohibition with it. So if a speaker says: There was narrated from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his forbidding it."
"4191","3663 - So he mentioned what Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade al-ightiyal. Then he said: Had it harmed anyone, it would have harmed Persia and Rome. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah Almighty, was: that forbidding may be for dislike without prohibition with it, as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade drinking standing, not because he forbade that, but because he feared its harm upon whoever does it. And we mentioned what was narrated in that in what preceded from us of this book of ours. And the proof that his forbidding (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of al-ghayl was not a forbidding of prohibition."
"4192","3664 - Is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal, from Urwah, from Aishah, from Judhamah the daughter of Wahb: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I intended to forbid al-ghilah until I remembered that Persia and Rome do that and it does not harm their children."
"4193","3665 - And what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof, except that he did not mention Judhamah in his hadith, and stopped it upon Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4194","3666 - And what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us; and what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir narrated to us, they said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Urwah, from Aishah, from Judhamah the daughter of Wahb, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like thereof."
"4195","3667 - And what Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ishaq al-Saylahini narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal, from Urwah, from Aishah, from Judhamah the daughter of Wahb al-Asadiyyah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like thereof."
"4196","3668 - And what Ahmad ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: Abu al-Aswad Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, he said: Urwah narrated to us, from Aishah, from Judhamah, then he mentioned with his chain the like thereof."
"4197","3669 - And what al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, he said: Haywah informed us, from Abu al-Aswad, that he heard Urwah narrating from Aishah, from Judhamah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like thereof."
"4198","3670 - And what Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari and Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus al-Basri narrated to us, they said: al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: Judhamah narrated to me, then they mentioned the like thereof. So in this hadith was what indicated his permitting (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to his nation what he had warned them of, when he stood upon that it does not harm Persia and Rome in their children. And a remnant thereof had remained in the breasts of the Arabs, until it was narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, therein."
"4199","What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Atiyyah ibn Jubayr, from his father, he said: A relative of mine died and left a son of his; so my wife suckled him. So I swore not to approach her until she weans the boy. When four months passed, it was said to me: Your wife has separated from you. So I asked Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: If you swore with insight, your wife has separated from you; otherwise she is your wife. And Malik ibn Anas went to this meaning: he was asked about a man who left his wife while she suckles until she weans her child; so she refused that to him and sought from him his intercourse with her. So he said: I see no proof for her in that, nor is he compelled to that, whether there was an oath therein or not. And I like the saying of Ali therein. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I intended to forbid al-ghilah. So Malik said: And it is that a man have intercourse with his wife while she suckles. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended that until he mentioned that Persia and Rome do it, so he refrained from it. So this is not of what is judged for her thereby, nor is he compelled to it; it is only what was upon the aspect of harm, and this is not harmful; he only wants the welfare of his child. So I see no saying for her in that, nor is he compelled in that to intercourse with her. Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim mentioned that from him in his hearing from him. And others opposed that; among them: Abu Hanifah and his companions: they made him therein one vowing abstention from her, if he swore not to approach her until she weans her child, when between him and the completion of the two years is four months or more. Ibn Abi Imran mentioned to us from Ibn Sama'ah, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, with no disagreement he mentioned therein between him and his companions. And this saying with us is worthier of the two sayings; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not forbid suckling in intercourse, but only disliked it out of compassion, then permitted it. So the one refraining thereof from his wife is like the one refraining from the like thereof in other than the state of suckling."
"4200","And a claimant claimed, and he is al-Layth ibn Sa'd, that people say: al-ghayl is intercourse with the pregnant, not intercourse with the suckling woman. Zayd ibn Bishr mentioned that, from Ibn Wahb, from him. As for Malik, his school therein was: that it is intercourse with the suckling woman. As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, from Malik."
